WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:11.434
The one thing I would have
to have an immediate bias

00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:13.434
was towards the Middle East.

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:17.167
I identify as transgender,
non-conforming.

00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:21.501
It's not often assumed
that I am part black.

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:24.300
Do you think that being gay
is a choice?

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.601
I can't be sure.

00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:28.434
Who should police the police?

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:29.467
The citizens.

00:00:29.567 --> 00:00:30.901
That actually scares me.

00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:32.968
If I had to have a conversation

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.501
with my kids about this,

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:36.400
I wouldn't know pretty much
where to start.

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:39.300
How I treat
and talk to my children

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:40.434
is very much reflective

00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:42.767
of the environment that
they walk into every day.

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:45.100
I'm a lawyer by trade.

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:46.767
- I better watch what I say.
- (chuckles)

00:00:46.868 --> 00:00:48.167
I've been a cop for 20 years.

00:00:48.234 --> 00:00:51.634
We must understand that
how we've been weaponized,

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:53.567
you know, against
the black community.

00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:06.267
Hello, everybody,
my name is Jesse Williams.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.033
I want to welcome everyone
joining us today

00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:13.000
as we discuss bias,
conscious and unconscious bias

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:16.701
and its impact on us
personally and the collective.

00:01:16.734 --> 00:01:19.667
I am here now
because I feel like

00:01:19.701 --> 00:01:22.100
we're experiencing
a real opportunity.

00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:24.834
The real opportunity is
in the public consciousness

00:01:24.868 --> 00:01:27.000
with all that's happening in
the world and in our nation.

00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:30.767
We are primed, I think,
to accept the possibility

00:01:30.801 --> 00:01:32.901
that maybe we have some
room for improvement.

00:01:32.934 --> 00:01:34.300
Maybe there are some things

00:01:34.334 --> 00:01:37.300
that we can learn about
ourselves and each other

00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:40.400
that will allow us
to grow forward.

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:42.767
So part of that is
acknowledging conscious

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:44.033
and unconscious bias,

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:45.968
the roles that we might
be playing

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.634
in this whole equation.

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:49.434
I think it's incredibly
important for us

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:52.868
to face it, talk about it,
be open to that possibly.

00:01:52.901 --> 00:01:55.234
Everyone has bias,
myself included.

00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:58.133
It is a real issue and it's
something that we can address

00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:00.734
and just learn more about.

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:02.734
It doesn't make anybody
inherently wrong.

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:04.667
It's something that's
worthy of exploring.

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:06.968
So over the next few hours
we're going to do just that.

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:09.033
We're going to watch
a series of conversations

00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:12.400
between complete strangers
meeting for the first time,

00:02:12.434 --> 00:02:15.033
and they're going to discuss
not only themselves,

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:17.000
but issues that bring up

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:19.400
the concept of bias
in their lives.

00:02:19.434 --> 00:02:21.467
This might be
uncomfortable at times.

00:02:21.501 --> 00:02:23.667
It certainly has moments
of discomfort for them.

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:24.934
It might for you as well.

00:02:24.968 --> 00:02:26.567
Feelings could be hurt.

00:02:26.601 --> 00:02:28.234
But that's part
of the process.

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:29.834
That's how growth happens.

00:02:29.868 --> 00:02:31.968
So amidst that discomfort,

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:34.934
these are all opportunities
for learning for us.

00:02:34.968 --> 00:02:37.000
So before we dive deeper,

00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:39.968
I do want to acknowledge
how we've come here today,

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:43.200
that can't do that without
thanking Procter &amp; Gamble

00:02:43.234 --> 00:02:47.667
for spreading the word
and spearheading this effort.

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:49.267
That also includes BuzzFeed,

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:50.901
who's allowing us to use
their platform,

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:51.801
so thank you so much.

00:02:51.901 --> 00:02:54.133
For years P&amp;G has been
using their voice

00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:55.901
and acting as a leading advertiser

00:02:55.934 --> 00:02:57.834
to shine a light on inequality,

00:02:57.868 --> 00:02:59.701
highlighting bias in particular

00:02:59.734 --> 00:03:01.734
with their recent work
in the last couple of years

00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:04.234
and sparking a dialogue
to promote understanding,

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:05.701
to promote action.

00:03:05.734 --> 00:03:07.634
So I'm not going to do this alone.

00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:11.701
We are going to dial up the
incredible Freddie Ransome,

00:03:11.734 --> 00:03:13.467
who is a content creator,

00:03:13.501 --> 00:03:15.467
has a lot of insight
around bias,

00:03:15.501 --> 00:03:16.767
and she's going to help us

00:03:16.801 --> 00:03:18.534
walk through this
whole experience

00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:20.067
and be a great asset
to us today.

00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:21.701
Hey, Jesse, how are you?

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.167
I'm great, how are you?

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.234
I'm good.

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:26.634
I'm excited for what
today's going to hold.

00:03:26.701 --> 00:03:29.634
Just to give everyone
a little bit more detail

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:32.167
about the ins and outs
of this experiment,

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.367
we've paired 14 strangers
together to talk about bias.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.934
And these are real people,

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:39.567
they are here because
they want to have dialog

00:03:39.601 --> 00:03:42.100
and they want to experience
some personal growth.

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:44.467
So please be respectful
to them in the comments,

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:46.467
even if you don't agree
on what they're saying

00:03:46.501 --> 00:03:47.734
because we're all
friends here,

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:51.133
we're all just here to learn
and take in new information

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:55.667
or just sort of spread light
and share light to each other

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:59.801
in regards to experiences
and personal biases.

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:02.667
It's interesting when talking
to new people or strangers,

00:04:02.701 --> 00:04:04.300
I think folks can relate

00:04:04.334 --> 00:04:06.501
to being at a party
or a new gathering,

00:04:06.534 --> 00:04:08.467
you just find yourself kind
of pouring your heart out

00:04:08.534 --> 00:04:09.567
to somebody you just met.

00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:12.734
I think the idea that
won't definitely follow you

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:16.033
in your social circle
or your family events,

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:17.400
you've got a little bit
of freedom

00:04:17.434 --> 00:04:19.133
to just kind of
unload something,

00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:21.234
to explore something,
experiment,

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:22.434
play around with ideas

00:04:22.467 --> 00:04:25.067
without it being
kind of attached to you

00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:26.634
like this static cling

00:04:26.667 --> 00:04:28.400
and you're going to have to be
accountable for it later.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:32.767
We all carry with us learned
assumptions, presumptions,

00:04:32.801 --> 00:04:36.067
we all are a byproduct of media

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.701
and marketing and information

00:04:37.734 --> 00:04:39.567
and cartoons and commercials.

00:04:39.634 --> 00:04:42.467
Also, now is a good time
just to let folks know

00:04:42.501 --> 00:04:45.601
it's not only Freddie and
myself participating today.

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:46.667
We are really lucky

00:04:46.701 --> 00:04:49.067
to have an absolutely
brilliant professor,

00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:50.567
Dr. Charisse L'Pree,

00:04:50.601 --> 00:04:52.934
sharing her incredible
talents and wherewithal.

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:55.868
Professional L'Pree is at
Syracuse University

00:04:55.901 --> 00:04:58.033
where she currently focuses
on how the media affects

00:04:58.067 --> 00:04:59.801
the way we think about ourselves,

00:04:59.834 --> 00:05:01.300
exactly what
we're talking about,

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:03.133
our perceptions
of ourselves and others.

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:04.267
We've been working with her

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.567
to bring an added layer of context

00:05:06.601 --> 00:05:08.767
and facts into this discussion,

00:05:08.801 --> 00:05:10.801
those pesky things
that are necessary.

00:05:10.834 --> 00:05:12.767
Beyond just watching,
we're going to be posting

00:05:12.801 --> 00:05:14.534
discussion prompts
in the comments,

00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:16.868
so pay attention to those
if you could.

00:05:16.901 --> 00:05:19.901
This allows us all to be able
to join the conversation

00:05:19.934 --> 00:05:21.901
from an informed perspective.

00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:23.234
So be sure to
add your thoughts

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:24.467
and questions
there as well.

00:05:24.501 --> 00:05:27.267
This is a safe space, so please

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.834
don't succumb to any habits.

00:05:29.868 --> 00:05:31.634
We've all been in
comment sections

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:34.868
and we can see how it can get
childish and ugly quickly.

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:37.200
Let's elevate today,
let's make that a safe space

00:05:37.234 --> 00:05:39.100
so we can constructive
conversations.

00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:41.601
And now, while we have
paired strangers together,

00:05:41.634 --> 00:05:43.100
they're not entirely random.

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:46.467
We intentionally went about
this in a curated fashion

00:05:46.501 --> 00:05:49.701
to encourage discourse beyond
their echo chamber.

00:05:49.734 --> 00:05:50.834
These have been
really expansive,

00:05:50.868 --> 00:05:52.300
long conversations

00:05:52.334 --> 00:05:54.033
when we've paired
these strangers together,

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.467
so we've naturally had
to edit them down for time.

00:05:56.501 --> 00:05:59.000
We did that in
the most responsible way.

00:05:59.033 --> 00:06:01.634
So in order to catalyze
these conversations,

00:06:01.667 --> 00:06:05.801
we have passed along P&amp;G's
award-winning short film

00:06:05.834 --> 00:06:08.033
called &lt;i&gt;The Look&lt;/i&gt;
to all of these pairings

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:09.634
so that they can
watch it together

00:06:09.667 --> 00:06:12.234
and it can be a great way to
spark conversation about bias.

00:06:18.334 --> 00:06:20.200
Let's start with
our first pairing.

00:06:20.234 --> 00:06:23.267
We have Chozy joining us
from Atlanta, Georgia,

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.601
and we have Lynn
in North Carolina.

00:06:25.634 --> 00:06:27.701
So we have them dialed up
and meeting each other

00:06:27.734 --> 00:06:30.801
for the very first time for
an interesting conversation.

00:06:30.834 --> 00:06:32.667
All right, let's do it.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.200
Hello.

00:06:43.234 --> 00:06:44.567
Hi.

00:06:44.601 --> 00:06:46.634
How 'ya doing?

00:06:46.667 --> 00:06:49.367
Well, I'm well
today and yourself?

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.601
I'm going great, just excited
about this experience.

00:06:52.634 --> 00:06:54.067
Isn't this something?

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.167
My name's Lynn.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:57.467
Lynn?

00:06:57.501 --> 00:06:58.534
Lynn.

00:06:58.567 --> 00:07:00.634
Hi, my name is Chozy.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.734
Oh come on, no.

00:07:17.334 --> 00:07:18.934
[sighs]

00:07:22.033 --> 00:07:27.701
Um, I can definitely
relate to the beginning,

00:07:27.734 --> 00:07:32.834
getting the weird looks
for looking different.

00:07:32.868 --> 00:07:34.234
I can't believe this stuff

00:07:34.267 --> 00:07:35.968
still happens
and I know it does

00:07:36.033 --> 00:07:39.300
and I cannot understand why.

00:07:39.334 --> 00:07:40.334
[sighs]

00:07:41.434 --> 00:07:42.534
Yeah.

00:07:42.567 --> 00:07:45.734
We still judge each other on
the way we immediately appear.

00:07:45.767 --> 00:07:47.868
Where do you think
that comes from?

00:07:47.901 --> 00:07:50.734
The reason I think a lot of
people think that way,

00:07:50.767 --> 00:07:54.200
which is not only in the U.S.,
it's a global thing,

00:07:54.234 --> 00:07:56.968
is just lack of interaction

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.234
with people that are
different than you.

00:07:59.267 --> 00:08:02.234
I am originally from
East Jerusalem, Palestine,

00:08:02.267 --> 00:08:06.868
and I came here in 2001, three
weeks before September 11.

00:08:06.901 --> 00:08:10.601
So, when I came here I was
saying, "I'm Middle Eastern,

00:08:10.634 --> 00:08:16.100
I'm Palestinian, I'm Muslim,"
and it is just who I am.

00:08:16.133 --> 00:08:18.200
I didn't think anything of it.

00:08:18.234 --> 00:08:21.133
So September 11 happens

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:24.868
and all of a sudden
there is this hate

00:08:24.901 --> 00:08:27.133
for anybody
that looks Middle Eastern.

00:08:27.167 --> 00:08:30.300
So if you're brown,
you're going to be a target.

00:08:30.334 --> 00:08:35.334
It doesn't matter if you're
Arab, Indian, Hispanic,

00:08:35.367 --> 00:08:39.334
mixed black and white,
just dark featured,

00:08:39.367 --> 00:08:41.067
and a lot of people got

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.434
harassed and
attacked on campus

00:08:43.467 --> 00:08:45.367
at Georgia Southern.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:49.701
And I was like I'm not going
to go out and say

00:08:49.734 --> 00:08:51.834
that I'm Middle Eastern.

00:08:51.868 --> 00:08:57.667
I'm going to have to dodge it,
lie it, try to be a chameleon.

00:08:57.701 --> 00:08:59.167
But it's hard to
be a chameleon

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.167
when the majority
is black and white

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:02.667
and nobody looks like me.

00:09:02.701 --> 00:09:05.000
What I'm hearing you say is

00:09:05.033 --> 00:09:07.634
that it comes
from non-exposure

00:09:07.667 --> 00:09:09.868
to different types of people

00:09:09.901 --> 00:09:12.234
and they're visually different

00:09:12.267 --> 00:09:14.567
and we have to
judge immediately.

00:09:15.934 --> 00:09:19.100
The one kind of group that
you can see my ignorance,

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:23.334
I think when I thought about
biases towards people,

00:09:23.367 --> 00:09:25.801
the one thing I would have
an immediate bias

00:09:25.834 --> 00:09:28.667
was towards whatever
I would call;

00:09:28.701 --> 00:09:31.267
and you're right, it's ignorance,
the Middle East.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:36.567
Because it's different and so,
as we've had more immigrants

00:09:36.601 --> 00:09:38.934
and as I've been able
to meet more people,

00:09:38.968 --> 00:09:41.467
you see, my views change.

00:09:41.501 --> 00:09:44.267
And this is the only way

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:49.434
that we can break down
these conclusions.

00:09:49.467 --> 00:09:51.267
It's kind of embarrassing here

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:55.300
to admit that
I've had these biases.

00:09:55.334 --> 00:09:56.434
It's embarrassing.

00:09:56.467 --> 00:09:58.601
But I have not
really been able

00:09:58.634 --> 00:10:01.934
to interact very much with
people like yourself, Chozy,

00:10:01.968 --> 00:10:03.934
so I don't know
how the universe knew

00:10:03.968 --> 00:10:06.267
that you're the person
I probably needed

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:07.634
to talk most to.

00:10:07.667 --> 00:10:08.734
Yeah.

00:10:08.767 --> 00:10:10.801
I've been in churches here
when I came to the U.S.

00:10:10.834 --> 00:10:12.400
and I've been to
Baptist churches.

00:10:12.434 --> 00:10:14.434
And I'm looking
around and I see

00:10:14.467 --> 00:10:16.267
this poster of a white person

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:18.968
that looks like Kurt Cobain
from Nirvana

00:10:19.033 --> 00:10:23.267
with blonde hair, blue eyes,
on a cross.

00:10:23.367 --> 00:10:26.934
And I'm like, "Who is that?"

00:10:26.968 --> 00:10:30.901
And he's like, "This is Jesus."

00:10:30.934 --> 00:10:33.467
And I'm like, "What?!"

00:10:33.501 --> 00:10:35.634
Jesus is from Bethlehem.

00:10:35.667 --> 00:10:41.000
Jesus was a Jewish person from
Bethlehem in Palestine.

00:10:41.033 --> 00:10:44.100
If you go to Palestine right now,

00:10:44.133 --> 00:10:46.868
nobody's blonde hair, blue eyes.

00:10:46.901 --> 00:10:48.167
I'm considered light-skinned.

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:50.033
People are a little bit
darker than me.

00:10:50.067 --> 00:10:51.100
Yeah.

00:10:51.133 --> 00:10:53.801
I don't care what the Census
says that we are.

00:10:53.834 --> 00:10:55.167
I'm not treated like I'm a white

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:56.501
person when I come to America.

00:10:56.534 --> 00:10:58.901
I'm accepted by blacks
more than whites.

00:10:58.934 --> 00:11:01.534
Where I grew up in
Northern California,

00:11:01.567 --> 00:11:04.000
there was a lot of dissension
with black people.

00:11:04.033 --> 00:11:08.033
We didn't even have anybody
from Palestine or Jerusalem.

00:11:08.067 --> 00:11:09.934
We didn't have Jewish people.

00:11:09.968 --> 00:11:14.300
And I drove through the
part of town that was black

00:11:14.334 --> 00:11:18.167
and because I was white,
they threw a rock at me

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:19.868
because they were angry.

00:11:21.567 --> 00:11:24.934
And that was when integration
was starting

00:11:24.968 --> 00:11:29.701
and they would bring black
children and white people

00:11:29.734 --> 00:11:31.334
and white children together.

00:11:33.434 --> 00:11:35.501
We're people and we're
doing the best we can,

00:11:35.534 --> 00:11:36.968
and it's not real good.

00:11:37.033 --> 00:11:41.100
But if we keep talking about
it and meeting each other,

00:11:41.133 --> 00:11:43.801
and if I can see your pain,

00:11:43.834 --> 00:11:46.267
and maybe you can see
my embarrassment,

00:11:47.667 --> 00:11:49.601
because that's just who I am

00:11:49.634 --> 00:11:51.734
and I'm still trying to change.

00:11:51.767 --> 00:11:54.534
Let me let you picture this.

00:11:54.567 --> 00:11:57.601
Four hundred years ago you've
been brought to this country

00:11:57.634 --> 00:11:59.901
in shackles, enslaved.

00:11:59.934 --> 00:12:03.234
Let's say they traded
your people for money.

00:12:03.267 --> 00:12:05.000
They gave you freedom,

00:12:05.033 --> 00:12:08.534
but everything you ever
built they will burn

00:12:08.567 --> 00:12:10.234
and they always
will make you

00:12:10.267 --> 00:12:12.133
feel like you're
the bad person.

00:12:12.167 --> 00:12:15.167
So no matter what you do
and how high you get in life,

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.934
you will still be that slave.

00:12:18.968 --> 00:12:20.167
How would you feel?

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:21.667
Would you throw
a brick at somebody?

00:12:23.968 --> 00:12:25.534
[sighs]

00:12:25.567 --> 00:12:28.267
I would probably try
to burn everything down

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.267
because it's so unfair.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:33.968
It's so unfair.

00:12:34.033 --> 00:12:37.534
I really honestly feel if
I had been at the scene

00:12:37.567 --> 00:12:40.701
where George Floyd
was being killed,

00:12:40.734 --> 00:12:42.133
because I'm a little
white woman,

00:12:42.167 --> 00:12:44.534
I probably could have
jumped in there

00:12:44.567 --> 00:12:47.300
and they would have been busy,
because I'm not a threat.

00:12:47.334 --> 00:12:49.000
It's called, "white privilege".

00:12:49.033 --> 00:12:50.934
The police
don't see me as a threat.

00:12:50.968 --> 00:12:53.834
I've been pulled over and let
go and they've said to me,

00:12:53.868 --> 00:12:56.234
"If you were black I'd take
you in, but you're not."

00:12:56.267 --> 00:12:59.634
How do we change this, Chozy,
how do we do this?

00:12:59.667 --> 00:13:02.334
People need to be aware of
their white privilege.

00:13:02.367 --> 00:13:06.434
During 9/11 I was walking
back to my dorm.

00:13:06.467 --> 00:13:08.267
I don't know what's going on.

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:10.534
I've been in America
for three weeks.

00:13:10.567 --> 00:13:12.467
I walk in.

00:13:12.501 --> 00:13:14.868
I'm looking at the big TV
in the lobby.

00:13:14.901 --> 00:13:18.968
I see a building on fire,
people jumping and chaos.

00:13:19.033 --> 00:13:20.300
And I'm like, "Oh, this
looks like back home."

00:13:20.334 --> 00:13:21.334
Why?

00:13:21.367 --> 00:13:23.767
Because we see this every day,
this is nothing new.

00:13:23.801 --> 00:13:27.834
So from that day on,
Middle Easterners and Muslims

00:13:27.868 --> 00:13:33.300
have suffered ten times
more till this day.

00:13:33.334 --> 00:13:38.367
And they burned mosques,
they killed Muslims,

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:41.467
they banned Muslims from
coming to this country.

00:13:41.501 --> 00:13:44.267
Well, I thought this country
is based on freedom

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:48.067
to worship any, you know,
to practice any religion.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:53.534
I'm very moved
by your description

00:13:53.567 --> 00:13:56.467
of exactly how
you were treated, Chozy,

00:13:56.501 --> 00:13:59.734
and the ignorance
that makes me cry.

00:13:59.767 --> 00:14:02.467
It makes me
really embarrassed.

00:14:02.501 --> 00:14:05.834
But things are changing,
people are moving all over

00:14:05.868 --> 00:14:11.267
and we need to help people
like you change it.

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:14.601
Actually change begins
with people like you

00:14:14.634 --> 00:14:17.834
that are thinking about
these issues,

00:14:17.868 --> 00:14:19.968
but still not taking action.

00:14:20.033 --> 00:14:23.634
The smallest
action you can take

00:14:23.667 --> 00:14:26.234
is, number one,
surround yourself

00:14:26.267 --> 00:14:28.334
with people that are
different than you.

00:14:28.367 --> 00:14:31.501
Number two, talk to people,
your family,

00:14:31.534 --> 00:14:33.667
know how to talk to them

00:14:33.701 --> 00:14:36.968
with having a constructive
conversation

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:40.334
that can have criticism and
some defense and offence.

00:14:40.367 --> 00:14:43.534
But we have to
unite the people.

00:14:43.567 --> 00:14:46.300
We have to put our
differences on the side.

00:14:46.334 --> 00:14:49.467
We can't just keep bringing
biased and prejudiced

00:14:49.501 --> 00:14:53.300
and racist things that keep
dividing us.

00:14:53.334 --> 00:14:56.067
I'm so moved by what
you said, Chozy,

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:01.734
and I understand that
the actions that I take

00:15:01.767 --> 00:15:03.834
are to talk, to be aware,

00:15:03.868 --> 00:15:06.934
but talk to people especially
as I meet them.

00:15:06.968 --> 00:15:09.968
Because I'm probably
not at all what I appear

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:11.634
and I didn't tell the producers

00:15:11.667 --> 00:15:12.901
that, you know, I'm gay.

00:15:12.934 --> 00:15:16.367
And certainly I've had
beer bottles thrown at me

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:19.501
and I have not wanted
to bother anybody,

00:15:19.534 --> 00:15:21.767
I just wanted to be left alone
and be myself,

00:15:21.801 --> 00:15:23.267
whatever that is.

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:24.968
And I've pretended a lot

00:15:25.033 --> 00:15:27.667
because I can get away
with pretending.

00:15:27.701 --> 00:15:29.601
So you're with a woman?

00:15:29.634 --> 00:15:30.801
Yes, sir.

00:15:30.834 --> 00:15:33.834
Okay, so you
didn't mention that

00:15:33.868 --> 00:15:36.334
so I'm glad you mentioned
that because—

00:15:36.367 --> 00:15:37.067
Well.

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:38.767
—then you would have
kind of experienced

00:15:38.801 --> 00:15:42.033
a lot more of what
we experience.

00:15:42.067 --> 00:15:44.067
I never really said too
much about it

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.133
because I just felt like
it's nobody's business

00:15:46.167 --> 00:15:48.300
and, honestly, I lied about it
because I could.

00:15:48.334 --> 00:15:52.000
I mean you can't see that I'm gay.

00:15:52.033 --> 00:15:53.934
As I've grown and seen

00:15:53.968 --> 00:15:56.367
the things that
people have gone through

00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:01.567
and the way that gay men
were treated in the '80s

00:16:01.601 --> 00:16:04.334
with the AIDS epidemic

00:16:04.367 --> 00:16:08.367
and losing so many people
that were my friends

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:11.267
and no one cared,
it does make sense

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:14.434
that if I had really stood up

00:16:14.467 --> 00:16:17.367
and copped to the whole
thing and made a stand

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:21.334
and said, "No, you don't treat
people this way

00:16:21.367 --> 00:16:24.300
because these people are me

00:16:24.334 --> 00:16:27.100
and if you like me, then
you like gay people, right?"

00:16:27.133 --> 00:16:29.968
But here in Hendersonville,
North Carolina,

00:16:30.033 --> 00:16:32.767
I've been actually
legally married

00:16:32.801 --> 00:16:37.567
and that is
a form of acceptance.

00:16:37.601 --> 00:16:41.868
And I have nephews and nieces
who have grown up

00:16:41.901 --> 00:16:44.434
and they don't think
a thing about this

00:16:44.467 --> 00:16:48.701
because of their exposure
to people that were gay

00:16:48.734 --> 00:16:52.234
and they go, "Well, so,
what's the big deal?"

00:16:52.267 --> 00:16:55.400
I thank you for sharing
that with me

00:16:55.434 --> 00:16:57.801
because that's part
of who you are.

00:16:57.834 --> 00:17:00.567
There's no way to see
that or tell that,

00:17:00.601 --> 00:17:06.000
so that was
a pretty interesting twist

00:17:06.033 --> 00:17:10.100
and it kind of changed
the whole aspect of this call

00:17:10.133 --> 00:17:12.634
because I knew then

00:17:12.667 --> 00:17:16.434
I can definitely relate and
make you relate mostly

00:17:16.467 --> 00:17:20.701
to what I've been feeling
and have empathy as well

00:17:20.734 --> 00:17:23.667
for what you've
been going through.

00:17:23.701 --> 00:17:25.868
And I know this struggle

00:17:25.901 --> 00:17:28.367
because I'm from
the Middle East;

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.634
and we're not open for that.

00:17:30.667 --> 00:17:35.067
And my cousin is gay.

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:39.133
He thinks he tries to make
a noise about it

00:17:39.167 --> 00:17:42.868
so he can get the equality
and the freedom,

00:17:42.901 --> 00:17:45.133
and I want you to do the same.

00:17:45.167 --> 00:17:48.167
So I had this epiphany
while you were talking

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:50.400
that this is how bias changes

00:17:50.434 --> 00:17:53.467
is that you've had this
conversation with me,

00:17:53.501 --> 00:17:55.534
and maybe you'll be
with somebody

00:17:55.567 --> 00:17:57.934
or your family in one way,

00:17:57.968 --> 00:17:59.934
and they'll talk
about something.

00:18:00.067 --> 00:18:02.367
But you've had this
experience; you'll go,

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:04.033
"Well, you know
there was this time

00:18:04.067 --> 00:18:05.601
I talked to this woman,"

00:18:05.634 --> 00:18:09.100
and believe me, if I see
anybody from Palestine

00:18:09.133 --> 00:18:10.534
it's going to be,

00:18:10.567 --> 00:18:12.100
"Well, you know
I talked to this

00:18:12.133 --> 00:18:13.567
man and he was
from Palestine

00:18:13.601 --> 00:18:16.501
and he's in this country
and this is how he feels."

00:18:16.534 --> 00:18:20.667
I want to thank you,
Lynn, for doing this

00:18:20.701 --> 00:18:25.167
and I want to also thank you
for sharing with me

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:30.133
and being able
to listen and respond

00:18:30.167 --> 00:18:32.834
and have a very healthy
conversation.

00:18:32.868 --> 00:18:33.901
Thank you.

00:18:33.934 --> 00:18:38.467
Well, Chozy, I think the best
part about talking to you,

00:18:38.501 --> 00:18:40.234
while I'm sitting here realizing

00:18:40.267 --> 00:18:42.934
that, oh, I just came out
to the entire world

00:18:42.968 --> 00:18:44.734
and I did because of you

00:18:44.767 --> 00:18:47.968
and because of what you
and the people

00:18:48.033 --> 00:18:51.334
that produced you
have been through

00:18:51.367 --> 00:18:55.400
and you made me understand
[gasps] the pain.

00:18:55.434 --> 00:18:58.000
And you've helped me
really immeasurably.

00:18:58.033 --> 00:18:59.734
Look at all the things I said.

00:18:59.767 --> 00:19:02.334
I owe that to you, sir.

00:19:02.367 --> 00:19:03.801
I do.

00:19:03.834 --> 00:19:05.701
How about that?

00:19:05.734 --> 00:19:06.934
Go figure.

00:19:06.968 --> 00:19:08.934
Thank you, thank you.

00:19:08.968 --> 00:19:09.767
Thank you.

00:19:20.601 --> 00:19:23.534
Okay, so that was
Chozy and Lynn;

00:19:23.567 --> 00:19:26.133
really a little plot-twist
towards the end.

00:19:26.167 --> 00:19:29.267
I know for me personally,
when I was watching the video

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:31.667
I was not expecting her

00:19:31.701 --> 00:19:33.968
to say that she was a part
of the queer community

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:35.934
and that she's married

00:19:35.968 --> 00:19:39.167
and has this whole other part
to her story that we were—

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:41.133
at least I was unaware of
at the beginning.

00:19:41.167 --> 00:19:42.934
So that was kind of interesting.

00:19:42.968 --> 00:19:46.067
And I think also,
kind of in real-time

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:47.567
this bias is sort
of playing out.

00:19:47.601 --> 00:19:50.667
It's like I'm thinking like,
oh yeah, she's cis,

00:19:50.701 --> 00:19:52.133
heterosexual, white woman

00:19:52.167 --> 00:19:53.934
who, like you know,

00:19:53.968 --> 00:19:57.868
probably hasn't really
experienced a lot of turmoil

00:19:57.901 --> 00:19:59.467
in terms of identity.

00:19:59.501 --> 00:20:01.667
And that's just me making
that assumption

00:20:01.701 --> 00:20:03.467
and then to find out

00:20:03.501 --> 00:20:06.901
that, oh, actually
she has experienced this.

00:20:06.934 --> 00:20:10.634
Okay, now I'm getting a better
understanding of who she is.

00:20:10.667 --> 00:20:12.200
Yeah, what was interesting to me

00:20:12.234 --> 00:20:15.267
was how it impacted
Chozy immediately.

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:18.634
It always makes folks
feel more comfortable

00:20:18.667 --> 00:20:21.968
having conversations
with people who can

00:20:22.033 --> 00:20:25.033
or are willing
to understand them,

00:20:25.067 --> 00:20:26.567
whatever that means,

00:20:26.601 --> 00:20:29.300
and a big part of that is
a shared actual experience.

00:20:29.334 --> 00:20:32.934
Parents talking about how
hard it is to have children,

00:20:32.968 --> 00:20:34.968
they can relate to actual
parents much better

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:37.167
than some single person
with no kids, right?

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:38.167
Exactly.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:40.801
It's really that basic.

00:20:40.834 --> 00:20:44.334
So when Chozy thinks
he's talking to somebody

00:20:44.367 --> 00:20:46.567
who kind of fits
all the dominant boxes,

00:20:46.601 --> 00:20:49.133
most of them outside
of gender,

00:20:49.167 --> 00:20:51.601
to hear, well, you actually
have to deal with identity,

00:20:51.634 --> 00:20:53.934
with making yourself small,
with making yourself quiet

00:20:53.968 --> 00:20:55.701
with not always
identifying yourself

00:20:55.734 --> 00:20:58.300
in a way that would
make you a target,

00:20:58.334 --> 00:21:02.100
okay, we're actually on closer
footing than I thought,

00:21:02.133 --> 00:21:04.834
that makes me see you
slightly different

00:21:04.868 --> 00:21:06.834
or maybe feel more comfortable

00:21:06.868 --> 00:21:08.400
expressing my experience

00:21:08.434 --> 00:21:11.267
in a way that has a better
shot of being received.

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:15.200
I think you watched
Lynn certainly,

00:21:15.234 --> 00:21:18.067
for lack of a better term,
kind of mature and grow

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:20.467
and settle
into her comfort zone.

00:21:20.501 --> 00:21:21.901
Yeah, you love to see it.

00:21:21.934 --> 00:21:23.167
And I can say, too,

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:26.501
I was really happy that Lynn
was self-aware

00:21:26.534 --> 00:21:29.667
in the fact and confident
in admitting

00:21:29.701 --> 00:21:31.901
that she lied about
her sexuality for years

00:21:31.934 --> 00:21:33.167
because she knew
that she could.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:35.501
She could sort of slide
under the radar

00:21:35.534 --> 00:21:38.434
as outwardly I guess presenting

00:21:38.467 --> 00:21:40.901
as a straight, white woman

00:21:40.934 --> 00:21:42.601
walking through the world.

00:21:42.634 --> 00:21:45.434
And that was interesting
because I think that

00:21:45.467 --> 00:21:48.200
that sort of transcends
in so many different scenarios

00:21:48.234 --> 00:21:50.300
whether you can pass race-wise

00:21:50.334 --> 00:21:53.534
or ethnically or in terms of
religion, sexuality.

00:21:53.567 --> 00:21:56.701
I think that we all could
come to the table

00:21:56.734 --> 00:21:59.000
with certain areas
where we can pass

00:21:59.033 --> 00:22:01.300
and we sort of use that
for survival.

00:22:02.667 --> 00:22:04.901
I don't necessarily think
that it's that she's ashamed,

00:22:04.934 --> 00:22:06.834
but a lot of times
it's about safety.

00:22:06.868 --> 00:22:09.434
So that's something
that stood out to me.

00:22:09.467 --> 00:22:12.868
A couple things that I clocked,

00:22:12.901 --> 00:22:14.834
not because they were new
but actually

00:22:14.868 --> 00:22:18.334
because they're fairly common
in my lived experience

00:22:18.367 --> 00:22:23.868
is what bias can do is it can
minimize or isolate

00:22:23.901 --> 00:22:25.501
the experiences of others.

00:22:25.534 --> 00:22:28.234
While Chozy explains
some of his experience

00:22:28.267 --> 00:22:32.067
in the discrimination
that he goes through,

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:34.133
I mean can you imagine
being a Palestinian

00:22:34.167 --> 00:22:36.634
landing here three weeks
before 9/11

00:22:36.667 --> 00:22:38.334
in Atlanta, Georgia

00:22:38.367 --> 00:22:39.367
[gasps]

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:40.734
—the fear that he has?

00:22:40.767 --> 00:22:42.133
How do you go out
and get coffee?

00:22:42.167 --> 00:22:44.200
Every day you're going out
and you could possibly be—

00:22:44.234 --> 00:22:46.167
we saw hate crimes
obviously skyrocket.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:48.133
And as he described
some of this,

00:22:48.167 --> 00:22:50.234
you see what I've seen commonly

00:22:50.267 --> 00:22:52.033
I imagine you have
as well Freddie

00:22:52.067 --> 00:22:54.067
is this kind
of incredulousness

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:57.501
from your white acquaintance.

00:22:57.534 --> 00:23:02.601
I had no idea,
totally either blind

00:23:02.634 --> 00:23:04.501
or reflectively
presenting themselves

00:23:04.534 --> 00:23:06.501
as blind

00:23:06.534 --> 00:23:10.968
to the lived experiences
of those around us.

00:23:11.033 --> 00:23:13.367
Oh, no way,
can you believe it?

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:15.367
I had no idea something
like this would happen!

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:18.701
And so it's the combination
of distancing,

00:23:18.734 --> 00:23:22.000
minimizing that experience,
whether consciously or not,

00:23:22.033 --> 00:23:24.167
and then making the burden

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:26.334
of solving it
on the person of color.

00:23:26.367 --> 00:23:32.601
It's sort of the whole idea
that racism is an issue

00:23:32.634 --> 00:23:34.767
that the marginalized
community has to solve

00:23:34.801 --> 00:23:37.901
or it's like a black issue
or an Asian issue

00:23:37.934 --> 00:23:39.634
or whichever
community that is.

00:23:39.667 --> 00:23:40.734
And it is not.

00:23:40.767 --> 00:23:43.200
It is a white issue,
an issue of the oppressor

00:23:43.234 --> 00:23:46.100
and of those with privilege
and those in power.

00:23:46.133 --> 00:23:47.067
Yeah.

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:55.434
All right, so let's move on
to our next pairing

00:23:55.467 --> 00:23:57.868
and it's going to be
JC and Garnet,

00:23:57.901 --> 00:24:01.567
both of whom are currently
based in Arizona.

00:24:09.033 --> 00:24:09.734
Hello?

00:24:09.767 --> 00:24:10.968
Hi.

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:12.467
Hi, my name's Garnet.

00:24:12.501 --> 00:24:13.334
I'm JC.

00:24:13.367 --> 00:24:14.467
Hi, JC.

00:24:14.501 --> 00:24:15.501
I love your outfit.

00:24:15.534 --> 00:24:16.434
Thank you.

00:24:16.467 --> 00:24:18.234
Where are you located?

00:24:18.267 --> 00:24:20.300
I'm in Arizona.
Where you at?

00:24:20.334 --> 00:24:23.334
Oh, that's where
I'm currently safer at home.

00:24:23.367 --> 00:24:24.300
Really?

00:24:24.334 --> 00:24:25.567
Yeah.

00:24:31.334 --> 00:24:33.167
Now we're getting stared at.

00:24:37.667 --> 00:24:41.000
Okay, now as this individual,
I'm very nervous.

00:24:41.033 --> 00:24:42.667
That was scary.

00:24:42.701 --> 00:24:44.067
Yeah.

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:45.601
We're in court?

00:24:47.033 --> 00:24:49.234
Are we the attorney?

00:24:49.267 --> 00:24:50.400
I don't know.

00:24:50.434 --> 00:24:52.400
Are we the judge?
Are we the jury?

00:24:52.434 --> 00:24:55.067
Oh, please let
it be the judge.

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:56.067
Oh, thank God.

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:56.868
Yes.

00:24:56.901 --> 00:24:58.267
[snaps fingers and laughs]
[chuckles]

00:24:58.300 --> 00:24:59.267
What got me the most

00:24:59.300 --> 00:25:00.901
was the moment we were
in the courtroom,

00:25:00.934 --> 00:25:02.467
my mind thought,

00:25:02.501 --> 00:25:06.634
okay, they're bringing out
the defendant and it's his P.O.V..

00:25:06.667 --> 00:25:09.934
I was viscerally relieved when
that wasn't the case.

00:25:09.968 --> 00:25:12.033
Yeah, automatically I'm like,
oh my God, I'm nervous

00:25:12.067 --> 00:25:14.501
and I'm nervous because
I hope that this person

00:25:14.534 --> 00:25:17.267
isn't going to get prosecuted
for incorrect reasons.

00:25:17.300 --> 00:25:19.734
So I think I was trying
to compensate with like—

00:25:19.767 --> 00:25:22.200
I hope he's the judge,
I hope he's the attorney,

00:25:22.234 --> 00:25:23.534
I hope he's the jury,

00:25:23.567 --> 00:25:25.367
so I could also like breathe a

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:27.300
sigh of relief from it as well.

00:25:27.334 --> 00:25:29.467
I think that I'm not immediately

00:25:29.501 --> 00:25:31.300
identified by other people,

00:25:31.334 --> 00:25:33.567
especially people
who are white.

00:25:33.601 --> 00:25:38.300
It's not often assumed
that I'm part black.

00:25:38.334 --> 00:25:40.801
It's just assumed
that I'm not white,

00:25:40.834 --> 00:25:43.234
which is not quite the same
especially now.

00:25:43.267 --> 00:25:45.501
For most of my adult life,
I had a shaved head.

00:25:45.534 --> 00:25:50.334
I used to get extra security
screenings, pat-downs,

00:25:50.367 --> 00:25:52.868
taken out of line,
questioned in the back room

00:25:52.901 --> 00:25:55.300
with no windows all the time.

00:25:55.334 --> 00:25:57.901
And I was basically being
racially profiled.

00:25:57.934 --> 00:25:59.701
And it wasn't because
I was part black.

00:25:59.734 --> 00:26:01.400
It was because I looked
Middle Eastern.

00:26:01.434 --> 00:26:02.801
And my mom said,

00:26:02.834 --> 00:26:04.834
"You need to shave
your hair."

00:26:04.868 --> 00:26:07.601
My first instinct was like,
yeah, that's a good idea.

00:26:07.634 --> 00:26:09.334
And then my next reaction was,

00:26:09.367 --> 00:26:12.634
no, that's the opposite
of what I want to do

00:26:12.667 --> 00:26:14.667
because that would mean

00:26:14.701 --> 00:26:16.934
I would be taking
one more visibly

00:26:16.968 --> 00:26:20.701
at-least-part-black individual
out of the situation.

00:26:20.734 --> 00:26:24.167
And that's exactly what people
who hate black people

00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:25.567
want to happen.

00:26:25.601 --> 00:26:27.300
They just want
fewer black people around.

00:26:27.334 --> 00:26:30.267
Yes, your hair is political!
[snaps fingers]

00:26:30.300 --> 00:26:31.868
- [laughs]

00:26:31.901 --> 00:26:36.133
- And I can relate to a lot of
what you were talking about.

00:26:36.167 --> 00:26:37.801
I also traveled a lot

00:26:37.834 --> 00:26:39.767
as a flight attendant
for the past six years.

00:26:39.801 --> 00:26:40.834
- Okay.

00:26:40.868 --> 00:26:42.567
And I also was getting profiled

00:26:42.601 --> 00:26:44.200
a lot as like Middle Eastern.

00:26:44.234 --> 00:26:46.567
And I've had pretty
serious altercation

00:26:46.601 --> 00:26:48.734
with one pilot in the cockpit

00:26:48.767 --> 00:26:51.234
that was treating
me really horribly

00:26:51.267 --> 00:26:55.901
and they basically
were pinning me

00:26:55.934 --> 00:26:58.100
to the inside
of the cockpit door

00:26:58.133 --> 00:27:02.934
and it was just so, so, so
scary [laughs] to have that.

00:27:02.968 --> 00:27:05.133
That's tough.
I'm sorry to hear that.

00:27:05.167 --> 00:27:08.067
I'm 25 percent black
and I'm 75 percent white.

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:10.567
I'm not American,
I'm actually Canadian.

00:27:10.601 --> 00:27:14.400
But it wasn't until
I arrived here in the U.S.

00:27:14.434 --> 00:27:16.667
that people started to regularly

00:27:16.701 --> 00:27:18.801
recognize the black part of me

00:27:18.834 --> 00:27:21.267
and mostly that
were other black people.

00:27:21.300 --> 00:27:25.834
And so I started to—
I would go get my hair cut,

00:27:25.868 --> 00:27:28.968
I would always seek out
like the blackest barbershop

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:31.067
because whenever I walked
into a place like that,

00:27:31.100 --> 00:27:34.834
there was never like,
"Who's this beige guy?"

00:27:34.868 --> 00:27:36.367
[chuckles]

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:37.734
"What's he about?"

00:27:37.767 --> 00:27:39.133
They automatically were like,

00:27:39.167 --> 00:27:40.267
yeah, this guy's part black and

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:41.334
he's part of this community.

00:27:41.367 --> 00:27:45.801
I identify as transgender
non-conforming.

00:27:45.834 --> 00:27:49.234
I basically wake up
and every single day

00:27:49.267 --> 00:27:51.300
is a little gender-reveal
party to myself.

00:27:51.334 --> 00:27:52.434
[laughs]

00:27:52.467 --> 00:27:53.400
Really?

00:27:53.434 --> 00:27:54.701
Because I just like
go with the flow.

00:27:54.734 --> 00:27:56.100
I feel like today I'm like,

00:27:56.133 --> 00:27:58.100
"I'm feeling more masculine,
I'm feeling more feminine."

00:27:58.133 --> 00:27:59.767
But when I would go to work

00:27:59.801 --> 00:28:01.834
and have to wear
a uniform with a tie,

00:28:01.868 --> 00:28:04.067
I would purposefully find
like one or two things

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:06.067
to "express my gender,"

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:08.467
whether that was the woman's
purse or something.

00:28:08.501 --> 00:28:12.200
And I would get constantly
asked by coworkers like,

00:28:12.234 --> 00:28:15.067
"Is that really—
are you allowed to do that?

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:16.701
They're not saying
anything to you?

00:28:16.734 --> 00:28:18.133
They're not
writing you letters?"

00:28:18.167 --> 00:28:20.801
And I'm like, "I'm wearing a
bag, the same bag you have,

00:28:20.834 --> 00:28:22.467
so why— like, no."

00:28:22.501 --> 00:28:23.634
Yeah.

00:28:23.667 --> 00:28:27.067
And it was really frustrating
to have to constantly validate

00:28:27.100 --> 00:28:30.234
my gender or like myself
or even have to explain,

00:28:30.267 --> 00:28:31.801
especially to folks

00:28:31.834 --> 00:28:33.734
who didn't really want
to be educated about it.

00:28:33.767 --> 00:28:35.734
They were just
sort of projecting

00:28:35.767 --> 00:28:37.200
their own views onto me.

00:28:37.234 --> 00:28:38.701
That would be
really challenging,

00:28:38.734 --> 00:28:40.100
definitely challenging.

00:28:40.133 --> 00:28:42.000
I mean I was in my early 20s
and I had a roommate.

00:28:42.033 --> 00:28:43.567
And it was around that time

00:28:43.601 --> 00:28:45.167
That I started
to sort of come into

00:28:45.200 --> 00:28:46.601
feeling out being part black

00:28:46.634 --> 00:28:48.501
and wanting to embrace
that part of myself

00:28:48.534 --> 00:28:49.868
because, up until then, I hadn't.

00:28:49.901 --> 00:28:52.367
I read Malcolm X's autobiography.

00:28:52.400 --> 00:28:54.968
I even went and saw
Louis Farrakhan speak.

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.767
I was just exploring.

00:28:57.801 --> 00:28:59.868
And he kind of confronted me in

00:28:59.901 --> 00:29:01.901
a way that I wasn't expecting.

00:29:01.934 --> 00:29:03.734
And this is one
of my best friends.

00:29:03.767 --> 00:29:05.767
He said, "Why are you
reading that?"

00:29:05.801 --> 00:29:07.167
or, "Why are you getting into

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:08.667
all of this like black stuff?"

00:29:08.701 --> 00:29:11.100
And I was like, "Well,
you know, I'm part black."

00:29:11.133 --> 00:29:14.968
And he's like, "Well, you're
only 25 percent black,

00:29:15.033 --> 00:29:17.968
so why don't you just get over it

00:29:18.033 --> 00:29:21.734
and embrace the majority
of yourself, which is white?"

00:29:21.767 --> 00:29:23.167
And I was really angry

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:27.000
because the fact is that
you only have to be

00:29:27.033 --> 00:29:28.434
a little bit non-white

00:29:28.467 --> 00:29:30.601
for people to see you
as not white.

00:29:30.634 --> 00:29:34.200
And that can really, for lack
of a better word,

00:29:34.234 --> 00:29:35.868
"color" your experience
in the world.

00:29:35.901 --> 00:29:37.200
Exactly.

00:29:37.234 --> 00:29:39.067
When you are in white spaces

00:29:39.100 --> 00:29:42.033
And you talk
about your blackness,

00:29:42.067 --> 00:29:43.934
how is that received?

00:29:43.968 --> 00:29:45.634
That's a good question.

00:29:45.667 --> 00:29:49.033
White people always want to ask

00:29:49.067 --> 00:29:50.367
what my background is.

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:52.234
[gasps and laughs]
Oh, God!

00:29:52.267 --> 00:29:53.334
Yeah, same.

00:29:53.367 --> 00:29:54.567
Where are you from?

00:29:54.601 --> 00:29:56.100
You're like, "Oh, well,
originally I'm from Calgary."

00:29:56.133 --> 00:29:57.567
"Where are your parents from?"

00:29:57.601 --> 00:29:59.701
- [laughing]
- "Oh, well, my dad's from Calgary

00:29:59.734 --> 00:30:01.667
and my mom's from Manitoba."

00:30:01.701 --> 00:30:03.367
They're like, "No, but where
are they really from?"

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:04.801
And I'm like, "Oh, I see, you're

00:30:04.834 --> 00:30:06.133
wondering why I'm not white."

00:30:06.167 --> 00:30:09.167
Because they want to get
to what race I am,

00:30:09.200 --> 00:30:11.100
where do I fit into their idea—

00:30:11.133 --> 00:30:12.334
Yep.

00:30:12.367 --> 00:30:14.434
—of who I should be
or what I represent.

00:30:14.467 --> 00:30:16.467
I understand exactly
what you're saying.

00:30:16.501 --> 00:30:21.467
Was there ever a time in your
life when you found yourself

00:30:21.501 --> 00:30:25.234
either suppressing
a part of your identity,

00:30:25.267 --> 00:30:27.234
whatever that may be,

00:30:27.267 --> 00:30:31.467
in order to make a social
situation or any situation

00:30:31.501 --> 00:30:33.133
easier or more comfortable

00:30:33.167 --> 00:30:35.100
for everyone in the room
kind of thing?

00:30:35.133 --> 00:30:36.868
I was like 12 or 13 years old

00:30:36.901 --> 00:30:39.634
and I just wanted to be like
a Manic Pixie Dream Girl

00:30:39.667 --> 00:30:42.634
that sits on countertops
and is cute and weird,

00:30:42.667 --> 00:30:44.467
and I just wanted
to be that for myself.

00:30:44.501 --> 00:30:47.133
So I was expressing myself
in that way

00:30:47.167 --> 00:30:49.200
and I remember
these men in my family

00:30:49.234 --> 00:30:50.734
pulled me aside and they were,

00:30:50.767 --> 00:30:54.067
"You're acting feminine
and you need to like macho-up

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:56.000
because you're turning
into like a young man,"

00:30:56.033 --> 00:30:57.434
and blah-blah-blah-blah.

00:30:57.467 --> 00:30:59.300
That's the way it felt,

00:30:59.334 --> 00:31:01.467
oh my God,
I'm doing something wrong

00:31:01.501 --> 00:31:05.467
and this really hurts my
family and I'm being shameful.

00:31:05.501 --> 00:31:12.601
And that moment
got internalized for years,

00:31:12.634 --> 00:31:15.067
like 10, 14, whatever years.

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:17.033
Even if I'm trying
to just change my diet,

00:31:17.067 --> 00:31:19.501
I feel like I have to like almost

00:31:19.534 --> 00:31:21.567
feel ashamed of my lifestyle

00:31:21.601 --> 00:31:23.534
as I explain it to my family.

00:31:23.567 --> 00:31:26.300
And it comes from
just years and years

00:31:26.334 --> 00:31:32.334
of never getting my needs met
or being seen as who I am.

00:31:32.367 --> 00:31:33.501
Mm.

00:31:33.534 --> 00:31:34.434
Yeah.

00:31:34.467 --> 00:31:36.267
And this might strike you
as, I don't know,

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:38.033
I don't know how
you're going to take this.

00:31:38.067 --> 00:31:39.667
But a time when I've suppressed

00:31:39.701 --> 00:31:43.501
something about myself
is I like karaoke.

00:31:43.534 --> 00:31:44.701
Yes!

00:31:44.734 --> 00:31:46.434
[laughs]
And when I was in Toronto,

00:31:46.467 --> 00:31:48.934
I was a karaoke host
for a long time, full time.

00:31:48.968 --> 00:31:53.300
And the best place that
I hosted at was a gay bar.

00:31:53.334 --> 00:31:54.801
Yes.

00:31:54.834 --> 00:31:56.501
And when I worked there,

00:31:56.534 --> 00:32:01.534
I found myself inwardly
embracing the fact

00:32:01.567 --> 00:32:04.501
that maybe people
thought I was gay

00:32:04.534 --> 00:32:06.200
because I happened
to be hosting

00:32:06.234 --> 00:32:07.901
a karaoke show in a gay bar.

00:32:07.934 --> 00:32:10.267
And I was happy about that.

00:32:10.300 --> 00:32:11.767
This actually brings up

00:32:11.801 --> 00:32:14.100
an important question
that I think is relevant,

00:32:14.133 --> 00:32:19.067
how do we consume and
appreciate and amplify voices

00:32:19.100 --> 00:32:22.334
that need to be heard without
being appropriating

00:32:22.367 --> 00:32:24.300
and/or gaining anything from it.

00:32:24.334 --> 00:32:26.234
Because as an ally,

00:32:26.267 --> 00:32:30.000
clearly I see you as an ally
for LGBT community,

00:32:30.033 --> 00:32:32.300
especially working
and hosting in a gay bar

00:32:32.334 --> 00:32:34.200
and being super
comfortable in there.

00:32:34.234 --> 00:32:38.901
But in what ways has your
white passing experience,

00:32:38.934 --> 00:32:40.934
how has that
showed up for you?

00:32:40.968 --> 00:32:42.567
I worked in a place

00:32:42.601 --> 00:32:45.200
that was a very high-level,
private-members club.

00:32:45.234 --> 00:32:48.000
I don't even think there
were any black members.

00:32:48.033 --> 00:32:51.934
And I was witness to people
talking about things

00:32:51.968 --> 00:32:54.501
that were blatantly racist,

00:32:54.534 --> 00:32:58.367
like telling N-word jokes
and stuff.

00:32:58.400 --> 00:33:00.467
I'm standing right there
and I'm thinking to myself,

00:33:00.501 --> 00:33:03.200
oh, they don't realize
I'm part black.

00:33:03.234 --> 00:33:07.801
And so at the moment,
I just didn't say anything

00:33:07.834 --> 00:33:10.000
because I didn't want to have
that confrontation

00:33:10.033 --> 00:33:11.734
in that environment.

00:33:11.767 --> 00:33:14.000
I want to just do
a quick little example I had

00:33:14.033 --> 00:33:15.901
from when
I was a flight attendant.

00:33:15.934 --> 00:33:18.300
And I was in the back of the
airplane and we were in flight

00:33:18.334 --> 00:33:20.701
and all of a sudden
I heard some music

00:33:20.734 --> 00:33:22.968
or something happening
in the cabin

00:33:23.000 --> 00:33:26.467
and I instantly went to this
black couple to go ask them

00:33:26.501 --> 00:33:29.067
to turn off their phones or
their speakers or whatever.

00:33:29.100 --> 00:33:31.467
And when I went there
they had nothing

00:33:31.501 --> 00:33:32.801
and then I was like,
"Excuse me,"

00:33:32.834 --> 00:33:34.167
and then they just
looked at me

00:33:34.200 --> 00:33:35.567
and they're like, "What's up?"

00:33:35.601 --> 00:33:37.133
And I'm like, oh my God;

00:33:37.167 --> 00:33:39.534
I just did something
that was super biased.

00:33:39.567 --> 00:33:41.701
It just really showed me
the bias that I had.

00:33:41.734 --> 00:33:44.234
I was just so inappropriate.

00:33:44.267 --> 00:33:46.133
JC, let me ask you a question.

00:33:46.167 --> 00:33:49.133
Why do you think bias
is dangerous?

00:33:50.634 --> 00:33:56.300
I don't think bias itself
is necessarily the danger.

00:33:56.334 --> 00:34:01.300
It's not recognizing that you
have bias that is dangerous.

00:34:01.334 --> 00:34:05.701
That eventually trickles down
to resentment and then to hate

00:34:05.734 --> 00:34:10.334
and then to extreme actions
that are just inexcusable

00:34:10.367 --> 00:34:12.567
and dangerous
for so many folks.

00:34:12.601 --> 00:34:14.934
Do you think that knowing

00:34:14.968 --> 00:34:19.200
that people have conscious
and unconscious biases

00:34:19.234 --> 00:34:22.701
do you think it's possible
for people to overcome?

00:34:22.734 --> 00:34:24.801
Not only do I think
that it's possible

00:34:24.834 --> 00:34:26.534
for people to overcome
their biases,

00:34:26.567 --> 00:34:31.601
I think people's lives
depend on it right now.

00:34:31.634 --> 00:34:34.534
People need to recognize
their own bias

00:34:34.567 --> 00:34:37.234
in how they can show up
and fix those biases

00:34:37.267 --> 00:34:40.133
for literally the survival
of black people.

00:34:40.167 --> 00:34:41.901
They can no
longer just show up

00:34:41.934 --> 00:34:43.767
by posting pictures
on Instagram,

00:34:43.801 --> 00:34:45.667
even walking in marches.

00:34:45.701 --> 00:34:47.100
It's like do this now because it's

00:34:47.133 --> 00:34:48.434
important right now in history,

00:34:48.467 --> 00:34:51.334
but do this from now on and
call it out when you see it

00:34:51.367 --> 00:34:52.801
and call it out within yourself.

00:34:52.834 --> 00:34:54.534
And also don't take
offense to it

00:34:54.567 --> 00:34:56.734
because you learned it
somewhere,

00:34:56.767 --> 00:34:58.267
but now you can unlearn it.

00:34:58.300 --> 00:34:59.701
And after you do that,

00:34:59.734 --> 00:35:01.300
you have the opportunity
to change as well.

00:35:01.334 --> 00:35:03.234
Well, and I think you're right.

00:35:03.267 --> 00:35:05.000
And I think it's more important

00:35:05.033 --> 00:35:06.334
to talk about, firstly,

00:35:06.367 --> 00:35:08.767
our own bias,
our personal bias,

00:35:08.801 --> 00:35:13.367
because calling someone
else's bias is easy to do

00:35:13.400 --> 00:35:16.367
but it also creates confrontation.

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:19.100
But I think if people witness
one another

00:35:19.133 --> 00:35:21.033
acknowledging
their own bias,

00:35:21.067 --> 00:35:24.334
I think that will open up
the ability for people

00:35:24.367 --> 00:35:29.067
to have more sort of truthful,
honest dialogue about it.

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:32.300
And it's sort of coming
from the 'me'

00:35:32.334 --> 00:35:34.634
rather than coming
from the 'you'.

00:35:34.667 --> 00:35:35.734
Yeah.

00:35:35.767 --> 00:35:40.367
Well, I think this was
a very interesting experience.

00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:43.467
I want to come away
from this dialogue

00:35:43.501 --> 00:35:46.934
with a more open mind
and open heart to strangers

00:35:46.968 --> 00:35:49.300
and not automatically
pass judgment on them

00:35:49.334 --> 00:35:51.701
and just learn from them and
see what they're all about.

00:35:51.734 --> 00:35:55.667
My biggest takeaway is maybe
how do we structure that?

00:35:55.701 --> 00:35:57.934
And I actually think
the video was helpful

00:35:57.968 --> 00:36:00.367
in terms of opening up
a dialogue.

00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:02.767
It just really opened up the
door for all these avenues

00:36:02.801 --> 00:36:04.567
for our own experiences
to come through

00:36:04.601 --> 00:36:06.300
and it was really cool.

00:36:06.334 --> 00:36:07.434
Yeah, if we're not that far

00:36:07.467 --> 00:36:08.767
apart, maybe I'll bump into you.

00:36:08.801 --> 00:36:09.868
[laughs]

00:36:09.901 --> 00:36:11.734
Yeah, maybe I'll see you
at the grocery store

00:36:11.767 --> 00:36:13.567
or something.
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:13.601 --> 00:36:14.767
All right, bye.

00:36:14.801 --> 00:36:16.901
Bye.
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:33.067 --> 00:36:34.634
All right, well, that quickly

00:36:34.667 --> 00:36:38.167
became one of my favorite
conversations of the day.

00:36:38.200 --> 00:36:42.234
JC and Garnet spoke to a whole
variety of things actually,

00:36:42.267 --> 00:36:44.834
half of which that I can
absolutely relate to

00:36:44.868 --> 00:36:47.367
and trigger experiences
of my own

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:50.100
in terms of ambiguity,
in terms of race

00:36:50.133 --> 00:36:54.033
and labeling and people's need
to know what you are.

00:36:54.067 --> 00:36:56.200
And I also learned a great deal

00:36:56.234 --> 00:37:01.934
and appreciated JC
kind of checking in

00:37:01.968 --> 00:37:03.901
with Garnet about
a couple of things

00:37:03.934 --> 00:37:06.367
and talking about allyship
versus appropriation

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:09.968
and how to be responsible and
thoughtful in that process.

00:37:10.033 --> 00:37:12.634
Yeah, I mean first of all,
they were so cute.

00:37:12.667 --> 00:37:15.067
It was such a pleasant
conversation and I was like,

00:37:15.100 --> 00:37:18.601
oh, like I hope they become
actual friends after this.

00:37:18.634 --> 00:37:19.334
Right.

00:37:19.367 --> 00:37:21.367
Also, they're local so, yeah,
it's actually doable.

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:22.868
Exactly, yeah.

00:37:22.901 --> 00:37:27.167
And I think they were both
incredibly respectful

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:30.701
and they had a lot
of I think similarities

00:37:30.734 --> 00:37:34.801
in some of their experiences
of both living—

00:37:34.834 --> 00:37:36.467
or I guess
sort of being ambiguous

00:37:36.501 --> 00:37:39.267
in how they present
gender-wise

00:37:39.300 --> 00:37:42.434
and also, you know, racially.

00:37:42.467 --> 00:37:44.300
So I think that this was a

00:37:44.334 --> 00:37:46.534
really productive conversation.

00:37:46.567 --> 00:37:51.000
Right, yeah, very much
highlights ingroup/outgroup

00:37:51.033 --> 00:37:52.567
and the role that that plays.

00:37:52.601 --> 00:37:54.100
I mean folks
are making comments

00:37:54.133 --> 00:37:55.667
because that's how they feel.

00:37:55.701 --> 00:37:59.300
And we often check
our surroundings,

00:37:59.334 --> 00:38:01.634
"boys will be boys"
locker-room conversation

00:38:01.667 --> 00:38:03.334
is probably different
in the locker-room

00:38:03.367 --> 00:38:05.033
with a bunch of cis jocks

00:38:05.067 --> 00:38:07.300
than it would be
at Thanksgiving dinner

00:38:07.334 --> 00:38:09.501
with a whole panoply
of other folks.

00:38:09.534 --> 00:38:11.267
So we constantly check.

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:13.067
We have our inside/outside
conversations,

00:38:13.100 --> 00:38:14.767
ingroup/outgroup conversations.

00:38:14.801 --> 00:38:16.667
There's a default to what are you?

00:38:16.701 --> 00:38:18.434
And this is coming from somebody

00:38:18.467 --> 00:38:20.767
who my entire life probably
the most common question

00:38:20.801 --> 00:38:22.868
that has been asked of me,

00:38:22.901 --> 00:38:26.434
the most popular three
words to me is,

00:38:26.467 --> 00:38:29.167
"What are you?"
since I was very little.

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:32.467
And it's in the first five
sentences of many interactions

00:38:32.501 --> 00:38:34.434
since I was little,
"What are you?"

00:38:34.467 --> 00:38:36.434
And what is that question?

00:38:36.467 --> 00:38:40.033
Why don't we talk
and you'll find out what I am,

00:38:40.067 --> 00:38:41.934
because represent themselves
through their actions.

00:38:41.968 --> 00:38:46.400
But you need some kind of
label in order to categorize.

00:38:46.434 --> 00:38:47.734
It's not always ill-intentioned,

00:38:47.767 --> 00:38:48.968
but it's definitely annoying.

00:38:49.033 --> 00:38:51.934
And for both of these folks,

00:38:51.968 --> 00:38:56.467
it was nice to see them
have a shared experience

00:38:56.501 --> 00:38:58.234
because they probably don't get it-

00:38:58.267 --> 00:38:59.534
who am I to say,

00:38:59.567 --> 00:39:01.501
but it's possible
they don't get it very often.

00:39:01.534 --> 00:39:05.801
Garnet certainly seemed like
he doesn't often get to relate

00:39:05.834 --> 00:39:08.467
to people on that level of
what it's like to be ambiguous

00:39:08.501 --> 00:39:10.534
and always need to be labeled.

00:39:10.567 --> 00:39:12.467
There is also I think
is worth mentioning

00:39:12.501 --> 00:39:14.467
a difference between ambiguity

00:39:14.501 --> 00:39:17.634
and looking like
you're a person of "color"

00:39:17.667 --> 00:39:19.067
versus being black.

00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:21.868
Anti-blackness is so specific

00:39:21.901 --> 00:39:24.501
and it's filled with such
vitriol and tradition,

00:39:24.534 --> 00:39:27.601
in this country at least,

00:39:27.634 --> 00:39:29.934
that it kind of stands on its own.

00:39:29.968 --> 00:39:31.734
And by "kind of" I mean definitely.

00:39:31.767 --> 00:39:34.400
I was looking at some
of Professor L'Pree's notes

00:39:34.434 --> 00:39:35.934
and she was saying

00:39:35.968 --> 00:39:38.267
that based on some
very specific research,

00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:39.968
it is proven that when strangers

00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:41.567
are walking about in the world

00:39:41.601 --> 00:39:43.667
and we are passing each
other on the streets

00:39:43.701 --> 00:39:45.567
and standing in line
behind each other

00:39:45.601 --> 00:39:47.267
at restaurants or et cetera,

00:39:47.300 --> 00:39:49.734
like those who are
ethnically ambiguous

00:39:49.767 --> 00:39:51.567
get started at way longer

00:39:51.601 --> 00:39:53.767
than those who you're able
to easily categorize

00:39:53.801 --> 00:39:55.067
and put in a box.

00:39:55.100 --> 00:39:57.334
And I wanted to know like
have you ever found yourself

00:39:57.367 --> 00:39:58.901
when you're walking
through the world—

00:39:58.934 --> 00:40:01.601
aside from because of the fact
you're Jesse Williams

00:40:01.634 --> 00:40:04.868
and you are well-known,
but even before then,

00:40:04.901 --> 00:40:06.434
have you ever experienced

00:40:06.467 --> 00:40:08.200
really noticing people
glaring at you

00:40:08.234 --> 00:40:10.000
or knowing that someone

00:40:10.033 --> 00:40:11.667
is really trying
got figure you out?

00:40:11.701 --> 00:40:12.701
Absolutely.

00:40:12.734 --> 00:40:14.667
In my entire childhood
coming up,

00:40:14.701 --> 00:40:17.868
especially because I lived
in entirely black areas,

00:40:17.901 --> 00:40:20.701
entirely white areas, entirely
poverty stricken' areas,

00:40:20.734 --> 00:40:23.467
entirely kind of lower-middle
class and big—

00:40:23.501 --> 00:40:25.868
and we have a lot

00:40:25.901 --> 00:40:28.067
of very insulated
pockets in this country

00:40:28.100 --> 00:40:29.734
and I've lived in many of them

00:40:29.767 --> 00:40:31.834
and have not fit in any of them.

00:40:31.868 --> 00:40:35.601
So, yeah, I've been the object
of that kind of staring.

00:40:35.634 --> 00:40:37.968
And because they don't know,

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:40.434
derision often comes with that.

00:40:40.467 --> 00:40:43.400
I resent the fact that you're
making it harder for me.

00:40:43.434 --> 00:40:45.167
It's worth noting also

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:48.901
just that JC rightly
pointed out to Garnet

00:40:48.934 --> 00:40:51.767
we should be thinking
about how to be an ally,

00:40:51.801 --> 00:40:53.834
how to be a productive ally

00:40:53.868 --> 00:40:56.534
without appropriating culture

00:40:56.567 --> 00:40:58.968
and making sure you are
still your authentic self

00:40:59.033 --> 00:41:00.834
without kind of dibbing
and dabbing

00:41:00.868 --> 00:41:03.067
and sampling cultures without
actually having any interest

00:41:03.100 --> 00:41:05.234
in the roots of their history
and how they're impacted

00:41:05.267 --> 00:41:07.400
initial a multitude of ways.

00:41:13.667 --> 00:41:15.501
All right, let's go into another

00:41:15.534 --> 00:41:16.968
one of our conversations.

00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:20.167
This one is between Marlee

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:22.534
and she's talking to Renee.

00:41:31.133 --> 00:41:32.300
Hello?

00:41:32.334 --> 00:41:33.400
Hi.

00:41:33.434 --> 00:41:34.801
Hi.

00:41:34.834 --> 00:41:35.400
Hi!

00:41:35.434 --> 00:41:36.200
Hi!

00:41:36.234 --> 00:41:37.300
How are you?

00:41:37.334 --> 00:41:39.100
I'm great. How are you?

00:41:39.133 --> 00:41:41.434
Good, I'm Marlee,
nice to meet you.

00:41:41.467 --> 00:41:43.868
And I'm Renee, nice
to meet you as well.

00:41:56.100 --> 00:41:58.834
So I've seen that happen.

00:41:58.868 --> 00:42:00.834
I mean even in L.A., as liberal

00:42:00.868 --> 00:42:02.501
and progressive of a place

00:42:02.534 --> 00:42:04.534
that we think we live in.

00:42:04.567 --> 00:42:08.767
Well, it's nothing new to me,
nothing to us.

00:42:08.801 --> 00:42:13.234
And it's something that
we've come to expect,

00:42:13.267 --> 00:42:17.834
something that we experience a lot

00:42:17.868 --> 00:42:20.300
and basically pretty
much all the time.

00:42:20.334 --> 00:42:27.167
And it's a shame because it's
based on what we look like.

00:42:27.200 --> 00:42:29.534
You could have all
the degrees in the world,

00:42:29.567 --> 00:42:32.601
but it has nothing to do
with any of that.

00:42:32.634 --> 00:42:35.167
You know, I went to high school,

00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:37.601
there was like one black
family in my hometown

00:42:37.634 --> 00:42:40.868
and there was a lot
of that happening.

00:42:40.901 --> 00:42:43.434
It happens every day.
You see it every day.

00:42:43.467 --> 00:42:46.267
I found myself breathing hard.

00:42:46.300 --> 00:42:48.868
My heart started beating fast

00:42:48.901 --> 00:42:53.901
because it's so typical
and it makes me angry.

00:42:53.934 --> 00:42:56.000
When we go into a store,

00:42:56.033 --> 00:42:58.400
it doesn't matter
how well-dressed we are,

00:42:58.434 --> 00:43:02.734
sometimes we're followed
immediately, we're assumed.

00:43:02.767 --> 00:43:06.000
And it bothers me, it really does.

00:43:06.033 --> 00:43:07.734
And it's so unfair.

00:43:07.767 --> 00:43:10.300
Yeah, I just want
to say to that, Renee,

00:43:10.334 --> 00:43:13.534
that I'm bothered for you
and it makes me so angry.

00:43:13.567 --> 00:43:15.033
There's so much

00:43:15.067 --> 00:43:17.167
that I'm learning
and unlearning that I realize

00:43:17.200 --> 00:43:20.267
just being born white
and with a certain privilege

00:43:20.300 --> 00:43:22.000
and where I grew up

00:43:22.033 --> 00:43:24.300
that there's so much
that's just innate

00:43:24.334 --> 00:43:26.868
or learned behavior
that you're learning.

00:43:26.901 --> 00:43:31.200
And unlearning and I'm trying
so hard to just grow

00:43:31.234 --> 00:43:32.701
and break the cycle.

00:43:32.734 --> 00:43:35.601
And it's like I just hope that
we can keep moving forward.

00:43:35.634 --> 00:43:37.901
Marlee, you know what I appreciate.

00:43:37.934 --> 00:43:41.234
And I think this is what
touches my heart so much

00:43:41.267 --> 00:43:44.100
is that at least
there's a willingness.

00:43:44.133 --> 00:43:47.901
And that's what I hear
so much from you,

00:43:47.934 --> 00:43:51.133
there's a willingness to listen,

00:43:51.167 --> 00:43:54.000
finally a willingness to listen

00:43:54.033 --> 00:43:56.501
when, for so many years,
it seemed as though

00:43:56.534 --> 00:43:58.300
if we mentioned
something like that,

00:43:58.334 --> 00:43:59.767
you know, the looks, the stares,

00:43:59.801 --> 00:44:01.267
what are you talking about?

00:44:01.300 --> 00:44:03.467
What are you talking about
somebody looked at you?

00:44:03.501 --> 00:44:04.934
For some reason,

00:44:04.968 --> 00:44:07.934
we weren't believed and the
curtain has been pulled back

00:44:07.968 --> 00:44:11.400
showing the truth of our lives,

00:44:11.434 --> 00:44:15.934
the truth of what we've had to
experience for so many years.

00:44:15.968 --> 00:44:18.033
It's so true and I was protesting

00:44:18.067 --> 00:44:19.934
a couple of days ago downtown

00:44:19.968 --> 00:44:22.200
and this black man
stopped me and he—

00:44:22.234 --> 00:44:24.334
because we were holding
"Black Lives Matter" sign—

00:44:24.367 --> 00:44:26.934
and he's like,
"It starts with you guys

00:44:26.968 --> 00:44:28.868
who are here willing to fight,

00:44:28.901 --> 00:44:31.901
put your bodies in front of us
and just like stand up."

00:44:31.934 --> 00:44:34.367
Because I had
a similar experience—

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:37.467
and this is the only way
I can relate where—

00:44:37.501 --> 00:44:39.133
I'm never going to understand

00:44:39.167 --> 00:44:41.334
and I know that
because of my skin color.

00:44:41.367 --> 00:44:43.133
I am very androgynous

00:44:43.167 --> 00:44:45.501
in the way that I dress
and express myself

00:44:45.534 --> 00:44:49.133
and I've gotten threatening
looks and those vibes.

00:44:49.167 --> 00:44:50.701
I know what you're saying

00:44:50.734 --> 00:44:52.267
where it's hard to explain
to people

00:44:52.300 --> 00:44:53.434
like what are
you talking about,

00:44:53.467 --> 00:44:54.434
where I've felt threatened

00:44:54.467 --> 00:44:56.734
because people are afraid
of the unknown.

00:44:56.767 --> 00:44:58.501
They're afraid
of what they don't know,

00:44:58.534 --> 00:45:00.968
so them trying
to figure me out,

00:45:01.000 --> 00:45:03.167
is she a boy, is she a girl?

00:45:03.200 --> 00:45:07.200
I was followed
into a bathroom last fall

00:45:07.234 --> 00:45:09.434
because they thought
I was entering

00:45:09.467 --> 00:45:12.033
the wrong bathroom
for my assigned gender.

00:45:12.067 --> 00:45:14.567
And that was
the first time I felt fear.

00:45:14.601 --> 00:45:17.767
I was shaking, and I'm shaking
just talking about it

00:45:17.801 --> 00:45:21.334
because I was like who's this
person to come in here

00:45:21.367 --> 00:45:23.634
and tell me that I'm not using
the right bathroom

00:45:23.667 --> 00:45:25.534
just based off of how I look?

00:45:25.567 --> 00:45:27.834
But I have that default
of like I'm white

00:45:27.868 --> 00:45:29.367
and, as scared as I was,

00:45:29.400 --> 00:45:31.000
I didn't think they were
going to shoot me.

00:45:31.033 --> 00:45:33.467
I'm never going to know
to that extent.

00:45:33.501 --> 00:45:35.434
It's frustrating,
you see on Facebook

00:45:35.467 --> 00:45:38.167
and I have a mixed bag of
progressives and conservatives

00:45:38.200 --> 00:45:39.834
because of where I'm from

00:45:39.868 --> 00:45:41.300
and there are still
so many people

00:45:41.334 --> 00:45:43.267
that it hasn't clicked in
in saying "All Lives Matter."

00:45:43.300 --> 00:45:45.200
It just shows me that
we have a long way to go,

00:45:45.234 --> 00:45:47.133
but you're right, it's a start.

00:45:47.167 --> 00:45:50.767
Why do you think that
they don't get it?

00:45:50.801 --> 00:45:52.901
Is it because
they don't want to

00:45:52.934 --> 00:45:57.033
or do they think
that we're less tan?

00:45:57.067 --> 00:45:58.901
I wish I could
understand that.

00:45:58.934 --> 00:46:01.767
I think it's everyone is
trapped in their bubble

00:46:01.801 --> 00:46:03.367
and doesn't have
enough exposure.

00:46:03.400 --> 00:46:05.601
They don't have friends
who are of color.

00:46:05.634 --> 00:46:07.734
Yeah, you can be nice
to someone a person of color,

00:46:07.767 --> 00:46:10.167
but that doesn't mean
you have racist thoughts

00:46:10.200 --> 00:46:11.801
or, like in that video,

00:46:11.834 --> 00:46:13.901
you're not going to roll up
your window.

00:46:13.934 --> 00:46:15.667
They don't see
who you are  at work

00:46:15.701 --> 00:46:17.100
versus who you are—

00:46:17.133 --> 00:46:19.801
I know I'm a different person
at work than I am here at home

00:46:19.834 --> 00:46:21.767
with my girlfriend and my dog.

00:46:21.801 --> 00:46:23.501
I feel sorry for them.

00:46:23.534 --> 00:46:25.667
If they knew me,

00:46:25.701 --> 00:46:27.701
they would recognize
that I am a human being

00:46:27.734 --> 00:46:30.400
just like they are.

00:46:30.434 --> 00:46:31.701
They would recognize I'm a

00:46:31.734 --> 00:46:33.300
child of God just like they are.

00:46:33.334 --> 00:46:36.634
Why are we so hated
in this country?

00:46:36.667 --> 00:46:40.501
It makes me
angry and it hurts.

00:46:40.534 --> 00:46:45.434
And I have grandsons
that I worry about.

00:46:45.467 --> 00:46:48.501
(voice breaking) They're
sweet as they could be.

00:46:48.534 --> 00:46:50.234
And to have to worry
about them

00:46:50.267 --> 00:46:52.234
not coming home one day,

00:46:52.267 --> 00:46:56.067
that's the hardest thing
for any mother.

00:46:56.100 --> 00:47:00.934
And then to hear George Floyd
to call for his mama?

00:47:00.968 --> 00:47:03.968
I don't have kids.

00:47:04.033 --> 00:47:05.901
I would like to someday.

00:47:05.934 --> 00:47:07.467
I was just wondering

00:47:07.501 --> 00:47:10.067
if you could expand
a little bit on that,

00:47:10.100 --> 00:47:11.968
how that resonated with you

00:47:12.033 --> 00:47:14.067
when George Floyd
did call out for his mom.

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:16.801
I think that, as a mom,

00:47:16.834 --> 00:47:19.901
I think that is
the greatest joy I think

00:47:19.934 --> 00:47:23.234
that I have ever had
was to have children.

00:47:23.267 --> 00:47:25.734
And it's just a miracle to me.

00:47:25.767 --> 00:47:27.767
It doesn't matter
what color you are,

00:47:27.801 --> 00:47:31.200
the fact that you have this child

00:47:31.234 --> 00:47:35.400
that you carried under your heart.

00:47:35.434 --> 00:47:36.667
So I mean you would do

00:47:36.701 --> 00:47:38.501
anything to protect that child.

00:47:38.534 --> 00:47:43.334
To take it maliciously like
that in front of people

00:47:43.367 --> 00:47:46.133
and your child is begging
for their life,

00:47:46.167 --> 00:47:49.334
begging "I can't breathe,"
it's just unbearable.

00:47:49.367 --> 00:47:52.067
There are no words, Marlee,
there are just no words.

00:47:52.100 --> 00:47:54.200
There's just so much I think

00:47:54.234 --> 00:47:57.767
that we have to help people
to understand.

00:47:57.801 --> 00:48:02.067
How do you think you can,
going forward,

00:48:02.100 --> 00:48:04.601
help some people understand?

00:48:04.634 --> 00:48:06.400
I think I just need
to be more willing

00:48:06.434 --> 00:48:08.300
to have these uncomfortable
conversations,

00:48:08.334 --> 00:48:09.968
especially with family.

00:48:10.033 --> 00:48:11.801
It's so much easier for me

00:48:11.834 --> 00:48:14.801
to not combat
what they're saying

00:48:14.834 --> 00:48:16.934
to make it easier for me.

00:48:16.968 --> 00:48:18.567
But if I'm making it
easier for me,

00:48:18.601 --> 00:48:22.334
I'm not making it easier
for you or people of color.

00:48:22.367 --> 00:48:26.234
So I know that I need to start
calling these people out

00:48:26.267 --> 00:48:32.334
and it's a lot of energy,
but it's worth the energy

00:48:32.367 --> 00:48:34.534
if people are being murdered
in cold blood.

00:48:34.567 --> 00:48:36.367
Hey, Marlee,
you said something

00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:38.400
about when you're at work

00:48:38.434 --> 00:48:42.667
that you hide your true self,
something like that.

00:48:42.701 --> 00:48:44.467
What was that about?

00:48:44.501 --> 00:48:46.467
When I started working
in the film industry—

00:48:46.501 --> 00:48:48.167
I work in the art
department primarily—

00:48:48.200 --> 00:48:51.701
there was a lot of men,
older men when I joined.

00:48:51.734 --> 00:48:56.367
And I'm androgynous and I look
like how most Cali boys look

00:48:56.400 --> 00:48:58.234
with long blonde hair.

00:48:58.267 --> 00:49:01.701
They would assume sometimes
on my first day at a new job

00:49:01.734 --> 00:49:03.567
that I was just some young guy.

00:49:03.601 --> 00:49:05.267
There were some times

00:49:05.300 --> 00:49:07.567
where I felt like I had
to hush my voice

00:49:07.601 --> 00:49:10.334
or not speak until spoken to
so that they didn't hear

00:49:10.367 --> 00:49:12.334
the tone of my voice
or the high pitch

00:49:12.367 --> 00:49:16.567
because I felt like
they're going to respect me

00:49:16.601 --> 00:49:18.601
first for being a man.

00:49:18.634 --> 00:49:20.567
Like if there's something
I don't know how to do

00:49:20.601 --> 00:49:21.901
because I'm new,

00:49:21.934 --> 00:49:23.367
they're not going
to judge me like,

00:49:23.400 --> 00:49:24.634
"Oh, because
she's a young girl."

00:49:24.667 --> 00:49:28.100
What made you decide that you
wanted to be androgynous?

00:49:28.133 --> 00:49:29.868
Looking back now—

00:49:29.901 --> 00:49:31.934
and like I said,
since I've moved out here

00:49:31.968 --> 00:49:34.000
there's been so much more that
I've discovered about myself,

00:49:34.033 --> 00:49:36.167
in gender, in being queer,

00:49:36.200 --> 00:49:38.501
in playing on the gender spectrum.

00:49:38.534 --> 00:49:40.901
I have this vivid memory
of when I was little.

00:49:41.834 --> 00:49:43.501
I remember my dad
and my brother

00:49:43.534 --> 00:49:44.934
were outside playing.

00:49:44.968 --> 00:49:46.601
My dad was mowing the grass.

00:49:46.634 --> 00:49:48.334
My brother and I were
playing with the ball.

00:49:48.367 --> 00:49:50.734
And they both had their shirts off.

00:49:50.767 --> 00:49:52.801
And I kept pulling my
shirt off and my dad's like,

00:49:52.834 --> 00:49:54.367
"No, you can't do that."

00:49:54.400 --> 00:49:57.501
And I just didn't understand
why that was taboo.

00:49:57.534 --> 00:49:59.000
That's how I feel now.

00:49:59.033 --> 00:50:03.100
I've had so many issues
with body dysphoria

00:50:03.133 --> 00:50:05.701
and ways that I feel about
feminine parts of my body

00:50:05.734 --> 00:50:08.300
that I just now have discovered.

00:50:08.334 --> 00:50:10.868
But looking back
there's been incidences

00:50:10.901 --> 00:50:12.501
that were always there.

00:50:12.534 --> 00:50:14.467
And it was definitely difficult.

00:50:14.501 --> 00:50:16.367
I was like the black sheep
of my family.

00:50:16.400 --> 00:50:17.968
I definitely got
made fun of a lot

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:20.834
because people just
did not understand that.

00:50:20.868 --> 00:50:25.868
I've recently learned that the
black LGBTQIA community

00:50:25.901 --> 00:50:30.968
is disproportionately
marginalized.

00:50:31.033 --> 00:50:33.400
Do you have any insight
as to why that is?

00:50:33.434 --> 00:50:35.200
Well, you know what, Marlee?

00:50:35.234 --> 00:50:37.167
You're going to have to do
me a favor, okay,

00:50:37.200 --> 00:50:39.501
because they keep adding
letters to that.

00:50:39.534 --> 00:50:40.868
So would you do me a favor

00:50:40.901 --> 00:50:44.100
and break down
the new letters, please?

00:50:44.133 --> 00:50:47.300
Lesbian, gay, bisexual,
transgender,

00:50:47.334 --> 00:50:50.167
queer, intersex a-gender.

00:50:50.200 --> 00:50:54.300
I came out as a lesbian
when I was like 20.

00:50:54.334 --> 00:50:56.467
I started to discover
these parts of me

00:50:56.501 --> 00:50:58.267
and realized it was
always there,

00:50:58.300 --> 00:50:59.801
but it was just
kind of suppressed

00:50:59.834 --> 00:51:01.801
because of the area
that I grew up in.

00:51:01.834 --> 00:51:04.901
Even my family,
it was hard for them

00:51:04.934 --> 00:51:07.300
because even though, like I say,

00:51:07.334 --> 00:51:09.367
they're pretty progressive
and open-minded,

00:51:09.400 --> 00:51:12.234
it always comes down to,
well, when it's your own kid

00:51:12.267 --> 00:51:14.334
it's like a different story.

00:51:14.367 --> 00:51:16.334
So I was just kind
of circling back

00:51:16.367 --> 00:51:18.701
just because I've been seeing
so many parallels

00:51:18.734 --> 00:51:22.601
in my own personal growth
and struggles or obstacles

00:51:22.634 --> 00:51:24.133
that I've had to face that,

00:51:24.167 --> 00:51:25.901
like I said, it's not comparable

00:51:25.934 --> 00:51:28.267
in a sense that I will ever
fully understand,

00:51:28.300 --> 00:51:29.901
but I think it's helpful in this

00:51:29.934 --> 00:51:31.534
learning and unlearning process

00:51:31.567 --> 00:51:32.968
of trying to draw your own

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:34.767
experiences as close as you can.

00:51:34.801 --> 00:51:36.167
Back in the '60s,

00:51:36.200 --> 00:51:39.367
we didn't really talk about
that until I got older.

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:41.367
We didn't talk
about those things.

00:51:41.400 --> 00:51:43.000
Do you agree that

00:51:43.033 --> 00:51:47.000
homosexuality is
a sin or not, you know—

00:51:47.033 --> 00:51:50.901
The way I was raised in
the word from adulthood

00:51:50.934 --> 00:51:56.767
is that homosexuality
in and of itself

00:51:56.801 --> 00:52:01.133
the lifestyle
of homosexuality is a sin.

00:52:01.167 --> 00:52:03.634
And in the Bible it does say,

00:52:03.667 --> 00:52:06.567
"That a man shall not lie
with a man."

00:52:06.601 --> 00:52:08.200
So according to the Bible,

00:52:08.234 --> 00:52:12.400
the Bible says everyone has
to live out their own salvation.

00:52:12.434 --> 00:52:14.367
You are going to live your life

00:52:14.400 --> 00:52:17.334
according to the way
you decide to live your life.

00:52:17.367 --> 00:52:19.234
I'm not going to judge you.

00:52:19.267 --> 00:52:20.901
That's not my place.
[laughs]

00:52:20.934 --> 00:52:23.534
Do you think that
being gay is a choice?

00:52:23.567 --> 00:52:25.133
I can't be sure.

00:52:25.167 --> 00:52:28.601
I really can't be sure because
I don't understand—

00:52:28.634 --> 00:52:30.601
just like you say

00:52:30.634 --> 00:52:32.934
that you don't understand
what it's like to be black

00:52:32.968 --> 00:52:34.934
and what you've experienced—

00:52:34.968 --> 00:52:38.434
and to be honest with you,
they are not the same thing.

00:52:38.467 --> 00:52:40.100
They're not.

00:52:40.133 --> 00:52:41.934
Being homosexual
and being black,

00:52:41.968 --> 00:52:43.968
I can't even equate
the two things

00:52:44.033 --> 00:52:46.000
because they're not
the same thing.

00:52:46.033 --> 00:52:50.100
But my not understanding
homosexuality,

00:52:50.133 --> 00:52:52.367
I don't know if it's a physical

00:52:52.400 --> 00:52:54.200
thing, a spiritual thing,

00:52:54.234 --> 00:52:57.200
a psychological thing...
I have no idea.

00:52:57.234 --> 00:53:03.567
I can tell you that, for me
personally, it's not a choice.

00:53:03.601 --> 00:53:08.033
There's been times with people
not understanding sexuality

00:53:08.067 --> 00:53:12.367
or my gender where there's
been so many times

00:53:12.400 --> 00:53:14.434
it's like, if I just
didn't feel like this,

00:53:14.467 --> 00:53:17.801
of course I would
choose the easier route.

00:53:17.834 --> 00:53:19.901
I would choose being straight

00:53:19.934 --> 00:53:23.934
because that's how society
is more accepting.

00:53:23.968 --> 00:53:27.267
I guess if we were ever able
to meet in person

00:53:27.300 --> 00:53:30.667
and you saw someone give me
the discriminatory look

00:53:30.701 --> 00:53:36.534
or made a comment to me
about my identity,

00:53:36.567 --> 00:53:38.300
would you stand up for me?

00:53:38.334 --> 00:53:42.067
Absolutely, I would stand up
for you, Marlee, absolutely.

00:53:42.100 --> 00:53:44.167
No one has a right
to discriminate

00:53:44.200 --> 00:53:47.567
against anyone, anyone.

00:53:47.601 --> 00:53:49.267
No one has that right.

00:53:49.300 --> 00:53:50.167
Yeah.

00:53:50.200 --> 00:53:52.367
And not just me, but I guess
or someone like me

00:53:52.400 --> 00:53:57.834
or especially people in
the black LGBTQ community.

00:53:57.868 --> 00:54:00.901
That's right.
No one has that right.

00:54:00.934 --> 00:54:02.634
Thank you.

00:54:02.667 --> 00:54:06.634
It's just crazy that we've
never even met and don't—

00:54:06.667 --> 00:54:08.334
and people are going to think
this is like a plug,

00:54:08.367 --> 00:54:09.701
I'm like we've never met.
- [laughs]

00:54:09.734 --> 00:54:11.367
- But already just in
these two hours

00:54:11.400 --> 00:54:13.434
I feel so connected to you

00:54:13.467 --> 00:54:16.767
because you have been
so open and willing to hear

00:54:16.801 --> 00:54:18.534
things that I've struggled with

00:54:18.567 --> 00:54:20.834
and I'm just beginning
to only learn of the things

00:54:20.868 --> 00:54:22.868
that you've struggled with.

00:54:22.901 --> 00:54:25.667
But you've been willing
to listen and learn

00:54:25.701 --> 00:54:28.234
just like I'm willing
to listen and learn.

00:54:28.267 --> 00:54:30.234
And it's just really refreshing.

00:54:30.267 --> 00:54:31.934
It is refreshing.

00:54:31.968 --> 00:54:35.000
And I think that's the bottom line.

00:54:35.033 --> 00:54:38.934
I think we have to be open and
willing to listen and learn.

00:54:38.968 --> 00:54:42.501
It doesn't mean that we're
going to agree on everything.

00:54:42.534 --> 00:54:44.968
Right.
[LAUGHTER]

00:54:46.534 --> 00:54:48.200
- Awesome.
- Thank you. Take care.

00:54:48.234 --> 00:54:49.968
- Talk soon, bye.
-Bye!

00:55:01.901 --> 00:55:04.033
So that was a really
unique conversation.

00:55:04.067 --> 00:55:06.033
They were both
really respectful.

00:55:06.067 --> 00:55:07.334
I think it was productive.

00:55:07.367 --> 00:55:10.601
I think from jump initially
when Renee was explaining

00:55:10.634 --> 00:55:12.868
sort of her reaction
to watching &lt;i&gt;The Look&lt;/i&gt;

00:55:12.901 --> 00:55:14.567
and saying—

00:55:14.601 --> 00:55:16.467
she said she found herself
breathing hard

00:55:16.501 --> 00:55:18.534
and her heart
started beating quickly,

00:55:18.567 --> 00:55:20.467
once they entered
the courtroom,

00:55:20.501 --> 00:55:22.200
it's so interesting

00:55:22.234 --> 00:55:26.267
because I completely relate
to those feelings

00:55:26.300 --> 00:55:30.200
when I'm in those types
of uncomfortable situations.

00:55:30.234 --> 00:55:32.367
It's like this visceral—

00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:35.467
these body reactions that I get

00:55:35.501 --> 00:55:39.133
that are subconscious
and I don't realize that,

00:55:39.167 --> 00:55:41.834
oh my gosh, I'm
actually soaked

00:55:41.868 --> 00:55:44.167
because I was sweating
out of nervousness

00:55:44.200 --> 00:55:47.901
for walking past this cop
or something like that.

00:55:47.934 --> 00:55:49.734
And even Marlee brought up

00:55:49.767 --> 00:55:51.300
that she also
experienced that

00:55:51.334 --> 00:55:53.300
when she was walking
into the restroom

00:55:53.334 --> 00:55:55.667
and realized that she had been
followed into the restroom.

00:55:55.701 --> 00:55:57.834
And then her body just
had this reaction

00:55:57.868 --> 00:56:01.267
because it's just like
this very specific reaction

00:56:01.300 --> 00:56:03.000
that I would be
so curious to know

00:56:03.033 --> 00:56:05.434
like white people who check off

00:56:05.467 --> 00:56:07.667
all of these boxes of power,

00:56:07.701 --> 00:56:11.901
do they ever really experience
these body reactions?

00:56:11.934 --> 00:56:16.701
Sometimes I think we get
into a bit of a suffer-off,

00:56:16.734 --> 00:56:18.734
like a competitive suffering,

00:56:18.767 --> 00:56:21.234
like measuring
who's had it worse.

00:56:21.267 --> 00:56:23.868
There is a difference
between saying,

00:56:23.901 --> 00:56:25.901
"I can relate to having
that feeling,

00:56:25.934 --> 00:56:27.634
I've gotten that feeling

00:56:27.667 --> 00:56:29.968
when this has happened
in a coffee shop."

00:56:30.033 --> 00:56:31.734
But it's different than saying,

00:56:31.767 --> 00:56:33.501
"I know exactly what that's like."

00:56:33.534 --> 00:56:36.601
What I did appreciate was
when Marlee was explaining

00:56:36.634 --> 00:56:38.934
her experience of having
those body reactions

00:56:38.968 --> 00:56:41.334
and a little bit of fear
when she realized

00:56:41.367 --> 00:56:43.434
that she had been
followed into the restroom

00:56:43.467 --> 00:56:46.534
was that she said that
she was really scared,

00:56:46.567 --> 00:56:49.300
but she acknowledged that she
wasn't scared for her life,

00:56:49.334 --> 00:56:52.501
which is a key difference in
I think the two experiences.

00:56:52.534 --> 00:56:54.467
And I think that it was
really beneficial

00:56:54.501 --> 00:56:57.767
and productive for her
to validate her feelings

00:56:57.801 --> 00:56:59.567
and acknowledge that
this is how she felt

00:56:59.601 --> 00:57:02.767
in those moments, but
that it is also not the same.

00:57:02.801 --> 00:57:05.033
Yeah, that's exactly
how it should be done.

00:57:05.067 --> 00:57:07.367
You can have a relation to—

00:57:07.400 --> 00:57:09.000
you can let somebody
know they're seen

00:57:09.033 --> 00:57:10.667
without making it equal.

00:57:10.701 --> 00:57:13.534
And I thought it was
interesting in Renee's answer,

00:57:13.567 --> 00:57:17.100
if I have this right, Marlee
asked about how she'd feel—

00:57:17.133 --> 00:57:18.934
"Renee, how do you feel?

00:57:18.968 --> 00:57:20.801
Do you think
that it's a choice,

00:57:20.834 --> 00:57:23.000
do you think that it's a sin?"

00:57:23.033 --> 00:57:26.434
And if I have it correctly
I feel like Renee answered

00:57:26.467 --> 00:57:28.901
not from the outside
how she thinks,

00:57:28.934 --> 00:57:30.801
but what she was taught.

00:57:30.834 --> 00:57:32.067
Taught.

00:57:32.100 --> 00:57:34.501
Which that shows itself
in other conversations

00:57:34.534 --> 00:57:38.501
that we're going through today
too is a bit of a pivot

00:57:38.534 --> 00:57:41.467
from really receiving
the question

00:57:41.501 --> 00:57:43.767
kind of flush but saying,

00:57:43.801 --> 00:57:47.300
"Ah, well, what I was
taught was this."

00:57:47.334 --> 00:57:49.968
Which is fine,
it's important to know,

00:57:50.000 --> 00:57:51.534
but it's not the answer.

00:57:51.567 --> 00:57:53.601
It's not really confronting how

00:57:53.634 --> 00:57:55.734
I feel and I wonder why that is.

00:57:55.767 --> 00:57:57.767
Renee is very similar

00:57:57.801 --> 00:58:00.400
to a lot of the older black
women in my family.

00:58:00.434 --> 00:58:02.234
- You know what I mean?
- Sure.

00:58:02.267 --> 00:58:04.701
Who are still trying
to figure things out

00:58:04.734 --> 00:58:06.434
and me having to explain

00:58:06.467 --> 00:58:08.501
what non-binary is
and all of these things

00:58:08.534 --> 00:58:11.167
to the older black folks
in my family.

00:58:11.200 --> 00:58:14.367
So it's one of those things
that can be frustrating

00:58:14.400 --> 00:58:17.000
if you're not willing
and open to learn.

00:58:17.033 --> 00:58:17.868
Totally.

00:58:17.901 --> 00:58:19.267
Especially, in fairness,

00:58:19.300 --> 00:58:21.567
that is an ever-changing world
of nomenclature

00:58:21.601 --> 00:58:24.801
and I don't want to put people
off from being curious

00:58:24.834 --> 00:58:28.033
with this kind of cancel
culture, that was offensive,

00:58:28.067 --> 00:58:30.067
because I want folks
who are absorbing this

00:58:30.100 --> 00:58:32.067
to feel like they
can ask questions.

00:58:32.100 --> 00:58:33.667
Find a way to
do it respectfully.

00:58:33.701 --> 00:58:35.567
Maybe don't go
"they keep" just saying,

00:58:35.601 --> 00:58:37.467
"Hey, I feel like
I may not be updated."

00:58:37.501 --> 00:58:39.467
Yeah, I think in general
my takeaway

00:58:39.501 --> 00:58:43.067
from Renee and Marlee's
conversation was that it was

00:58:43.100 --> 00:58:46.234
more of sort of like that
generational push and pull

00:58:46.267 --> 00:58:48.701
based on keeping up

00:58:48.734 --> 00:58:51.567
with what's happening
in the LGBTQ community

00:58:51.601 --> 00:58:54.501
and also what's happening
in the black community.

00:58:54.534 --> 00:58:57.567
Renee as a mother,
really touching as a mother

00:58:57.601 --> 00:59:01.734
and being concerned that your
child will return safely home

00:59:01.767 --> 00:59:04.400
and won't be killed or beaten

00:59:04.434 --> 00:59:07.467
at the hands of those
in privilege and in power,

00:59:07.501 --> 00:59:09.033
a.k.a. the police.

00:59:09.067 --> 00:59:10.934
So I think there were a lot
of different topics

00:59:10.968 --> 00:59:13.100
that were touched on
with this conversation

00:59:13.133 --> 00:59:15.400
and I kind of wish
it could have gone longer

00:59:15.434 --> 00:59:17.634
because I think that there was
a lot more to unpack,

00:59:17.667 --> 00:59:21.367
especially on Renee's end in
learning more about Marlee

00:59:21.400 --> 00:59:24.801
and the queer community
and things of that nature.

00:59:24.834 --> 00:59:29.634
Yeah, I would add I think that
Renee, even after the call,

00:59:29.667 --> 00:59:33.033
I would hope that she sits
with the challenges

00:59:33.067 --> 00:59:34.667
that were put before her,

00:59:34.701 --> 00:59:36.634
the difference between
what I was taught

00:59:36.667 --> 00:59:38.367
and what I actually think.

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:40.133
And maybe you don't know
what you think in that time

00:59:40.167 --> 00:59:42.434
because now you're actually
faced with somebody.

00:59:48.634 --> 00:59:49.834
All right, Jesse,

00:59:49.868 --> 00:59:53.267
so our next pairing will be
Judy who's based in Texas,

00:59:53.300 --> 00:59:55.634
and Martin who's based in Illinois.

00:59:55.667 --> 00:59:58.667
This one was a doozy.

00:59:58.701 --> 01:00:01.100
[LAUGHTER]

01:00:01.133 --> 01:00:04.267
Stop what you're doing
and watch this video.

01:00:04.300 --> 01:00:06.167
Please watch
this one, you guys.

01:00:06.200 --> 01:00:08.968
I think it's important not
only because of what happens,

01:00:09.000 --> 01:00:13.000
but because we know folks with
a lot of these perceptions

01:00:13.033 --> 01:00:14.734
and we know the impact
they can have.

01:00:14.767 --> 01:00:18.534
So without further ado,
we give you Martin and Judy.

01:00:29.868 --> 01:00:31.067
Hello.

01:00:31.534 --> 01:00:32.501
Hi.

01:00:32.534 --> 01:00:34.934
How you doing?
Judy, is it?

01:00:34.968 --> 01:00:36.868
I'm Judy.
What is your name?

01:00:36.901 --> 01:00:38.000
I'm Martin.

01:00:38.033 --> 01:00:39.601
A little bit of background
about me.

01:00:39.634 --> 01:00:42.501
I'm married.
I've been married since 2002.

01:00:42.534 --> 01:00:44.901
I have four boys

01:00:44.934 --> 01:00:49.133
and they range in age from
34 all the way down to 8.

01:00:49.167 --> 01:00:50.701
How old are you?

01:00:50.734 --> 01:00:53.334
Now you're getting personal.

01:00:53.367 --> 01:00:54.601
No, I'm 54.

01:00:54.634 --> 01:00:56.634
You don't look like you should
have a 34 year old.

01:00:56.667 --> 01:00:58.501
Oh, no, no.
I'm 54 years old.

01:00:58.534 --> 01:00:59.968
- What?
- I'm a grandfather.

01:01:00.100 --> 01:01:01.534
Yes, the whole nine.

01:01:01.567 --> 01:01:03.567
Oh, that's pretty cool.

01:01:03.601 --> 01:01:05.067
What's your story, Judy?

01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:07.400
I'm 53 and I have
two daughters.

01:01:07.434 --> 01:01:09.000
Well, it's a pleasure
meeting you.

01:01:09.033 --> 01:01:11.000
It's a pleasure meeting you.

01:01:20.367 --> 01:01:21.767
Hmm.

01:01:21.801 --> 01:01:25.100
I didn't like what I just
saw there, but okay.

01:01:31.434 --> 01:01:33.067
That wasn't very nice either,

01:01:33.100 --> 01:01:35.267
them closing the elevator
door on my man.

01:01:35.300 --> 01:01:37.067
That's life in America.

01:01:37.100 --> 01:01:39.367
Because the first thing
you see when you see me

01:01:39.400 --> 01:01:40.934
is a black man.

01:01:40.968 --> 01:01:43.734
You have no idea what my
educational background is,

01:01:43.767 --> 01:01:46.767
what my financial background
is, what my upbringing is.

01:01:46.801 --> 01:01:49.200
So, all you know is that I'm black.

01:01:49.234 --> 01:01:51.000
The first thing you see
when I walk in the door.

01:01:51.033 --> 01:01:53.267
I can honestly say

01:01:53.300 --> 01:01:57.868
the only time I was ever
afraid of being in a car

01:01:57.901 --> 01:02:02.033
and pulling up my window was—

01:02:02.067 --> 01:02:04.434
I don't know what you
call it in Chicago.

01:02:04.467 --> 01:02:07.234
I think Cabrini-Green.

01:02:07.267 --> 01:02:09.234
But it was out of safety.

01:02:09.267 --> 01:02:10.734
Other than that,

01:02:10.767 --> 01:02:13.167
I've never really
been afraid of black people

01:02:13.200 --> 01:02:17.033
and I've always given everyone
the benefit of the doubt.

01:02:17.067 --> 01:02:19.400
But I don't look at things like that.

01:02:19.434 --> 01:02:21.467
I have a lot of black friends.

01:02:21.501 --> 01:02:23.200
You just gotta, I don't know.

01:02:23.234 --> 01:02:24.634
You saw it in the film.

01:02:24.667 --> 01:02:26.167
This guy's a judge.

01:02:26.200 --> 01:02:28.133
He's probably brilliant,
probably has money,

01:02:28.167 --> 01:02:29.901
more money than both of us.

01:02:29.934 --> 01:02:31.567
But yet, they looked at him

01:02:31.601 --> 01:02:33.234
almost like he was
a second-class citizen.

01:02:33.267 --> 01:02:35.567
And that's what I mean,
life in America.

01:02:35.601 --> 01:02:38.234
I think like, just, what hurts me

01:02:38.267 --> 01:02:41.400
is seeing a lot of black people

01:02:41.434 --> 01:02:43.968
hurting their own people.

01:02:44.033 --> 01:02:45.567
Do you see any of that?

01:02:45.601 --> 01:02:47.067
Well, I do see that.

01:02:47.100 --> 01:02:49.067
But always
remember this, Judy.

01:02:49.100 --> 01:02:52.067
People hurt the people
that they live around.

01:02:52.100 --> 01:02:54.133
So I also know from statistics

01:02:54.167 --> 01:02:56.400
that white people hurt and kill

01:02:56.434 --> 01:02:59.167
and fight other white people
in their areas

01:02:59.200 --> 01:03:01.234
because that's
who they're around.

01:03:01.267 --> 01:03:04.100
So, when I hear about
black-on-black crime,

01:03:04.133 --> 01:03:05.567
I get it because I see it.

01:03:05.601 --> 01:03:07.501
I'm right outside of Chicago.

01:03:07.534 --> 01:03:09.801
But I also know if you go
into certain areas

01:03:09.834 --> 01:03:11.901
where there's predominantly
white people,

01:03:11.934 --> 01:03:13.701
they hurt each other, too.

01:03:13.734 --> 01:03:16.234
If you were in a situation where

01:03:16.267 --> 01:03:18.467
someone pulled a gun on you,

01:03:18.501 --> 01:03:21.834
no matter what color,
and you pull your gun out,

01:03:21.868 --> 01:03:25.234
is that trying to protect yourself

01:03:25.267 --> 01:03:28.834
or is that you trying
to kill somebody?

01:03:28.868 --> 01:03:30.534
No, that's self-defense.

01:03:30.567 --> 01:03:31.968
You see, the problem what—

01:03:32.000 --> 01:03:34.400
the reason why we're probably
on this line right now

01:03:34.434 --> 01:03:36.367
is because what you've seen

01:03:36.400 --> 01:03:37.901
and I'm sure you've seen
on the news

01:03:37.934 --> 01:03:39.534
if you've talked
to your black friends

01:03:39.567 --> 01:03:41.467
and they really talk to you,

01:03:41.501 --> 01:03:44.734
then you'll know that there's
black people unarmed

01:03:44.767 --> 01:03:46.734
that are being killed by police.

01:03:46.767 --> 01:03:48.601
Sometimes it's unprovoked.

01:03:48.634 --> 01:03:50.000
Sometimes it's just because

01:03:50.033 --> 01:03:52.767
the police officer
"feared for his life",

01:03:52.801 --> 01:03:55.200
but there was really
nothing to fear.

01:03:55.234 --> 01:03:57.667
You know, because
the person was unarmed.

01:03:57.701 --> 01:04:00.167
Like, if you look at what
happened to George Floyd

01:04:00.200 --> 01:04:03.834
or look at Eric Garner or look
at Jonathan—

01:04:03.868 --> 01:04:05.534
I could go on
a laundry list of

01:04:05.567 --> 01:04:07.100
black people
that were killed

01:04:07.133 --> 01:04:08.601
that were unarmed.

01:04:08.634 --> 01:04:09.934
And I always say,

01:04:09.968 --> 01:04:11.400
"Well then damn,
that could have been me."

01:04:11.434 --> 01:04:13.334
The problem we have
as black people,

01:04:13.367 --> 01:04:15.467
we can't have a bad day
with the police.

01:04:15.501 --> 01:04:17.434
You know, I can do
like you could do.

01:04:17.467 --> 01:04:19.501
You could say, "Hey, get the
hell out of my face, you pig.

01:04:19.534 --> 01:04:21.200
Why'd you pull me over?"

01:04:21.234 --> 01:04:23.067
You could do that
and get away with it.

01:04:23.100 --> 01:04:24.334
I've seen it.

01:04:24.367 --> 01:04:25.667
I can't do that.

01:04:25.701 --> 01:04:27.267
The minute I even say, Judy,

01:04:27.300 --> 01:04:29.267
if I say, "Well, why'd
you pull me over?"

01:04:29.300 --> 01:04:31.400
"You questioning me?
Get out the car."

01:04:31.434 --> 01:04:32.801
I've seen it.

01:04:32.834 --> 01:04:35.434
It's like they're waiting for
me to say the wrong thing

01:04:35.467 --> 01:04:36.801
so that they
could do the wrong

01:04:36.834 --> 01:04:38.167
thing and this thing escalates.

01:04:38.200 --> 01:04:40.868
That's why I always use that
term, de-escalation, Judy,

01:04:40.901 --> 01:04:43.934
because it doesn't have
to get that way.

01:04:43.968 --> 01:04:47.834
What I'm learning is right,
wrong or indifferent;

01:04:47.868 --> 01:04:50.501
no one is respecting
the police force.

01:04:50.534 --> 01:04:52.167
There's some bad ones in

01:04:52.200 --> 01:04:54.300
there, but they're not all bad.

01:04:54.334 --> 01:04:58.968
So, what's kind of scary
for me is going forward,

01:04:59.033 --> 01:05:01.634
who wants
to join the police force?

01:05:01.667 --> 01:05:04.501
They're damned if they do and
they're damned if they don't.

01:05:04.534 --> 01:05:06.968
You know, George Floyd.

01:05:07.000 --> 01:05:09.467
He had a laundry list
of bad crimes.

01:05:09.501 --> 01:05:11.934
Trust me and believe in me,

01:05:11.968 --> 01:05:14.167
that guy should not have done
what he done.

01:05:14.200 --> 01:05:17.434
What I don't like or
what I don't agree with

01:05:17.467 --> 01:05:20.701
if you don't mind me saying
and help me understand this,

01:05:20.734 --> 01:05:23.033
is to use him as a martyr.

01:05:23.067 --> 01:05:26.133
We never said that
George Floyd was our hero.

01:05:26.167 --> 01:05:28.367
We just know
that he died needlessly

01:05:28.400 --> 01:05:31.100
and this death was the catalyst

01:05:31.133 --> 01:05:32.801
that sparked this movement.

01:05:32.834 --> 01:05:34.801
He wasn't combative.

01:05:34.834 --> 01:05:36.434
He was subdued.

01:05:36.467 --> 01:05:39.167
He was already in cuffs and
surrounded by police officers.

01:05:39.200 --> 01:05:40.934
Yeah, and he was on drugs.

01:05:40.968 --> 01:05:43.300
Well, see he could have
been on a lot of drugs,

01:05:43.334 --> 01:05:45.801
but when you saw him
at that point

01:05:45.834 --> 01:05:48.400
where that officer's knee
was on his neck,

01:05:48.434 --> 01:05:50.167
there was no struggling.

01:05:50.200 --> 01:05:51.167
Sure.

01:05:51.200 --> 01:05:53.400
There was no wailing
or hidden fighting.

01:05:53.434 --> 01:05:55.868
He was already in cuffs,
correct, Judy?

01:05:55.901 --> 01:05:56.934
Absolutely.

01:05:56.968 --> 01:05:58.000
Okay.

01:05:58.033 --> 01:06:01.801
And this is why and
I'm not getting on you

01:06:01.834 --> 01:06:03.501
because I hear this all the time.

01:06:03.534 --> 01:06:05.000
Whenever one of us dies

01:06:05.033 --> 01:06:07.601
at the hand of the police
or a vigilante,

01:06:07.634 --> 01:06:09.400
we'll come up with something

01:06:09.434 --> 01:06:11.667
that Trayvon Martin smoked reefer

01:06:11.701 --> 01:06:14.367
or Eric Gardner shouldn't have
been selling loose cigarettes.

01:06:14.400 --> 01:06:17.801
You'll hear every type of way
to justify those deaths.

01:06:17.834 --> 01:06:21.501
And that's why I'm going,
you see my thing to you is,

01:06:21.534 --> 01:06:23.400
even if he was on drugs.

01:06:23.434 --> 01:06:26.133
I think some people
are under the impression

01:06:26.167 --> 01:06:28.067
that we're idolizing
some of these people

01:06:28.100 --> 01:06:30.267
as heroes when we're not.

01:06:30.300 --> 01:06:33.300
We're really more focused
on the incident

01:06:33.334 --> 01:06:35.300
and the injustice
of the incident

01:06:35.334 --> 01:06:38.033
then the person, their
background, how it happened

01:06:38.067 --> 01:06:40.534
versus kind of why
it could have happened

01:06:40.567 --> 01:06:42.367
or what their background was

01:06:42.400 --> 01:06:44.868
because to us
it's two different avenues.

01:06:44.901 --> 01:06:48.601
My question to you is,
what do police officers do?

01:06:48.634 --> 01:06:50.267
And not in this case,

01:06:50.300 --> 01:06:52.934
because obviously this guy
and he's charged with murder

01:06:52.968 --> 01:06:54.501
and that's the
way it should be.

01:06:54.534 --> 01:07:00.868
They are up against
a lot of bad people.

01:07:00.901 --> 01:07:04.067
And now they're afraid
to defend themselves

01:07:04.100 --> 01:07:07.667
because as soon as they do,
they're ridiculed for it.

01:07:07.701 --> 01:07:09.400
All we want them to do

01:07:09.434 --> 01:07:12.601
is give us the same
opportunity to surrender

01:07:12.634 --> 01:07:15.267
and comply that they do
with white people.

01:07:15.300 --> 01:07:17.267
Let me give you
a couple of instances.

01:07:17.300 --> 01:07:20.033
If you remember,
a guy named Dylann Roof

01:07:20.067 --> 01:07:23.100
shot up nine black people
in a church.

01:07:23.133 --> 01:07:25.968
They took him
to Burger King alive.

01:07:26.000 --> 01:07:29.801
The Aurora killer who killed
the people in the theater,

01:07:29.834 --> 01:07:31.968
they took him alive.

01:07:32.000 --> 01:07:34.467
I got a young man
by the name of Tamir Rice,

01:07:34.501 --> 01:07:36.701
12 years old in Ohio.

01:07:36.734 --> 01:07:40.868
They pull up on him within
less than two seconds, Judy,

01:07:40.901 --> 01:07:43.868
that 12-year-old
young man was dead.

01:07:43.901 --> 01:07:45.901
So, my question is,

01:07:45.934 --> 01:07:49.167
well, how did you not give
him a chance to comply?

01:07:49.200 --> 01:07:51.000
"Get on the ground,
throw your weapon down."

01:07:51.033 --> 01:07:52.734
You could have did that
from the PA.

01:07:52.767 --> 01:07:54.968
So, all we want
is the same respect

01:07:55.000 --> 01:07:57.634
and the same type
of compassion

01:07:57.667 --> 01:07:59.734
and a chance to surrender.

01:07:59.767 --> 01:08:01.400
Give me a chance
to at least go,

01:08:01.434 --> 01:08:03.067
"Sir, I'm not doing anything.

01:08:03.100 --> 01:08:05.801
Let me live
and I can plead my case."

01:08:05.834 --> 01:08:09.267
I understand what you're saying.

01:08:09.300 --> 01:08:12.434
I think that the examples
that you're using,

01:08:12.467 --> 01:08:14.467
you're giving me a few.

01:08:14.501 --> 01:08:19.000
There are more examples
of black-on-black crime

01:08:19.033 --> 01:08:20.868
than what you're giving
me right now.

01:08:20.901 --> 01:08:21.934
Wow.

01:08:21.968 --> 01:08:23.200
And it's true.

01:08:23.234 --> 01:08:26.133
Black-on-black crime
is over the top.

01:08:26.167 --> 01:08:29.868
I can give you all type of
examples if you want me to.

01:08:29.901 --> 01:08:34.901
I feel if they can stop doing
that to each other,

01:08:34.934 --> 01:08:37.567
I truly believe things
would be better.

01:08:37.601 --> 01:08:41.000
I wanted to interject because
I've said this to people

01:08:41.033 --> 01:08:43.734
and I want
to get your take on it.

01:08:43.767 --> 01:08:47.667
Black people know that
gang bangers, drug dealers

01:08:47.701 --> 01:08:50.200
and thieves kill and rob people.

01:08:50.234 --> 01:08:53.100
We don't get when police do it.

01:08:53.133 --> 01:08:54.801
You see? That's the difference.

01:08:54.834 --> 01:08:56.501
Like, if you want to talk
about black-on-black crime.

01:08:56.534 --> 01:08:57.868
I got you. It's a problem.

01:08:57.901 --> 01:09:01.968
It's an issue, but I don't
expect a police officer

01:09:02.000 --> 01:09:04.801
to be that gun wielding
and shooting me down

01:09:04.834 --> 01:09:06.467
like the gang banger did.

01:09:06.501 --> 01:09:08.567
Like, I can't win.

01:09:08.601 --> 01:09:10.934
I'm going to be honest with you.

01:09:10.968 --> 01:09:13.968
I didn't see the whole thing.

01:09:14.000 --> 01:09:15.934
It was disturbing enough

01:09:15.968 --> 01:09:18.000
what the first police officer did.

01:09:18.033 --> 01:09:19.567
I'll say this.

01:09:19.601 --> 01:09:21.868
The other police officers
should have said,

01:09:21.901 --> 01:09:25.234
"We have him.
He's under cuff, let's go."

01:09:25.267 --> 01:09:27.767
That's wrong in my mind.

01:09:27.801 --> 01:09:29.234
What they did was wrong.

01:09:29.267 --> 01:09:30.801
Like, they should
have stopped him

01:09:30.834 --> 01:09:36.400
and put him in the court
system to defend himself.

01:09:36.434 --> 01:09:38.634
I believe that.

01:09:38.667 --> 01:09:40.501
But I also believe

01:09:40.534 --> 01:09:45.067
that not every white cop
out there is a bad person

01:09:45.100 --> 01:09:47.767
and only wants
to kill black people.

01:09:47.801 --> 01:09:49.934
Like, I don't believe that.

01:09:49.968 --> 01:09:52.067
I think they try to do their job

01:09:52.100 --> 01:09:55.667
and it's gotten
a lot difficult to do that

01:09:55.701 --> 01:09:57.701
because of these situations.

01:09:57.734 --> 01:09:59.467
So, I understand that,

01:09:59.501 --> 01:10:02.801
but I also know that they need
to step up like you just said.

01:10:02.834 --> 01:10:06.267
If they see a wrong doing,
they need to step up.

01:10:06.300 --> 01:10:10.534
What you're seeing on
the news is the filming of it.

01:10:10.567 --> 01:10:12.801
There's a bunch of black
people getting beat up, killed,

01:10:12.834 --> 01:10:14.901
choked out and abused

01:10:14.934 --> 01:10:17.901
that you will never see
because it wasn't taped.

01:10:17.934 --> 01:10:19.734
But this has been going on
for decades.

01:10:19.767 --> 01:10:22.300
We've been being beaten
and locked up for no reason

01:10:22.334 --> 01:10:23.667
and the whole nine.

01:10:23.701 --> 01:10:26.000
But now we're seeing it
more on tape.

01:10:26.033 --> 01:10:30.601
There's always an epidemic
or a protest or something

01:10:30.634 --> 01:10:32.167
right before an election.

01:10:32.200 --> 01:10:34.000
I think that has something
to do with it.

01:10:34.033 --> 01:10:35.968
Black people are getting
killed all the time.

01:10:36.033 --> 01:10:38.434
It doesn't happen
just in an election year.

01:10:38.467 --> 01:10:40.501
When Sandra Bland
or Trayvon Martin

01:10:40.534 --> 01:10:42.701
or Eric Gardner
or Freddie Gray.

01:10:42.734 --> 01:10:44.033
Like, these things
are happening

01:10:44.067 --> 01:10:45.200
every several months, right?

01:10:45.234 --> 01:10:46.734
You see it on the news.

01:10:46.767 --> 01:10:49.267
So, it has nothing to do
with an election cycle.

01:10:49.300 --> 01:10:53.033
This just happened to happen
at, you know, during this time,

01:10:53.067 --> 01:10:55.434
but what's to stop something
from happening in October,

01:10:55.467 --> 01:10:57.601
right before the November election?

01:10:57.634 --> 01:10:59.400
- Oh, it's going to.
- It makes no sense.

01:10:59.434 --> 01:11:00.934
You know why
it's going to, Judy?

01:11:00.968 --> 01:11:02.601
I absolutely believe it will.

01:11:02.634 --> 01:11:03.901
Because it happens so often,

01:11:03.934 --> 01:11:06.601
we can almost predict it'll be
another video that comes out

01:11:06.634 --> 01:11:08.501
at some point of somebody
getting dragged around

01:11:08.534 --> 01:11:09.734
or beaten around.

01:11:09.767 --> 01:11:11.734
Why now? Like, why?

01:11:11.767 --> 01:11:14.234
Because there's no excuse now.

01:11:14.267 --> 01:11:16.934
But it made
even white people say,

01:11:16.968 --> 01:11:20.901
"He was not resisting arrest.
He was already in handcuffs.

01:11:20.934 --> 01:11:22.634
There were
multiple cops around."

01:11:22.667 --> 01:11:24.534
This was the one that
you couldn't deny.

01:11:24.567 --> 01:11:26.534
Like, you could go,
"Well, Trayvon this.

01:11:26.567 --> 01:11:28.267
Eric Gardner wasn't in cuffs, yet.

01:11:28.300 --> 01:11:31.200
Rodney King wasn't in cuffs,
yet. They were still a threat."

01:11:31.234 --> 01:11:32.767
That's all I hear.

01:11:32.801 --> 01:11:34.934
All I hear from my
counterparts, white people,

01:11:34.968 --> 01:11:36.567
they always can find
a way to say,

01:11:36.601 --> 01:11:38.467
"Well, you know, he bad guy."

01:11:38.501 --> 01:11:41.067
With this one, even though
George Floyd might have had

01:11:41.100 --> 01:11:42.634
a background and history,

01:11:42.667 --> 01:11:45.300
when he was being killed
as you even pointed out,

01:11:45.334 --> 01:11:47.968
he was subdued on his
stomach, in cuffs, surrounded.

01:11:48.000 --> 01:11:49.367
He wasn't going anywhere.

01:11:49.400 --> 01:11:51.467
So, for him to die that way,

01:11:51.501 --> 01:11:54.133
even the majority of everybody
who saw that tape,

01:11:54.167 --> 01:11:56.667
I don't know too many people
that wouldn't agree with me

01:11:56.701 --> 01:11:58.934
because at the end of the day
if you're unarmed

01:11:58.968 --> 01:12:02.100
and you weren't really
a threat, you should be alive.

01:12:02.133 --> 01:12:05.300
So, if they look me up and I'm
driving and they pull me over,

01:12:05.334 --> 01:12:08.400
they're going to find out I
have a concealed carry permit.

01:12:08.434 --> 01:12:09.467
Okay.

01:12:09.501 --> 01:12:11.667
How do you think they should
interact with me at that point?

01:12:11.701 --> 01:12:14.133
Do you have a concealed
weapon on you?

01:12:14.167 --> 01:12:15.467
Yes or no?

01:12:15.501 --> 01:12:17.634
You say, "Yes, sir, I do.
It's right here."

01:12:17.667 --> 01:12:20.133
Okay, you're being honest.
They're being honest.

01:12:20.167 --> 01:12:23.901
Hey, Judy, you remember
Philando Castile?

01:12:23.934 --> 01:12:26.734
Philando Castile was
in another Michigan resident,

01:12:26.767 --> 01:12:28.501
had a concealed carry permit.

01:12:28.534 --> 01:12:30.100
He's not even driving.

01:12:30.133 --> 01:12:31.767
His girlfriend's driving.

01:12:31.801 --> 01:12:34.167
Tells the cop, "I have
a concealed carry permit.

01:12:34.200 --> 01:12:37.234
When I reach inside this glove
compartment, you know,

01:12:37.267 --> 01:12:38.901
you're going to see the weapon,

01:12:38.934 --> 01:12:40.868
but this is where my insurance
and registration is."

01:12:40.901 --> 01:12:42.801
He opens up
the glove compartment,

01:12:42.834 --> 01:12:46.501
cop puts one right into his heart.

01:12:46.534 --> 01:12:48.400
- And he was honest and open.
- There's a bad one.

01:12:48.434 --> 01:12:50.467
See, but that's our thing.

01:12:50.501 --> 01:12:52.400
Like, even when
we're honest and open

01:12:52.434 --> 01:12:56.100
and totally transparent, we catch it.

01:12:56.133 --> 01:12:58.100
Okay, let me ask you a question.

01:12:58.133 --> 01:13:00.467
Who should police the police?

01:13:00.501 --> 01:13:01.801
The citizens,

01:13:01.834 --> 01:13:04.501
the same people that are
putting mayors in their seats,

01:13:04.534 --> 01:13:06.167
chief of police in their seat.

01:13:06.200 --> 01:13:08.901
All you have to do is get what
we call a citizen's panel.

01:13:08.934 --> 01:13:10.634
It doesn't have to be
a ton of people.

01:13:10.667 --> 01:13:12.767
It could be six or seven people
from different backgrounds

01:13:12.801 --> 01:13:15.334
and let them look
at the different scenarios

01:13:15.367 --> 01:13:17.834
and say, "Okay, I think we got
a bad egg here."

01:13:17.868 --> 01:13:20.267
Scenarios, background, education.

01:13:20.300 --> 01:13:23.801
Like, you just want
some ordinary citizen

01:13:23.834 --> 01:13:25.934
to start policing the police?

01:13:25.968 --> 01:13:28.200
Don't those same ordinary citizens

01:13:28.234 --> 01:13:30.067
act as jurors in the courtroom?

01:13:30.100 --> 01:13:32.334
That actually scares me.

01:13:32.367 --> 01:13:34.834
What scares us is a prosecutor

01:13:34.868 --> 01:13:36.634
who works with the police

01:13:36.667 --> 01:13:38.934
every single day
to prosecute crimes,

01:13:38.968 --> 01:13:43.100
now calling himself being
unbiased and neutral

01:13:43.133 --> 01:13:45.400
policing that police officer
that killed my son.

01:13:45.434 --> 01:13:47.567
Because of course
you're not really neutral.

01:13:47.601 --> 01:13:49.400
You're not non-biased.
You work with guy every day.

01:13:49.434 --> 01:13:51.367
You guys do coffee, go bowling.

01:13:51.400 --> 01:13:53.734
How do I know you're going
to give me justice for my kid?

01:13:53.767 --> 01:13:56.200
We gotta do better.
We can do better.

01:13:56.234 --> 01:13:59.167
We just gotta figure out
the system to put in place

01:13:59.200 --> 01:14:01.400
to make and hold people
accountable

01:14:01.434 --> 01:14:03.167
and force them to do better,

01:14:03.200 --> 01:14:05.534
because we can't keep
doing this. We can't.

01:14:05.567 --> 01:14:07.167
I agree.

01:14:07.200 --> 01:14:10.734
What I got out of this
whole conversation

01:14:10.767 --> 01:14:16.767
is two people both believe
things are wrong or right,

01:14:16.801 --> 01:14:18.734
things need to be fixed.

01:14:18.767 --> 01:14:22.300
And I believe we had
a really good conversation.

01:14:22.334 --> 01:14:24.334
And coming up
with a good solution

01:14:24.367 --> 01:14:26.200
from learning from Martin.

01:14:26.234 --> 01:14:28.000
Yeah, and I feel
that this conversation

01:14:28.033 --> 01:14:31.667
was beneficial also
because now I get a chance

01:14:31.701 --> 01:14:33.601
to kind of see
where people are coming

01:14:33.634 --> 01:14:35.467
from outside of my community.

01:14:35.501 --> 01:14:37.133
And I want to thank Judy

01:14:37.167 --> 01:14:38.968
for her time taking part
in this conversation,

01:14:39.000 --> 01:14:41.934
being open and honest with us
because it was great.

01:14:41.968 --> 01:14:43.133
Thank you, Martin.

01:14:43.167 --> 01:14:44.200
I agree.

01:14:44.234 --> 01:14:45.467
It was really good.

01:14:45.501 --> 01:14:47.367
Nice to meet you and hope
to talk to you again.

01:14:47.400 --> 01:14:48.901
Nice to meet you, too.
It was a pleasure.

01:14:48.934 --> 01:14:50.133
- Thank you.
- You, too.

01:14:55.067 --> 01:14:57.400
I don't know where to start
with this one, Freddie.

01:14:57.434 --> 01:15:00.734
I think that there is a whole
lot to unpack here

01:15:00.767 --> 01:15:02.868
and I am, you know,

01:15:02.901 --> 01:15:05.100
grateful for the opportunity.

01:15:05.133 --> 01:15:06.634
Because I think
we checked a lot.

01:15:06.667 --> 01:15:08.567
This conversation
checks a lot of the boxes

01:15:08.601 --> 01:15:11.501
that are... on popular belief

01:15:11.534 --> 01:15:13.968
and on websites
and comment threads.

01:15:14.000 --> 01:15:19.000
So, firstly, I would like
to point out we all know it.

01:15:19.033 --> 01:15:21.200
The go-to distracting move

01:15:21.234 --> 01:15:24.067
when talking about putting its
boot on the neck of citizens

01:15:24.100 --> 01:15:27.734
if those citizens happen to be
black is the ever-distracting,

01:15:27.767 --> 01:15:30.434
what about black-on-black crime?

01:15:30.467 --> 01:15:35.734
I would say that whenever you're on
a topic in a conversation

01:15:35.767 --> 01:15:39.200
and the other people needs
to change the subject,

01:15:39.234 --> 01:15:40.734
they are conceding the point.

01:15:40.767 --> 01:15:41.767
Yes.

01:15:41.801 --> 01:15:44.968
As Martin rightly points out,
crime is proximate.

01:15:45.000 --> 01:15:45.901
Okay?

01:15:45.934 --> 01:15:48.467
People eat, drink, worship,

01:15:48.501 --> 01:15:51.567
often work in proximity
to where they dwell.

01:15:51.601 --> 01:15:54.667
So, there's that 85 percent
of white people are killed

01:15:54.701 --> 01:15:56.400
by the hands
of other white folks.

01:15:56.434 --> 01:15:59.601
There's very little difference
in terms of those stats.

01:15:59.634 --> 01:16:02.334
Again, George Floyd's murder
as they were talking about,

01:16:02.367 --> 01:16:04.601
there was no black-on-black
crime involved.

01:16:04.634 --> 01:16:06.400
You're just as
effective talking

01:16:06.434 --> 01:16:08.334
about how ham sandwiches
are made.

01:16:08.367 --> 01:16:12.100
She also, I want to know what
you think about the default,

01:16:12.133 --> 01:16:15.601
"I have black friends" move.

01:16:15.634 --> 01:16:17.033
Classic, classic.

01:16:17.067 --> 01:16:20.434
What does that mean?

01:16:21.934 --> 01:16:24.501
What does that mean?

01:16:24.534 --> 01:16:25.634
When do we employ that?

01:16:25.667 --> 01:16:27.567
When we're talking
about sexual assault.

01:16:27.601 --> 01:16:30.100
Oh, well my sister is a woman.

01:16:30.133 --> 01:16:32.601
That has nothing to do
with what we're talking about.

01:16:32.634 --> 01:16:35.200
Is there some early elements
of the conversation

01:16:35.234 --> 01:16:36.534
that stood out to you?

01:16:36.567 --> 01:16:37.934
Before we even jump into this,

01:16:37.968 --> 01:16:39.667
I just want to say how
important it is to really see

01:16:39.701 --> 01:16:42.067
some of these conversations
play out in real time.

01:16:42.100 --> 01:16:43.033
Yeah.

01:16:43.067 --> 01:16:44.901
Because you mentioned
echo chambers earlier

01:16:44.934 --> 01:16:46.601
and how we all are
sort of you know

01:16:46.634 --> 01:16:48.801
are inundated with
our own echo chambers.

01:16:48.834 --> 01:16:51.367
And it's all down to like
who we follow on Instagram,

01:16:51.400 --> 01:16:53.868
who we follow on Twitter,
our news sources.

01:16:53.901 --> 01:16:55.200
Like, everything
is pretty much

01:16:55.234 --> 01:16:58.367
like in line with, I think,
our worldviews.

01:16:58.400 --> 01:17:00.601
And so you know when you see
things floating around like,

01:17:00.634 --> 01:17:02.901
"Well, what about
black-on-black crime?"

01:17:02.934 --> 01:17:06.334
And, well, "You know,
he was on drugs

01:17:06.367 --> 01:17:10.200
so that's why it was okay,"
you know, things like that.

01:17:10.234 --> 01:17:11.567
Those are all just kind of

01:17:11.601 --> 01:17:13.200
like whispers
in the air for me

01:17:13.234 --> 01:17:14.968
because I don't surround
myself with people

01:17:15.033 --> 01:17:16.400
who speak that
way or who talk

01:17:16.434 --> 01:17:17.868
about these
topics in that way.

01:17:17.901 --> 01:17:19.601
So, to see it play out in real

01:17:19.634 --> 01:17:21.634
time like this with
Judy and Martin

01:17:21.667 --> 01:17:23.868
was honestly really
enlightening to me.

01:17:23.901 --> 01:17:25.501
It was very triggering,
you know,

01:17:25.534 --> 01:17:27.434
throughout multiple parts
of the conversation,

01:17:27.467 --> 01:17:29.501
but I was like wow,
like these really, you know,

01:17:29.534 --> 01:17:30.934
people really
have these beliefs

01:17:30.968 --> 01:17:32.234
and are passionate
about them.

01:17:32.267 --> 01:17:34.501
It's not only like, "Well, I
don't really know the answer,

01:17:34.534 --> 01:17:36.200
but like this is what I think."

01:17:36.234 --> 01:17:39.100
It's like, no Judy was very,
very confident in her outlook

01:17:39.133 --> 01:17:41.367
and on her view
with these scenarios.

01:17:41.400 --> 01:17:43.367
And so it was just, you know,
it was really interesting

01:17:43.400 --> 01:17:45.267
to also watch Martin

01:17:45.300 --> 01:17:47.400
so gracefully, you know,
give his point of view.

01:17:47.434 --> 01:17:49.467
If I took anything from this
conversation,

01:17:49.501 --> 01:17:52.367
it's just how to be— it was
how to graceful and patient.

01:17:52.400 --> 01:17:56.400
This is a perfect example
of the role that bias can play

01:17:56.434 --> 01:18:00.601
and the connective thread
between a simple bias

01:18:00.634 --> 01:18:04.200
and the loss of life and
violence upon someone else.

01:18:04.234 --> 01:18:06.734
If I am predisposed,
if I have been taught,

01:18:06.767 --> 01:18:08.834
indoctrinated is a better word,

01:18:08.868 --> 01:18:12.634
to believe that black people
are more prone to violence,

01:18:12.667 --> 01:18:14.868
black men are more prone
to violence,

01:18:14.901 --> 01:18:17.701
they're more likely
to be criminals,

01:18:17.734 --> 01:18:19.868
which is to say less worthy

01:18:19.901 --> 01:18:22.100
of the rights which
they are promised

01:18:22.133 --> 01:18:24.334
and pay taxes
for and its woven

01:18:24.367 --> 01:18:26.601
into the fabric of this nation.

01:18:26.634 --> 01:18:29.467
If they're more likely
to be violent,

01:18:29.501 --> 01:18:31.968
then when I see an example
or really not see anything.

01:18:32.000 --> 01:18:34.167
When I hear somebody
on a comment thread

01:18:34.200 --> 01:18:36.801
say they had a criminal history,

01:18:36.834 --> 01:18:38.701
I'm more likely to make
that pathology

01:18:38.734 --> 01:18:40.834
to be violent offenders

01:18:40.868 --> 01:18:43.067
when in the history
of the world

01:18:43.100 --> 01:18:44.734
clearly there is no one close

01:18:44.767 --> 01:18:46.467
to as violent as the white
male or white society.

01:18:46.501 --> 01:18:47.868
It's not even close.

01:18:47.901 --> 01:18:49.400
When you hear a new word,

01:18:49.434 --> 01:18:51.334
you start hearing
that word everywhere.

01:18:51.367 --> 01:18:54.968
So, you're antennas only up
for specific dog whistles,

01:18:55.033 --> 01:18:58.067
specific catch phrases,
specific terms and visuals.

01:18:58.100 --> 01:19:03.534
So, she has this little
mini-box of five clichés

01:19:03.567 --> 01:19:06.567
and three sentences
that just rotate.

01:19:06.601 --> 01:19:08.000
Well black-on-black crime.

01:19:08.033 --> 01:19:09.567
Well, that doesn't apply here.

01:19:09.601 --> 01:19:13.634
Okay, but he had drugs in his
system and he did crime before.

01:19:13.667 --> 01:19:15.167
Okay.

01:19:15.200 --> 01:19:18.133
Give me a list of crimes
that are punishable

01:19:18.167 --> 01:19:20.734
by spontaneous public execution.

01:19:24.100 --> 01:19:25.968
There are none.

01:19:26.033 --> 01:19:27.300
We have system
of law and order.

01:19:27.334 --> 01:19:28.734
You said we should follow it.

01:19:28.767 --> 01:19:30.767
We should let the system
take its course.

01:19:30.801 --> 01:19:33.734
Why aren't we doing
that when their skin is dark?

01:19:33.767 --> 01:19:36.834
When white violence
doesn't count,

01:19:36.868 --> 01:19:39.968
but black violence can
be amplified at any moment,

01:19:40.000 --> 01:19:43.400
when black life never matters

01:19:43.434 --> 01:19:45.501
because its value
can be snatched away

01:19:45.534 --> 01:19:47.367
in a second with a Tweet,

01:19:47.400 --> 01:19:51.667
but white value is perpetual
because its best effort,

01:19:51.701 --> 01:19:53.634
it always has value whether
you're shooting up a school

01:19:53.667 --> 01:19:55.300
or a church,

01:19:55.334 --> 01:19:57.067
you're given
the benefit of the doubt.

01:19:57.100 --> 01:19:58.934
It's a really tough
mountain to climb

01:19:58.968 --> 01:20:01.434
and Martin did
an excellent job trying.

01:20:01.467 --> 01:20:04.701
But I'm glad that we have
this conversation on display

01:20:04.734 --> 01:20:06.968
where you really have to be open

01:20:07.000 --> 01:20:08.934
at the beginning
of a conversation

01:20:08.968 --> 01:20:10.167
to learn something.

01:20:10.200 --> 01:20:11.334
Yeah, absolutely.

01:20:11.367 --> 01:20:14.567
And I think, too, Professor
L'Pree had sort of flagged

01:20:14.601 --> 01:20:16.467
the term "aversive racism"

01:20:16.501 --> 01:20:19.200
which I think is really
evident in this conversation

01:20:19.234 --> 01:20:21.934
with Judy and Martin
which is aversive racism

01:20:21.968 --> 01:20:24.033
is the lack of contact
with a group

01:20:24.067 --> 01:20:25.801
creates stereotypes, right?

01:20:25.834 --> 01:20:28.033
And so, I think it's pretty
clear that like, you know,

01:20:28.067 --> 01:20:31.234
Judy says that
she has black friends, right.

01:20:31.267 --> 01:20:35.133
That does not mean
that she has made a habit

01:20:35.167 --> 01:20:39.801
of surrounding herself with
people who are unlike her own

01:20:39.834 --> 01:20:42.734
in order to learn
and sort of break down

01:20:42.767 --> 01:20:45.000
some of those stereotypes
throughout life.

01:20:45.033 --> 01:20:47.300
So, you know she was saying
that after &lt;i&gt;The Look&lt;/i&gt;,

01:20:47.334 --> 01:20:49.067
after they watched &lt;i&gt;The Look&lt;/i&gt;

01:20:49.100 --> 01:20:51.334
she was saying, "You know,
I've never rolled up my windows

01:20:51.367 --> 01:20:54.000
or locked my door except for
when I was in Cabrini-Green."

01:20:54.033 --> 01:20:54.901
Safety.

01:20:54.934 --> 01:20:56.834
Yeah, for safety, right.

01:20:56.868 --> 01:21:00.934
And I think, too,
white people really like to,

01:21:00.968 --> 01:21:02.701
instead of saying
black neighborhoods,

01:21:02.734 --> 01:21:04.667
they'll say, high-crime areas

01:21:04.701 --> 01:21:06.801
which is just code
for black neighborhoods

01:21:06.834 --> 01:21:08.300
because of redlining

01:21:08.334 --> 01:21:09.968
and because of the history
of this country

01:21:10.033 --> 01:21:12.400
in really segregating
these communities

01:21:12.434 --> 01:21:16.133
in ways that black people
specifically cannot control.

01:21:16.167 --> 01:21:19.200
So I think that was something
that really stood out to me.

01:21:19.234 --> 01:21:20.834
There was just so many points.

01:21:20.868 --> 01:21:22.701
You know, I think that we could
talk about this conversation,

01:21:22.734 --> 01:21:24.300
you know, for hours

01:21:24.334 --> 01:21:26.167
because there's just so much
to unpack here.

01:21:26.200 --> 01:21:27.667
But I think Martin did a really

01:21:27.701 --> 01:21:29.067
good job of drawing parallels

01:21:29.100 --> 01:21:32.200
to white criminals
and how they're treated

01:21:32.234 --> 01:21:34.701
versus how black suspects
are treated.

01:21:34.734 --> 01:21:37.667
And I think one of the quotes
that stood out to me the most

01:21:37.701 --> 01:21:40.267
was, "Give us a chance
to surrender",

01:21:40.300 --> 01:21:42.367
like they did Dylann Roof.

01:21:42.400 --> 01:21:44.334
I want to just try
to put it concisely

01:21:44.367 --> 01:21:46.234
what Judy is a
real example of...

01:21:46.267 --> 01:21:49.434
is how your bias can come
to any case

01:21:49.467 --> 01:21:52.334
and remix and repurpose
the entire experience.

01:21:52.367 --> 01:21:55.434
If my bias for an unarmed,
handcuffed man on the ground

01:21:55.467 --> 01:21:57.467
is, "Well, he was violent."

01:21:57.501 --> 01:21:59.234
Yeah, but not this time.

01:21:59.267 --> 01:22:00.734
"Well, he had a history."

01:22:00.767 --> 01:22:02.300
Yeah, but that wasn't
part of this.

01:22:02.334 --> 01:22:04.000
"Yeah, but he could have."

01:22:04.033 --> 01:22:07.634
If you're lobbing,
we weaponize our bias.

01:22:07.667 --> 01:22:10.000
It gives a pre-checklist instead

01:22:10.033 --> 01:22:11.868
of actually being present

01:22:11.901 --> 01:22:14.267
and letting that person be
a human being.

01:22:14.300 --> 01:22:17.234
It really is worthy of examination.

01:22:17.267 --> 01:22:20.667
And we have folks just
like her in our family,

01:22:20.701 --> 01:22:22.901
a lot of us do.

01:22:22.934 --> 01:22:25.968
And that is where there's
a real opportunity

01:22:26.000 --> 01:22:30.067
to break some new ground here
because what are you without,

01:22:30.100 --> 01:22:34.667
as Toni Morrison said, "What
are you without your racism?

01:22:34.701 --> 01:22:36.767
Are you any good?

01:22:36.801 --> 01:22:38.234
Is there a reason
you're hiding from it?

01:22:38.267 --> 01:22:39.767
What are you without it?

01:22:39.801 --> 01:22:42.467
What if we can actually just
talk about these individuals?"

01:22:42.501 --> 01:22:44.934
That's the challenge.

01:22:51.033 --> 01:22:53.434
So, for our next pairing,
we're going to be joining Eric

01:22:53.467 --> 01:22:55.334
who's based in Florida.

01:22:55.367 --> 01:22:57.467
And Danielle
who's based in Illinois.

01:22:57.501 --> 01:22:59.901
And you guys, I was really
nervous going into this one,

01:22:59.968 --> 01:23:01.734
so tune in.

01:23:09.767 --> 01:23:10.634
Hi, Eric.

01:23:10.667 --> 01:23:11.968
Hi. Hi, Danielle.

01:23:12.033 --> 01:23:13.400
I just saw your name
pop up there.

01:23:13.434 --> 01:23:14.634
How are you?

01:23:14.667 --> 01:23:15.767
I'm great. How are you?

01:23:15.801 --> 01:23:17.501
I am well.

01:23:17.534 --> 01:23:18.801
So, what do you do for work?

01:23:18.834 --> 01:23:20.567
I am in education.

01:23:20.601 --> 01:23:25.868
So, I teach at a law school
and I'm a lawyer by trade.

01:23:25.901 --> 01:23:28.000
Oh, so I gotta watch
what I say.

01:23:29.267 --> 01:23:31.534
- No, that's awesome.
- No. Please don't.

01:23:31.567 --> 01:23:33.033
That's awesome.

01:23:33.067 --> 01:23:35.734
I've been a cop for 20 years.

01:23:35.767 --> 01:23:36.667
Okay.

01:23:36.701 --> 01:23:38.667
So, my least favorite place
is the courthouse.

01:23:38.701 --> 01:23:41.267
Don't feel intimidated
by the space.

01:23:58.133 --> 01:24:00.267
Oh, please let him
be the judge.

01:24:07.868 --> 01:24:09.133
[chuckles]

01:24:24.234 --> 01:24:25.567
Now, I love it.

01:24:25.601 --> 01:24:27.501
That gave me chills.
I like that.

01:24:27.534 --> 01:24:30.467
I was taken by this black man

01:24:30.501 --> 01:24:33.467
kind of going
about his everyday life

01:24:33.501 --> 01:24:38.634
and experiencing what
I would call micro aggressions

01:24:38.667 --> 01:24:41.100
just from the look.

01:24:41.133 --> 01:24:44.801
And people automatically
having certain opinions

01:24:44.834 --> 01:24:47.767
or thoughts
about that individual.

01:24:47.801 --> 01:24:51.934
And, you know, changing
their demeanor

01:24:51.968 --> 01:24:56.934
because of just seeing him as
a black man or black person.

01:24:56.968 --> 01:24:59.133
You see that happen.

01:24:59.167 --> 01:25:02.200
You know, people who drive
through neighborhoods

01:25:02.234 --> 01:25:03.968
and lock the doors.

01:25:04.033 --> 01:25:06.801
If you lock them because
it's a high crime area, okay.

01:25:06.834 --> 01:25:10.167
If you lock them because you
see somebody just walking,

01:25:10.200 --> 01:25:11.534
totally different.

01:25:11.567 --> 01:25:15.067
You know one thing
that troubles me, Eric,

01:25:15.100 --> 01:25:17.334
sometimes is seeing
the different ways

01:25:17.367 --> 01:25:20.267
in which officers have
handled black offender,

01:25:20.300 --> 01:25:21.601
I guess I'll call it an offender.

01:25:21.634 --> 01:25:24.067
A black offender
versus a white offender

01:25:24.100 --> 01:25:27.033
and how white offenders can,

01:25:27.067 --> 01:25:30.000
you know, honestly cut
the complete fool

01:25:30.033 --> 01:25:34.434
and still be arrested
where, you know,

01:25:34.467 --> 01:25:37.701
black folks just don't have
that kind of experience.

01:25:37.734 --> 01:25:41.934
And they end up harmed often
and sometimes dead.

01:25:41.968 --> 01:25:44.100
It's not acceptable what the

01:25:44.133 --> 01:25:46.534
officers historically have done,

01:25:46.567 --> 01:25:50.467
but I also, looking at the
sheer number of encounters

01:25:50.501 --> 01:25:53.200
you know that hey,
not all of them are bad.

01:25:53.234 --> 01:25:57.601
Eric, what is it like to be
a police officer right now?

01:25:57.634 --> 01:25:59.367
It's horrible.

01:25:59.400 --> 01:26:01.501
Because we get judged

01:26:01.534 --> 01:26:03.234
and this is going
to sound very familiar,

01:26:03.267 --> 01:26:05.200
by what others have done.

01:26:05.234 --> 01:26:07.367
I understand as a police officer

01:26:07.400 --> 01:26:10.000
this is kind of, you know,
this is unfortunate.

01:26:10.033 --> 01:26:12.300
You know, one of the things
I would say

01:26:12.334 --> 01:26:14.701
about the George Floyd situation

01:26:14.734 --> 01:26:18.567
is there were officers that
were surrounding him, right?

01:26:18.601 --> 01:26:22.801
And none of those officers
did anything.

01:26:22.834 --> 01:26:25.334
And I'm not saying that
every one police is like that

01:26:25.367 --> 01:26:27.167
but what I am saying is

01:26:27.200 --> 01:26:30.601
it seems like when officers
see something wrong,

01:26:30.634 --> 01:26:33.167
there is this kind
of code of silence.

01:26:33.200 --> 01:26:35.801
Like, you know, I'm not going
to do anything about it.

01:26:35.834 --> 01:26:38.801
And I think that also impacts

01:26:38.834 --> 01:26:41.968
the perception of police officers.

01:26:42.033 --> 01:26:43.601
If more officers said,

01:26:43.634 --> 01:26:46.601
"We will not stand for this.
This is not acceptable.

01:26:46.634 --> 01:26:50.267
We do not want
to perpetuate discrimination.

01:26:50.300 --> 01:26:53.467
We don't want
to perpetuate racism.

01:26:53.501 --> 01:26:55.667
That is not who we are
and what we are about

01:26:55.701 --> 01:26:59.367
as opposed to kind of holding
on to that themselves."

01:26:59.400 --> 01:27:01.868
But not stepping up
and, you know,

01:27:01.901 --> 01:27:06.467
and breaking that code of silence.

01:27:06.501 --> 01:27:07.701
Oh, I agree.

01:27:07.734 --> 01:27:09.501
I think some of the things

01:27:09.534 --> 01:27:11.701
that stop officers
from saying something

01:27:11.734 --> 01:27:13.934
is that if the person
they observe doing it

01:27:13.968 --> 01:27:15.767
is a senior officer,

01:27:15.801 --> 01:27:19.067
they think well I'm a junior
officer, what do I know?

01:27:19.100 --> 01:27:21.234
Or if they're of higher rank,

01:27:21.267 --> 01:27:23.234
well, one day I want
to be to their rank

01:27:23.267 --> 01:27:25.133
so I want them
on my good side.

01:27:25.167 --> 01:27:27.801
When these type
of horrific events

01:27:27.834 --> 01:27:30.501
go on in other parts
of the country,

01:27:30.534 --> 01:27:33.901
we talk about it because
the best thing we can do

01:27:33.934 --> 01:27:36.100
is to not have
it happen to us.

01:27:36.133 --> 01:27:39.567
But it's to talk about,
well, why did this happen?

01:27:39.601 --> 01:27:42.634
How can we keep
from this happening here?

01:27:42.667 --> 01:27:48.801
So, regarding the incidents
around George Floyd,

01:27:48.834 --> 01:27:54.167
I haven't seen enough to say,
of the entire video to say

01:27:54.200 --> 01:28:00.200
that officer did that to that
man because he was black.

01:28:00.234 --> 01:28:05.934
What I've seen was an officer
who had a subdued subject

01:28:05.968 --> 01:28:08.801
who was not flailing around
on the ground,

01:28:08.834 --> 01:28:11.567
keep his knee
and obstruct his airway

01:28:11.601 --> 01:28:13.868
from what I observed

01:28:13.901 --> 01:28:16.234
and there was no need for that.

01:28:16.267 --> 01:28:23.834
I hear you and understand the
perspective of the department,

01:28:23.868 --> 01:28:26.634
but I would just—
I really challenge that

01:28:26.667 --> 01:28:31.634
because I think that, you
know, we're talking about bias.

01:28:31.667 --> 01:28:35.467
And people have biases and
police officers have biases.

01:28:35.501 --> 01:28:39.567
I just find it hard to believe

01:28:39.601 --> 01:28:43.868
that had he been a white man

01:28:43.901 --> 01:28:48.467
or even a white and certainly
a white woman,

01:28:48.501 --> 01:28:52.801
the officer directly involved
with the knee on his neck

01:28:52.834 --> 01:28:57.501
as well as the officers
that were surrounding,

01:28:57.534 --> 01:28:59.200
that were standing
there looking on

01:28:59.234 --> 01:29:00.601
would have handled it that way.

01:29:00.634 --> 01:29:02.901
Can you imagine that officer

01:29:02.934 --> 01:29:07.133
having his knee
on a white woman's neck

01:29:07.167 --> 01:29:09.267
like that for 10 minutes?

01:29:09.300 --> 01:29:13.734
That would not have
happened, right?

01:29:13.767 --> 01:29:15.434
I'm going to tell you,

01:29:15.467 --> 01:29:18.067
I agree that do I think
that I would see it

01:29:18.100 --> 01:29:20.534
as a white lady
and the officer knee?

01:29:20.567 --> 01:29:23.033
No. I agree with you.

01:29:23.067 --> 01:29:26.934
But you look at the Orlando
police sergeant.

01:29:26.968 --> 01:29:29.400
She was a black female.

01:29:29.434 --> 01:29:31.934
She got murdered
by a black suspect,

01:29:31.968 --> 01:29:35.234
but there was no Black Lives
Matter movement for that

01:29:35.267 --> 01:29:37.267
and that's what frustrates
me is you don't see

01:29:37.300 --> 01:29:41.634
the outcry of police officers
getting shot and killed.

01:29:41.667 --> 01:29:43.667
And sometimes I scratch
my head and I wonder.

01:29:43.701 --> 01:29:46.200
I'm like why is that?

01:29:46.234 --> 01:29:49.434
People have kind of
misunderstood

01:29:49.467 --> 01:29:54.434
that Black Lives Matter was
an affirmation directly related

01:29:54.467 --> 01:29:57.567
to black people being killed
by the police.

01:29:57.601 --> 01:30:00.400
If you are an officer and that
is your line of work,

01:30:00.434 --> 01:30:02.000
there's a potential

01:30:02.033 --> 01:30:03.834
that someone
you know may harm you,

01:30:03.868 --> 01:30:06.534
may kill you whether they're
black, white of what have you.

01:30:06.567 --> 01:30:09.868
But the expectation
in terms of on the flipside,

01:30:09.901 --> 01:30:11.534
if you are a citizen

01:30:11.567 --> 01:30:14.100
is that you will not be killed
by the police.

01:30:14.133 --> 01:30:15.334
You know, like you said.

01:30:15.367 --> 01:30:16.601
We sign up.

01:30:16.634 --> 01:30:18.067
We put on a badge, a gun

01:30:18.100 --> 01:30:20.367
and we go to the work with
the expectation to come home,

01:30:20.400 --> 01:30:22.367
but the reality is you may not.

01:30:22.400 --> 01:30:25.734
And a large part of me
feels extremely sad

01:30:25.767 --> 01:30:28.234
that the Black Lives Matter
movement

01:30:28.267 --> 01:30:32.234
has got intertwined with
rioters because that's not it.

01:30:32.267 --> 01:30:33.467
There's a big difference

01:30:33.501 --> 01:30:35.033
between protesting
and rioting.

01:30:35.067 --> 01:30:38.400
You know, black folks are
an oppressed community.

01:30:38.434 --> 01:30:40.300
And we've been oppressed

01:30:40.334 --> 01:30:43.067
for the 400 years
that we've been here.

01:30:43.100 --> 01:30:47.734
And, you know, slavery,
all kinds of policies

01:30:47.767 --> 01:30:50.667
that negatively affected our lives

01:30:50.701 --> 01:30:54.801
and our abilities
to live the American dream.

01:30:54.834 --> 01:30:56.801
People have
to understand the history

01:30:56.834 --> 01:31:01.334
to really understand why those
feelings are so deep seated.

01:31:01.367 --> 01:31:04.801
When I think about, you know,
what happened in Tulsa

01:31:04.834 --> 01:31:07.534
and what happened
in Rosewood where, you know,

01:31:07.567 --> 01:31:10.467
black people who were
sustaining their communities

01:31:10.501 --> 01:31:12.767
and were thriving economically

01:31:12.801 --> 01:31:16.734
were bombed and slaughtered
by white people.

01:31:16.767 --> 01:31:20.000
We have to keep in mind
all of these experiences

01:31:20.033 --> 01:31:23.133
so when you're looking
at these people out there

01:31:23.167 --> 01:31:27.834
who are protesting peacefully,
who are rioting,

01:31:27.868 --> 01:31:30.000
who are looting,

01:31:30.033 --> 01:31:32.501
I think that it's all a combination

01:31:32.534 --> 01:31:35.033
of just that frustration

01:31:35.067 --> 01:31:37.100
however it went down,

01:31:37.133 --> 01:31:40.868
it's gotten the attention
that its needed to get.

01:31:40.901 --> 01:31:46.567
So, I know that I'm anti-looting,

01:31:46.601 --> 01:31:48.901
but I'm pro-protesting.

01:31:48.934 --> 01:31:50.567
Okay, so why loot?

01:31:50.601 --> 01:31:53.734
And I said, okay, because
I've heard people say,

01:31:53.767 --> 01:31:55.767
"We'll they're stealing,"

01:31:55.801 --> 01:31:57.501
the looters are stealing

01:31:57.534 --> 01:31:59.767
from the communities
which they live in.

01:31:59.801 --> 01:32:02.601
And then I saw something
else that said,

01:32:02.634 --> 01:32:05.868
"Property can be replaced,
lives can't."

01:32:05.901 --> 01:32:07.667
So, then that got me thinking.

01:32:07.701 --> 01:32:12.901
So, if I'm a looter, other
than just personal greed,

01:32:12.934 --> 01:32:14.934
I'm stealing from a store.

01:32:14.968 --> 01:32:18.467
The store typically has insurance.

01:32:18.501 --> 01:32:21.667
So, the store will be made whole,

01:32:21.701 --> 01:32:25.100
but I'm getting my media
attention like you said.

01:32:25.133 --> 01:32:26.767
I'm getting, you know,

01:32:26.801 --> 01:32:30.133
I'm getting noticed
whether it's good or bad

01:32:30.167 --> 01:32:31.934
I'm getting noticed.

01:32:31.968 --> 01:32:34.067
As much as I don't
agree with it,

01:32:34.100 --> 01:32:36.534
the insurance
companies pay out.

01:32:36.567 --> 01:32:39.434
Well, if you're paying out more
money than you're bringing in,

01:32:39.467 --> 01:32:41.267
that's a problem for business.

01:32:41.300 --> 01:32:45.968
That's a problem for the COOs,
the CEOs and who runs them?

01:32:46.033 --> 01:32:48.200
Typically powerful white men.

01:32:48.234 --> 01:32:49.701
How do they get in business?

01:32:49.734 --> 01:32:50.868
Who do they know?

01:32:50.901 --> 01:32:52.567
Typically politicians.

01:32:52.601 --> 01:32:58.400
So, do I try to look at as
that, that a looter is saying,

01:32:58.434 --> 01:33:00.534
"This is the only way

01:33:00.567 --> 01:33:03.701
I can get the powerful
person's attention,

01:33:03.734 --> 01:33:06.033
white, black, Hispanic,
typically white,

01:33:06.067 --> 01:33:08.033
if you look at the makeup
of congress,

01:33:08.067 --> 01:33:09.567
this is how I get their attention."

01:33:09.601 --> 01:33:11.734
Do I agree with it?
Absolutely not.

01:33:11.767 --> 01:33:14.267
Have I tried to
understand it? Yes.

01:33:14.300 --> 01:33:16.300
That's great that you
have tried to understand

01:33:16.334 --> 01:33:18.200
and just broaden
your perspective on it.

01:33:18.234 --> 01:33:23.367
The recourse that people have
to change unjust systems

01:33:23.400 --> 01:33:30.033
is protesting, advocating,
doing policy work

01:33:30.067 --> 01:33:31.968
and trying to get
to the root causes

01:33:32.000 --> 01:33:33.567
of some of these issues,

01:33:33.601 --> 01:33:35.567
so people are in the streets.

01:33:35.601 --> 01:33:36.901
They're angry.

01:33:36.934 --> 01:33:40.133
They're rioting because
they're trying to get awareness

01:33:40.167 --> 01:33:42.100
and attention to this issue

01:33:42.133 --> 01:33:45.133
because for so long they've gone

01:33:45.167 --> 01:33:47.601
through these different
kind of measures of change

01:33:47.634 --> 01:33:51.167
and recourse without action
really being taken

01:33:51.200 --> 01:33:55.300
or without action taken to the
extent that it needs to be done.

01:33:55.334 --> 01:33:58.434
To tell people
how they should protest

01:33:58.467 --> 01:34:03.434
and what they should do
and what's appropriate

01:34:03.467 --> 01:34:06.801
and what's not appropriate
I think is out of line.

01:34:06.834 --> 01:34:12.767
This country was founded
by people who protested.

01:34:12.801 --> 01:34:16.200
You know, the Boston Tea Party
and all of these things,

01:34:16.234 --> 01:34:18.434
they looted and tore
things up and so on,

01:34:18.467 --> 01:34:21.868
you know the feeling of desperation,

01:34:21.901 --> 01:34:24.901
the feeling of not having
is what pours

01:34:24.934 --> 01:34:27.067
into the looting part of it

01:34:27.100 --> 01:34:29.300
or the rioting part of it.

01:34:29.334 --> 01:34:31.501
One of the things I'd love to see

01:34:31.534 --> 01:34:37.000
is not just, hey we're here
and we want change,

01:34:37.033 --> 01:34:40.367
but what is acceptable.

01:34:40.400 --> 01:34:43.367
Now the new thing is
the disbanding and defunding

01:34:43.400 --> 01:34:46.367
of police departments
is the pushing.

01:34:48.667 --> 01:34:50.801
What does a real reform
look like?

01:34:50.834 --> 01:34:53.534
What does defunding a police
department look like?

01:34:53.567 --> 01:34:56.400
The core that is about money.

01:34:56.434 --> 01:34:59.601
Since we're paying them,
we want accountability.

01:34:59.634 --> 01:35:02.601
If money from policing
is diverted to education,

01:35:02.634 --> 01:35:06.300
you know, wouldn't that
be helpful for the police?

01:35:06.334 --> 01:35:08.434
I like that because
it makes me go,

01:35:08.467 --> 01:35:11.400
okay so it's maybe not a
hundred percent a bad thing.

01:35:11.434 --> 01:35:15.968
What can I do to say this is
a step in the right direction?

01:35:16.000 --> 01:35:18.000
One thing is just kind of

01:35:18.033 --> 01:35:20.467
being educated on these issues.

01:35:20.501 --> 01:35:23.234
You know, there's all kinds
of ways to address,

01:35:23.267 --> 01:35:26.734
to learn about bias
because we all have them.

01:35:26.767 --> 01:35:29.534
And there are not more officers

01:35:29.567 --> 01:35:34.400
that are stepping up to say no.

01:35:34.434 --> 01:35:36.267
This is not how these

01:35:36.300 --> 01:35:39.000
situations should be addressed.

01:35:39.033 --> 01:35:40.667
And this is, you know,

01:35:40.701 --> 01:35:44.767
this is a result of, you know,
a bias or whatever it is,

01:35:44.801 --> 01:35:47.934
but officers taking
on this issue.

01:35:47.968 --> 01:35:50.968
I agree that you have got

01:35:51.000 --> 01:35:54.200
to have a sense of this
is the job you want to do.

01:35:54.234 --> 01:35:56.534
Don't let anybody call
anything into question.

01:35:56.567 --> 01:35:58.801
Do the right thing
all the time.

01:35:58.834 --> 01:36:01.667
Thanks, Eric, for this opportunity

01:36:01.701 --> 01:36:05.167
and joining in this very
important conversation

01:36:05.200 --> 01:36:07.200
that needs to be had.

01:36:07.234 --> 01:36:08.601
We need that.

01:36:08.634 --> 01:36:11.767
We are all dependent on
each other for, you know,

01:36:11.801 --> 01:36:14.334
having some actionable
things take place.

01:36:14.367 --> 01:36:16.133
We know that
there is a process

01:36:16.167 --> 01:36:17.667
that needs to get started.

01:36:17.701 --> 01:36:22.234
But what I can tell you is
I certainly appreciate you.

01:36:22.267 --> 01:36:24.901
You represent the
City of Chicago very well

01:36:24.934 --> 01:36:28.567
so they should be very proud
because I am a better person

01:36:28.601 --> 01:36:31.667
for spending the last couple
of hours with you.

01:36:31.701 --> 01:36:33.534
Thank you, same here.

01:36:48.601 --> 01:36:52.467
So, one of the first things
that jumped out at me

01:36:52.501 --> 01:36:54.534
in this conversation
just at the outset,

01:36:54.567 --> 01:36:56.767
I just want to have
a through line

01:36:56.801 --> 01:37:00.067
that several of these other
conversations we have seen

01:37:00.100 --> 01:37:04.501
this through line of one
of the participants reacting

01:37:04.534 --> 01:37:06.834
to &lt;i&gt;The Look &lt;/i&gt;video

01:37:06.868 --> 01:37:09.033
by mentioning
whether they like it or not,

01:37:09.067 --> 01:37:10.934
whether they agree
with it or not.

01:37:10.968 --> 01:37:14.234
And man, Eric really took me
on a journey there sometimes

01:37:14.267 --> 01:37:18.501
where my assumption based
on what he does for a living

01:37:18.534 --> 01:37:20.133
as a Deputy Sheriff

01:37:20.167 --> 01:37:22.133
or maybe just the direction
of his language sounded

01:37:22.167 --> 01:37:24.067
like he was going
to land somewhere

01:37:24.100 --> 01:37:25.501
but he kind of took us
on a ride

01:37:25.534 --> 01:37:27.934
and actually had been
quite thoughtful about,

01:37:27.968 --> 01:37:29.734
he kind of took us through
behind the curtain

01:37:29.767 --> 01:37:33.000
as to how he processed things
and weighed pros and cons

01:37:33.033 --> 01:37:35.868
in an interesting way that was
a little more fleshed out

01:37:35.901 --> 01:37:37.901
than I think folks often do.

01:37:37.934 --> 01:37:39.567
So, I actually appreciated

01:37:39.601 --> 01:37:42.467
that a couple of times
in the conversation

01:37:42.501 --> 01:37:45.801
around has he broke down
the rationale

01:37:45.834 --> 01:37:50.167
or end result of so-called looting.

01:37:50.200 --> 01:37:51.300
Yeah, yeah.

01:37:51.334 --> 01:37:53.234
I think, too, something that
jumped out

01:37:53.267 --> 01:37:55.534
from jump in this video

01:37:55.567 --> 01:37:58.367
was that as soon
as Danielle told him

01:37:58.400 --> 01:38:00.300
and revealed
that she's a lawyer,

01:38:00.334 --> 01:38:03.267
you could sort of see
Eric's reaction to that.

01:38:03.300 --> 01:38:04.834
And I think that, you know,

01:38:04.868 --> 01:38:06.834
I think he was a little
bit intimidated

01:38:06.868 --> 01:38:08.067
By the fact that she's a lawyer

01:38:08.100 --> 01:38:11.133
and they would be engaging
in this conversation about bias.

01:38:11.167 --> 01:38:12.634
And so, I do wonder

01:38:12.667 --> 01:38:14.567
and if she had
a different occupation

01:38:14.601 --> 01:38:16.334
or if he a little bit
more comfortable

01:38:16.367 --> 01:38:19.567
and less like feel like
he needed to stay on his toes,

01:38:19.601 --> 01:38:22.033
would he have spoken to her

01:38:22.067 --> 01:38:24.434
or spoken about this topic
in the same way?

01:38:24.467 --> 01:38:27.267
Eric made a distinction
between pro-protesting,

01:38:27.300 --> 01:38:30.801
"I'm against looting", but
understanding that we can't—

01:38:30.834 --> 01:38:34.567
when we talk about policing
the major through line

01:38:34.601 --> 01:38:37.100
is never the worst among
them as the headline.

01:38:37.133 --> 01:38:40.467
Protesting has very little
to do with white agitation

01:38:40.501 --> 01:38:42.100
and so-called
anarchists people

01:38:42.133 --> 01:38:44.133
bussing themselves into protest

01:38:44.167 --> 01:38:46.934
to obviously be destroying property

01:38:46.968 --> 01:38:50.701
and instigating property damage.

01:38:50.734 --> 01:38:52.200
But it detracts.

01:38:52.234 --> 01:38:53.434
It's easily,

01:38:53.467 --> 01:38:54.934
if you're not interested
in the subject matter,

01:38:54.968 --> 01:38:56.434
detracts from the story.

01:38:56.467 --> 01:38:58.734
It has to be addressed
in a dominant fashion.

01:38:58.767 --> 01:39:00.767
It eats up time and energy
instead of talking

01:39:00.801 --> 01:39:03.868
about the real frustration.

01:39:03.901 --> 01:39:09.067
But he did land on an interesting
point about looting

01:39:09.100 --> 01:39:12.133
actually being the only way to
get some people's attention.

01:39:12.167 --> 01:39:13.634
Yeah, which was great.

01:39:13.667 --> 01:39:15.267
It seemed like he
had really thought it through

01:39:15.300 --> 01:39:17.634
versus just saying,
like, you know,

01:39:17.667 --> 01:39:20.767
"These folks are taking
advantage of the situation.

01:39:20.801 --> 01:39:22.601
They're stealing.
They're doing X, Y and Z."

01:39:22.634 --> 01:39:26.601
Which is the way to deflect
from the actual issue at hand

01:39:26.634 --> 01:39:28.501
and I think that it's important

01:39:28.534 --> 01:39:31.601
that we keep calling
that out, these deflections

01:39:31.634 --> 01:39:34.133
that keep coming up
in these conversations.

01:39:34.167 --> 01:39:36.634
And when we are in a time
which we are now

01:39:36.667 --> 01:39:38.767
of really having
a public conversation

01:39:38.801 --> 01:39:41.567
about transformational change
around policing,

01:39:41.601 --> 01:39:43.467
we're talking about
defunding the police.

01:39:43.501 --> 01:39:44.701
People are trying to figure out

01:39:44.734 --> 01:39:46.834
what exactly that does
and does not mean.

01:39:46.868 --> 01:39:49.534
In order to really be present for

01:39:49.567 --> 01:39:51.634
and active in that conversation,

01:39:51.667 --> 01:39:53.901
you do need to understand
some of the context

01:39:53.934 --> 01:39:55.534
that they're laying out.

01:39:55.567 --> 01:39:57.434
So, that's a step towards saying,

01:39:57.467 --> 01:39:59.834
okay, if this is not working in

01:39:59.868 --> 01:40:02.300
the way it claims to be working,

01:40:02.334 --> 01:40:04.300
if it was we wouldn't have
to talk about it.

01:40:04.334 --> 01:40:06.834
If people felt both
protected and served,

01:40:06.868 --> 01:40:08.467
we'd be on to
a different topic.

01:40:08.501 --> 01:40:09.934
We defund things all the time.

01:40:09.968 --> 01:40:11.534
We defund education,
we defund health care,

01:40:11.567 --> 01:40:13.133
we defund public assistance.

01:40:13.167 --> 01:40:15.667
We reduce funding in
those departments.

01:40:15.701 --> 01:40:20.534
And I felt like Eric really
started in a good place

01:40:20.567 --> 01:40:21.934
and that he was open

01:40:21.968 --> 01:40:24.901
and would be open
to kind of understanding

01:40:24.934 --> 01:40:27.501
that we actually have a lot
of these mechanisms in place

01:40:27.534 --> 01:40:29.567
and as a police officer
I think he embodies

01:40:29.601 --> 01:40:33.400
what a lot of deputy sheriffs
or police officers,

01:40:33.434 --> 01:40:35.801
however you define law
enforcement in that case,

01:40:35.834 --> 01:40:37.968
they know they're not
the ones to call for.

01:40:38.033 --> 01:40:39.234
They're exhausted.

01:40:39.267 --> 01:40:40.434
They know
they're not the ones

01:40:40.467 --> 01:40:41.701
to call for mental
health crises

01:40:41.734 --> 01:40:43.300
or when you lost your dog

01:40:43.334 --> 01:40:46.000
or for a neighbor's arguing
with each other

01:40:46.033 --> 01:40:48.033
about the sound or a driver

01:40:48.067 --> 01:40:50.334
who pulled over to fall asleep.

01:40:50.367 --> 01:40:52.267
You know, they know
they're not the one

01:40:52.300 --> 01:40:53.901
that they're spread thin
on these issues.

01:40:53.934 --> 01:40:55.400
And if they're only trained

01:40:55.434 --> 01:40:58.701
to enforce criminal code with
various forms of violence,

01:40:58.734 --> 01:41:00.801
we're doing us all a favor
by taking that off their plate

01:41:00.834 --> 01:41:03.601
and putting that
into a safer place.

01:41:03.634 --> 01:41:06.501
So, I was glad to see
that he was open

01:41:06.534 --> 01:41:08.133
to kind of wrestle
with an idea

01:41:08.167 --> 01:41:11.167
and not have an easy,
deflective quip.

01:41:11.200 --> 01:41:12.467
Mm-hmm.

01:41:12.501 --> 01:41:15.400
And I wanted to really quickly
just touch on the idea

01:41:15.434 --> 01:41:20.367
of around morality as a police
officer or as a deputy sheriff

01:41:20.400 --> 01:41:22.767
or just working in
that field in general.

01:41:22.801 --> 01:41:24.400
One thing that stuck out to me

01:41:24.434 --> 01:41:28.200
was Eric was saying that
when all else fails,

01:41:28.234 --> 01:41:30.801
or, you know, if anything's
ever in question,

01:41:30.834 --> 01:41:33.701
I teach my trainees
to always do the right thing.

01:41:33.734 --> 01:41:37.567
And that is,
it's too broad of an idea

01:41:37.601 --> 01:41:40.567
when people's lives are
in your hands, right.

01:41:40.601 --> 01:41:42.367
So, it's like you
know we're sure

01:41:42.400 --> 01:41:46.167
that the police officer who
was on George Floyd's neck

01:41:46.200 --> 01:41:48.067
thought he was doing
the right thing.

01:41:48.100 --> 01:41:48.934
Sure.

01:41:48.968 --> 01:41:51.534
Your bias directly affects
what you think in that moment

01:41:51.567 --> 01:41:53.200
is the right thing to do.

01:41:53.234 --> 01:41:56.267
So if we're not all addressing
our biases

01:41:56.300 --> 01:41:59.667
and because they have
the power to, you know,

01:41:59.701 --> 01:42:03.734
really craft and mold the
outcomes of these situations,

01:42:03.767 --> 01:42:05.267
there's a larger conversation

01:42:05.300 --> 01:42:07.300
that needs to be had within
these police departments

01:42:07.334 --> 01:42:10.200
because you know I mean,
it all starts with bias.

01:42:10.234 --> 01:42:13.767
And across the board
can truly affect

01:42:13.801 --> 01:42:15.501
whether someone lives or dies.

01:42:21.901 --> 01:42:24.033
All right, let's dive into
our next conversation.

01:42:24.067 --> 01:42:27.200
We have Fred calling in
from Pennsylvania

01:42:27.234 --> 01:42:29.467
and we have
Paul from Colorado.

01:42:38.868 --> 01:42:40.834
There we go.

01:42:40.868 --> 01:42:41.567
Sweet.

01:42:41.601 --> 01:42:43.067
Hey, Fred, how you doing?

01:42:43.100 --> 01:42:44.767
Good. How are you doing, Paul?

01:42:44.801 --> 01:42:49.734
Oh, just peachy, man.
[laughs]

01:42:49.767 --> 01:42:51.067
Where you from?

01:42:51.100 --> 01:42:52.901
Oh, you're a handsome lad.

01:42:52.934 --> 01:42:54.868
[laughs] Thank you.

01:42:54.901 --> 01:42:57.234
I am originally from Montreal.

01:42:57.267 --> 01:42:58.801
Married with children?

01:42:58.834 --> 01:43:02.400
I married a Puerto Rican
women two years ago.

01:43:02.434 --> 01:43:06.000
Out of that I got a 22
and a 21 year old.

01:43:06.033 --> 01:43:07.400
They're gorgeous kids.

01:43:07.434 --> 01:43:08.400
Very blessed.

01:43:08.434 --> 01:43:09.434
Good, nice.

01:43:09.467 --> 01:43:11.400
My oldest son's an engineer

01:43:11.434 --> 01:43:15.801
and my daughters
go to private school.

01:43:15.834 --> 01:43:18.834
And my oldest, next oldest son
was going to private school

01:43:18.868 --> 01:43:20.601
as well for a while.

01:43:20.634 --> 01:43:25.234
So, we're fortunate to be able
to provide our kids with...

01:43:25.267 --> 01:43:26.501
Yeah.

01:43:26.534 --> 01:43:28.634
...you know,
that kind of education.

01:43:28.667 --> 01:43:32.100
Let me intercept here
on what you just said that

01:43:32.200 --> 01:43:35.567
"We're very fortunate
that we can do this".

01:43:35.601 --> 01:43:38.033
What does that mean?

01:43:38.067 --> 01:43:45.567
That means that I grew up in
a pretty rough neighborhood.

01:43:45.601 --> 01:43:46.601
Okay.

01:43:46.634 --> 01:43:51.601
I had very serious run-ins
with the police as a child.

01:43:51.634 --> 01:43:55.100
I've been arrested
four times by the police.

01:43:55.133 --> 01:43:57.701
I've been roughed up
by the police several times

01:43:57.734 --> 01:44:00.300
and had the unfortunate experience

01:44:00.334 --> 01:44:02.934
of burying over 50 kids
from gang violence.

01:44:02.968 --> 01:44:06.334
And then, I had a kid die
in my arms in '91.

01:44:06.367 --> 01:44:09.234
And so, I've known death a lot.

01:44:09.267 --> 01:44:11.467
And so, I say fortunate

01:44:11.501 --> 01:44:15.367
because I've been able to ascend

01:44:15.400 --> 01:44:18.968
in the job market in our region.

01:44:19.033 --> 01:44:20.934
I'm the first black CEO

01:44:20.968 --> 01:44:23.667
of the company
I'm currently in charge of.

01:44:23.701 --> 01:44:26.167
I can't relate to what
you're talking about

01:44:26.200 --> 01:44:29.434
because where I come from,
we don't have these issues.

01:44:29.467 --> 01:44:33.934
The biggest problem I had
growing up as a kid,

01:44:33.968 --> 01:44:36.400
it wasn't race for me.

01:44:36.434 --> 01:44:38.501
I grew up French, okay?

01:44:38.534 --> 01:44:41.767
I didn't learn to really speak
English until I was 12.

01:44:41.801 --> 01:44:47.501
Most of my education
was all in French.

01:44:47.534 --> 01:44:52.200
But my name was English.

01:44:52.234 --> 01:44:54.200
That caused
problems at school.

01:44:54.234 --> 01:44:56.868
I got into a lot of fights
in the schoolyard,

01:44:56.901 --> 01:44:58.400
allies or whatever

01:44:58.434 --> 01:44:59.934
because I had an English name

01:44:59.968 --> 01:45:01.567
and I lived in a French sector.

01:45:01.601 --> 01:45:04.667
A lot of bombs were going off
in English sectors.

01:45:04.701 --> 01:45:07.200
And my mom was very
concerned about that

01:45:07.234 --> 01:45:09.968
because I was going to school
in an English area.

01:45:10.000 --> 01:45:15.634
Then I ended up moving back
to Colorado in 1996

01:45:15.667 --> 01:45:17.167
and I've been here ever since.

01:45:33.167 --> 01:45:36.100
I can't agree to a lot
of the visuals I just saw

01:45:36.133 --> 01:45:39.567
because that's just not who I am.

01:45:39.601 --> 01:45:42.267
I've never been a
part of that, never will.

01:45:42.300 --> 01:45:45.434
And so, I find it disturbing.

01:45:45.467 --> 01:45:52.901
And I think the idea, the deep
idea has really not improved.

01:45:52.934 --> 01:45:57.367
For me, unfortunately
I resonated very much

01:45:57.400 --> 01:46:01.501
with the video,

01:46:01.534 --> 01:46:03.767
in particular him being a judge.

01:46:03.801 --> 01:46:07.200
I have some level of recognition

01:46:07.234 --> 01:46:09.901
in the City of Pittsburgh

01:46:09.934 --> 01:46:13.968
and so actually
I get treated differently

01:46:14.000 --> 01:46:16.300
if I come into a place
and I'm dressed down.

01:46:16.334 --> 01:46:18.033
People don't even speak to me,

01:46:18.067 --> 01:46:20.000
people that have known
me for years.

01:46:20.033 --> 01:46:26.133
And so, people are used
to this suit being me.

01:46:26.167 --> 01:46:30.167
I would say from
a context perspective,

01:46:30.200 --> 01:46:33.067
there has always been slavery
all around the world

01:46:33.100 --> 01:46:34.667
for millennia.

01:46:34.701 --> 01:46:36.000
Oh, yeah, yeah.

01:46:36.033 --> 01:46:38.400
The kind of slavery
that took place in America

01:46:38.434 --> 01:46:41.434
is the only one of its kind,
chattel slavery.

01:46:41.467 --> 01:46:43.534
For 246 years,

01:46:43.567 --> 01:46:47.467
America used black people
from the continent,

01:46:47.501 --> 01:46:49.300
from sun up to sun down,

01:46:49.334 --> 01:46:54.067
to create a super power
in less than 200 years.

01:46:54.100 --> 01:46:57.667
After 246 years of slavery,

01:46:57.701 --> 01:47:02.000
it was followed by a 135 years
of institutional racism.

01:47:02.033 --> 01:47:07.501
Jim Crow laws, redlining,
making it illegal for blacks

01:47:07.534 --> 01:47:10.334
to have the same benefits
of their white counterparts

01:47:10.367 --> 01:47:14.033
even after they went to fight
in the world wars.

01:47:14.067 --> 01:47:15.534
And now in America

01:47:15.567 --> 01:47:19.033
we have 55 years
of alleged freedom and equity

01:47:19.067 --> 01:47:23.667
which many of us in America
don't see that way.

01:47:23.701 --> 01:47:26.334
And I can tell you that
my experiences

01:47:26.367 --> 01:47:28.501
as a black man with education,

01:47:28.534 --> 01:47:32.501
having went to school
several times,

01:47:32.534 --> 01:47:38.033
I am challenged every day
by microaggressions.

01:47:38.067 --> 01:47:40.067
I'm challenged when
I walk in a store.

01:47:40.100 --> 01:47:44.100
I know that feeling
of sitting in a restaurant

01:47:44.133 --> 01:47:46.300
and people being
directed to sit near you.

01:47:46.334 --> 01:47:48.234
And they go,
"No, I want to sit over here."

01:47:48.267 --> 01:47:49.667
What?

01:47:49.701 --> 01:47:52.300
And you have to tell your child

01:47:52.334 --> 01:47:54.801
it's okay when it's not okay.

01:47:54.834 --> 01:47:57.033
And so, Paul,
I was very interested

01:47:57.067 --> 01:47:59.434
and struck by your experiences

01:47:59.467 --> 01:48:04.801
and also struck by the fact
that that video moved you

01:48:04.834 --> 01:48:08.634
because you never had that
experience and that's not you.

01:48:08.667 --> 01:48:11.400
But were you able
to, for a moment,

01:48:11.434 --> 01:48:14.868
put yourself in that
person's shoes and window

01:48:14.901 --> 01:48:17.100
and just wonder
for a brief moment

01:48:17.133 --> 01:48:18.968
what that might feel like?

01:48:19.033 --> 01:48:21.334
Well, let me interject here.

01:48:21.367 --> 01:48:25.167
First of all, it's not
that it didn't move me.

01:48:25.200 --> 01:48:26.200
It did.

01:48:26.234 --> 01:48:27.834
It pissed me off.

01:48:27.868 --> 01:48:30.367
You know, Fred,
I'm just not seeing

01:48:30.400 --> 01:48:34.267
that this country as I mentioned
earlier, is really improving.

01:48:34.300 --> 01:48:36.667
Have you ever thought about,

01:48:36.701 --> 01:48:41.267
or had to give the talk
to your daughter

01:48:41.300 --> 01:48:45.133
or to your new—

01:48:45.167 --> 01:48:46.033
Son?

01:48:46.067 --> 01:48:49.901
—children in your marriage
about any kind of interaction

01:48:49.934 --> 01:48:54.601
where their race or beliefs
might cause them

01:48:54.634 --> 01:48:59.534
some social discomfort or
be challenged by authorities?

01:48:59.567 --> 01:49:04.100
If I had to have a conversation
with my kids about this,

01:49:04.133 --> 01:49:05.868
it would be hard for me

01:49:05.901 --> 01:49:08.400
because I wouldn't know
pretty much where to start

01:49:08.434 --> 01:49:10.634
because I mean,
I haven't had this problem.

01:49:10.667 --> 01:49:12.033
But you know what?

01:49:12.067 --> 01:49:15.801
You have to because they're
being bombarded with this

01:49:15.834 --> 01:49:18.834
24 hours a day.

01:49:18.868 --> 01:49:23.367
How I treat and talk to my
children is very much reflective

01:49:23.400 --> 01:49:27.000
of the environment that they
walk into every day.

01:49:27.033 --> 01:49:29.300
One of the most
difficult conversations

01:49:29.334 --> 01:49:33.100
I've had to have with my son
is how to conduct yourself

01:49:33.133 --> 01:49:34.634
when the police
pulled him over

01:49:34.667 --> 01:49:36.801
and they will get pulled over.

01:49:36.834 --> 01:49:41.000
My most difficult conversation
was years ago

01:49:41.033 --> 01:49:45.100
having my oldest son
drive one of my vehicles

01:49:45.133 --> 01:49:48.934
and be pulled over by the
police and harassed physically

01:49:48.968 --> 01:49:51.834
because he was driving
a very expensive vehicle.

01:49:51.868 --> 01:49:54.300
Then the officer threw him up
against the vehicle

01:49:54.334 --> 01:49:56.200
and started harassing him

01:49:56.234 --> 01:49:59.033
about what does his father
do or mother do?

01:49:59.067 --> 01:50:05.601
And so, you know,
that really hurt us.

01:50:05.634 --> 01:50:07.868
What you see today
in the marching

01:50:07.901 --> 01:50:13.801
and the sit-ins and the protesting

01:50:13.834 --> 01:50:17.801
is really saying enough is enough.

01:50:17.834 --> 01:50:20.701
You've had your foot
on our necks

01:50:20.734 --> 01:50:23.667
literally for 400 years

01:50:23.701 --> 01:50:26.234
and we're not going
to take it anymore.

01:50:26.267 --> 01:50:28.801
Well, you know, I'm going
to say something

01:50:28.834 --> 01:50:32.367
about the protest movements,
you know.

01:50:32.400 --> 01:50:36.701
The change or the difference
I found is that they became—

01:50:36.734 --> 01:50:40.667
this became global.

01:50:40.701 --> 01:50:43.400
You know, you were having
different countries in the world

01:50:43.434 --> 01:50:46.100
that were walking around
with banners

01:50:46.133 --> 01:50:50.100
on what happened to George,
even my country.

01:50:50.133 --> 01:50:54.033
Now, I've got some interesting
feelings about this looting

01:50:54.067 --> 01:50:58.067
and all the stuff that's going
on behind the protestors

01:50:58.100 --> 01:51:01.234
because that's not the message.

01:51:01.267 --> 01:51:03.367
So, I think that
that's something

01:51:03.400 --> 01:51:06.701
that might have been thrown
in the wash and tainted.

01:51:06.734 --> 01:51:09.834
Why don't you look at the other
side of the fence, you know?

01:51:09.868 --> 01:51:13.734
The police department,
you know, what they're dealing with.

01:51:13.767 --> 01:51:15.334
Okay? They got a job to do,

01:51:15.367 --> 01:51:20.767
but I think some of them
unfortunately take that badge

01:51:20.801 --> 01:51:24.234
and the equipment that comes
with it a little too seriously.

01:51:24.267 --> 01:51:27.367
And unfortunately,
there's a problem there.

01:51:27.400 --> 01:51:31.234
And it's everywhere in
this country, okay?

01:51:31.267 --> 01:51:32.400
We know.

01:51:32.434 --> 01:51:35.200
We got a sideline of cops
that work together as a unit.

01:51:35.234 --> 01:51:36.767
They back each other up

01:51:36.801 --> 01:51:39.300
and they're all part
of a little clique

01:51:39.334 --> 01:51:42.133
that sometimes causes
problems like this.

01:51:42.167 --> 01:51:45.200
And they feel because
they have this uniform

01:51:45.234 --> 01:51:47.200
they can take all this authority.

01:51:47.234 --> 01:51:49.467
One of the things I honor
about people

01:51:49.501 --> 01:51:54.901
is when people are transparent
and fully vulnerable

01:51:54.934 --> 01:51:59.067
and I think that's something

01:51:59.100 --> 01:52:03.000
that men in particular struggle with.

01:52:03.033 --> 01:52:08.501
Our bravado, our disposition,

01:52:08.534 --> 01:52:12.334
the things people expect us
to do and focus on.

01:52:12.367 --> 01:52:16.901
It really has created a false
sense of control for us.

01:52:16.934 --> 01:52:21.234
And so, you know, Paul,
I feel like we come

01:52:21.267 --> 01:52:23.868
from two different sides
of the world.

01:52:23.901 --> 01:52:27.968
There's some similarities
in how we were raised.

01:52:28.000 --> 01:52:29.601
I'll tell you
something right now

01:52:29.634 --> 01:52:31.868
in comparison
to where we relate again,

01:52:31.901 --> 01:52:37.801
is that growing up I didn't
have anybody to guide me.

01:52:37.834 --> 01:52:39.868
I didn't have a father, okay.

01:52:39.901 --> 01:52:42.434
I didn't have a father
to guide me,

01:52:42.467 --> 01:52:48.567
to give me wisdom, to teach me
to basically even back me up.

01:52:48.601 --> 01:52:55.467
So, my whole life as a kid,
I had like a mask on.

01:52:55.501 --> 01:52:57.901
And I was always trying to be
something that I wasn't

01:52:57.934 --> 01:52:59.667
because I didn't feel
I was good enough.

01:52:59.701 --> 01:53:01.434
It's funny,
the more that you talk,

01:53:01.467 --> 01:53:03.000
the more we have similarities.

01:53:03.033 --> 01:53:05.601
I grew up from a broken home.

01:53:05.634 --> 01:53:08.300
My mother and father divorced
when I was young.

01:53:08.334 --> 01:53:09.100
Yep.

01:53:09.133 --> 01:53:11.133
Because when you allow yourself

01:53:11.167 --> 01:53:15.501
to listen to another
person's perspective,

01:53:15.534 --> 01:53:19.901
you realize that many of their
experiences mirrors your own.

01:53:19.934 --> 01:53:23.100
But when you dig deeper,
and this is implicit biases,

01:53:23.133 --> 01:53:25.400
I can look at him
and he can look at me

01:53:25.434 --> 01:53:26.968
and go,
"Hey, that's a black guy."

01:53:27.000 --> 01:53:28.701
I could go, "That's a white guy."

01:53:28.734 --> 01:53:31.667
But after an hour
and a half, two hours,

01:53:31.701 --> 01:53:36.701
we're two different people who
happen to have one brown skin,

01:53:36.734 --> 01:53:38.467
one white skin.

01:53:38.501 --> 01:53:41.934
But the people who
possess those skins

01:53:41.968 --> 01:53:44.701
are not defined by black or white.

01:53:44.734 --> 01:53:46.501
And I think that's the importance

01:53:46.534 --> 01:53:50.100
of having conversations
about bias and implicit bias

01:53:50.133 --> 01:53:54.200
is that it allows us
to expand our own horizons

01:53:54.234 --> 01:53:58.534
in a way to push us to
new levels of understanding,

01:53:58.567 --> 01:54:00.868
appreciation and patience

01:54:00.901 --> 01:54:03.133
that is required for us to figure out

01:54:03.167 --> 01:54:05.234
how we're going to turn
this thing around.

01:54:05.267 --> 01:54:06.601
I need Paul.

01:54:06.634 --> 01:54:09.734
It's the totality of all of
our experiences that are going

01:54:09.767 --> 01:54:13.934
to offer solutions to the
global phenomena we face.

01:54:13.968 --> 01:54:15.868
I can just see us, you know,

01:54:15.901 --> 01:54:17.834
bumping into each other in a bar

01:54:17.868 --> 01:54:19.567
and we would have
this conversation.

01:54:19.601 --> 01:54:21.133
And I'm pretty sure
you'd probably say,

01:54:21.167 --> 01:54:23.367
"You're not from here,
are you?"

01:54:23.400 --> 01:54:29.167
And but there should be more
people that are not from here.

01:54:29.200 --> 01:54:32.434
When you find a kindred spirit,

01:54:32.467 --> 01:54:35.200
it's really about honoring

01:54:35.234 --> 01:54:39.934
their humanity in ways that
allows the DNA of the exchange

01:54:39.968 --> 01:54:42.868
to the conversation to live
in a part of you

01:54:42.901 --> 01:54:47.868
so that you always honor
the person who moved you

01:54:47.901 --> 01:54:49.667
or filled your vessel

01:54:49.701 --> 01:54:51.834
with a different fluid
that allows you

01:54:51.868 --> 01:54:55.234
to have more diversity of
thought, action and beliefs.

01:54:55.267 --> 01:54:58.601
So, I appreciate that
and thank you.

01:54:58.634 --> 01:55:03.234
As I would say,
"&lt;i&gt;A la &lt;/i&gt;prochaine fois, mon ami,"

01:55:03.267 --> 01:55:05.834
which means,
"Until next time, my friend."

01:55:05.868 --> 01:55:07.067
&lt;i&gt;Oui&lt;/i&gt;.

01:55:08.667 --> 01:55:10.033
As long as you don't say it twice.

01:55:10.067 --> 01:55:12.367
That means,
"You gotta go somewhere".

01:55:12.400 --> 01:55:14.334
All right, man.
Love you, buddy.

01:55:14.367 --> 01:55:16.234
All right, love you, man.
Appreciate you.

01:55:16.267 --> 01:55:17.601
- Take care.
- Yes, sir.

01:55:17.634 --> 01:55:19.100
- Okay, bye-bye.
- Bye-bye.

01:55:38.968 --> 01:55:42.334
Well, that was a cute little
ending to a conversation.

01:55:42.367 --> 01:55:44.634
I felt like they had this like
really beautiful camaraderie

01:55:44.667 --> 01:55:46.400
and almost like
a brotherhood by the end

01:55:46.434 --> 01:55:48.834
which was really
refreshing and nice to see.

01:55:48.868 --> 01:55:50.801
Just a reminder you guys,
these are strangers.

01:55:50.834 --> 01:55:52.701
This was their first time
ever speaking to

01:55:52.734 --> 01:55:54.267
and meeting one another.

01:55:54.300 --> 01:55:56.767
So the fact that they were
able to share experiences

01:55:56.801 --> 01:55:58.434
and sort of compare
and contrast

01:55:58.467 --> 01:56:00.901
and then you know end it
on such a really loving note,

01:56:00.934 --> 01:56:02.934
was really nice.

01:56:02.968 --> 01:56:03.701
Yeah.

01:56:03.734 --> 01:56:05.000
It was sweet.

01:56:05.033 --> 01:56:06.334
I think that
it's also an example

01:56:06.367 --> 01:56:07.934
because they're
very different.

01:56:07.968 --> 01:56:10.300
They have very different
experiences as were highlighted

01:56:10.334 --> 01:56:12.334
through the conversation
which we'll talk about

01:56:12.367 --> 01:56:16.167
and some presuppositions
and misunderstandings

01:56:16.200 --> 01:56:18.200
of different understandings of reality.

01:56:18.234 --> 01:56:19.901
But what it does show

01:56:19.934 --> 01:56:22.734
is really for me the value
of these conversations

01:56:22.767 --> 01:56:25.167
because you start
where you are.

01:56:25.200 --> 01:56:27.334
It doesn't matter if you have 15

01:56:27.367 --> 01:56:29.367
or 55, you start where you are

01:56:29.400 --> 01:56:30.968
in terms of in terms
of exposure

01:56:31.033 --> 01:56:32.367
and wrestling
with new ideas

01:56:32.400 --> 01:56:34.434
being challenged
on your position.

01:56:34.467 --> 01:56:37.200
There is no too early in this case

01:56:37.234 --> 01:56:39.567
we're talking about adults
to have these conversations.

01:56:39.601 --> 01:56:41.501
And I feel like Fred
challenged Paul

01:56:41.534 --> 01:56:43.434
on a lot of his presuppositions.

01:56:43.467 --> 01:56:46.300
We talked about
the aging up of black youth.

01:56:46.334 --> 01:56:48.534
This making them—

01:56:48.567 --> 01:56:50.567
you know, we've had studies
that demonstrate

01:56:50.601 --> 01:56:53.367
that there's after about nine,
10 years old

01:56:53.400 --> 01:56:55.601
black minors become adults,

01:56:55.634 --> 01:56:57.501
they get older than
the way they actually are

01:56:57.534 --> 01:56:59.234
to make them more threatening.

01:56:59.267 --> 01:57:00.901
They are inflated.

01:57:00.934 --> 01:57:02.601
Their persona has inflated

01:57:02.634 --> 01:57:04.167
to make it more
dangerous, menacing,

01:57:04.200 --> 01:57:06.567
easy to be described as a threat.

01:57:06.601 --> 01:57:08.734
These are things that
Paul was familiar with,

01:57:08.767 --> 01:57:10.901
but whenever
he gets exposed to it,

01:57:10.934 --> 01:57:12.400
he will be forever changed.

01:57:12.434 --> 01:57:15.501
You can't unsee and unlearn
historical context.

01:57:15.534 --> 01:57:17.467
So that's really one
of the beautiful things

01:57:17.501 --> 01:57:19.767
about having these
conversations.

01:57:19.801 --> 01:57:22.601
I want to quickly jump
into the idea of parenting

01:57:22.634 --> 01:57:24.701
and sort of the difference
between, you know,

01:57:24.734 --> 01:57:27.868
how white people are
raising their children

01:57:27.901 --> 01:57:29.968
versus how black people
are raising their children

01:57:30.000 --> 01:57:31.567
and the conversations,

01:57:31.601 --> 01:57:33.334
and how the conversations
differ, right?

01:57:33.367 --> 01:57:35.501
So, Paul was saying, you know,
he wouldn't even know

01:57:35.534 --> 01:57:38.033
where to start in terms of
having these conversations

01:57:38.067 --> 01:57:40.934
with his kids about racism,
about bias, about prejudice

01:57:40.968 --> 01:57:42.834
and how to be more
aware of your own

01:57:42.868 --> 01:57:44.534
so that you can make
the lives of others,

01:57:44.567 --> 01:57:46.200
who are different
from you, better,

01:57:46.234 --> 01:57:49.000
like help them have a little
bit more of a better day

01:57:49.033 --> 01:57:51.067
than they may have
previously experienced

01:57:51.100 --> 01:57:52.567
before coming in contact
with them.

01:57:52.601 --> 01:57:54.801
But then Fred was saying
like after watching &lt;i&gt;The Look&lt;/i&gt;

01:57:54.834 --> 01:57:56.734
he was like,
"Oh yeah, you know,

01:57:56.767 --> 01:57:59.234
I can completely relate
to all of these scenarios.

01:57:59.267 --> 01:58:01.400
I've had the elevator
door shut on me."

01:58:01.434 --> 01:58:03.868
People have locked their
doors when I've walked by

01:58:03.901 --> 01:58:06.734
and, you know,
and he wears suits for work.

01:58:06.767 --> 01:58:09.367
So, he notices a real
difference in how he's treated

01:58:09.400 --> 01:58:10.734
when he's wearing suits

01:58:10.767 --> 01:58:12.367
and then when he's out
with his kids

01:58:12.400 --> 01:58:14.000
and these microaggressions
happen.

01:58:14.033 --> 01:58:16.434
He has to sort of,
you know, lay low,

01:58:16.467 --> 01:58:19.567
not get too, you know,
visually upset

01:58:19.601 --> 01:58:21.801
and sort of to calm the situation

01:58:21.834 --> 01:58:23.634
and to deescalate it,
tell his kids,

01:58:23.667 --> 01:58:25.167
"You know, it's okay.
It's not a problem"

01:58:25.200 --> 01:58:26.667
when it is a problem.

01:58:26.701 --> 01:58:28.167
And I think that
that's something

01:58:28.200 --> 01:58:30.968
that I think that you know
I can absolutely relate to

01:58:31.000 --> 01:58:34.200
in terms of just sort of that,
you know, how I was raised

01:58:34.234 --> 01:58:35.734
versus I think, you know,

01:58:35.767 --> 01:58:37.634
how some of my white
counterparts were raised

01:58:37.667 --> 01:58:39.267
who don't really
deal with that.

01:58:39.300 --> 01:58:43.701
Yeah, it's exhausting to have
to consistently pace yourself

01:58:43.734 --> 01:58:46.000
and be able to make it—

01:58:46.033 --> 01:58:48.434
do I use all my energy
on this now?

01:58:48.467 --> 01:58:51.667
Do I change the whole
experience in the room

01:58:51.701 --> 01:58:54.100
or do I want to just get out
of here and take a breath

01:58:54.133 --> 01:58:56.367
and deal with it
in the car or later?

01:58:56.400 --> 01:58:58.300
The degree to which
we don't have control

01:58:58.334 --> 01:59:00.267
over the onslaught,

01:59:00.300 --> 01:59:03.434
control over when the
wave is going to crash on us

01:59:03.467 --> 01:59:05.167
and what a luxury it is

01:59:05.200 --> 01:59:07.534
to just not ever have
to be confronted with this

01:59:07.567 --> 01:59:09.601
or have to talk to your
family about it

01:59:09.634 --> 01:59:11.400
and just being able
to go about your life

01:59:11.434 --> 01:59:13.067
pursuing your dreams
and interests.

01:59:13.100 --> 01:59:14.868
And whether the realities
of our experience

01:59:14.901 --> 01:59:16.334
is not for you to agree.

01:59:16.367 --> 01:59:18.667
It's not a matter of agree
or disagree.

01:59:18.701 --> 01:59:20.400
It's a matter of do I care or

01:59:20.434 --> 01:59:22.300
do I not, and being interested.

01:59:22.334 --> 01:59:24.033
I think a big takeaway for me

01:59:24.067 --> 01:59:26.934
was also sort of had to do
with the short film &lt;i&gt;The Look&lt;/i&gt;.

01:59:26.968 --> 01:59:28.634
And, you know,

01:59:28.667 --> 01:59:32.033
black man walking through
the world being mistreated.

01:59:32.067 --> 01:59:34.501
And then the reveal at the end
is that he's a judge.

01:59:34.534 --> 01:59:37.567
But I think something
to really highlight here

01:59:37.601 --> 01:59:40.000
is that even
if he was not the judge,

01:59:40.033 --> 01:59:43.367
even if he was the bailiff or
even if he was the custodian,

01:59:43.400 --> 01:59:45.567
he still does not deserve
to be treated

01:59:45.601 --> 01:59:47.033
in any of those ways.

01:59:47.067 --> 01:59:50.801
Even if people who belong in
marginalized communities

01:59:50.834 --> 01:59:53.934
are not stand-up citizens
or aren't heroes,

01:59:53.968 --> 01:59:55.868
they still deserve respect

01:59:55.901 --> 01:59:59.634
and they still deserve to go
home at the end of the day.

01:59:59.667 --> 02:00:04.734
Decency, fairness and freedom

02:00:04.767 --> 02:00:07.701
does not have to be re-earned

02:00:07.734 --> 02:00:10.501
every time a black person
steps outside of their house

02:00:10.534 --> 02:00:14.267
when white people
just get to be white.

02:00:22.000 --> 02:00:23.067
All right.

02:00:23.100 --> 02:00:24.868
Let's go into
this conversation,

02:00:24.901 --> 02:00:26.501
which really
stood out to me.

02:00:26.534 --> 02:00:29.701
This is between Kaylah
who calls in from New York

02:00:29.734 --> 02:00:32.200
and Angela calling
in from California.

02:00:32.234 --> 02:00:34.067
They're some gems.

02:00:41.167 --> 02:00:42.534
Hi.

02:00:42.567 --> 02:00:44.033
Hi.

02:00:44.067 --> 02:00:45.934
I'm Angela.

02:00:45.968 --> 02:00:47.133
I'm Kaylah.

02:00:47.167 --> 02:00:49.567
- Nice to meet you.
- You too.

02:01:07.667 --> 02:01:12.033
I feel like I have experienced

02:01:12.067 --> 02:01:15.701
the look my whole life
in high education,

02:01:15.734 --> 02:01:17.801
in predominately white spaces,

02:01:17.834 --> 02:01:22.267
like people assuming
I don't belong somewhere

02:01:22.300 --> 02:01:25.133
or wondering
why I am somewhere.

02:01:25.167 --> 02:01:30.701
For me what really stood out
is having these stares.

02:01:30.734 --> 02:01:33.234
And while I didn't grow
up with that—

02:01:33.267 --> 02:01:35.801
actually growing up
it was quite the opposite,

02:01:35.834 --> 02:01:39.300
no one seeing me
as an Asian American.

02:01:39.334 --> 02:01:43.601
And then now,
in the time of COVID-19,

02:01:43.634 --> 02:01:47.868
we go from invisibility
to hypervisibility.

02:01:47.901 --> 02:01:50.000
So when I walk out that door

02:01:50.033 --> 02:01:56.167
I am hypervigilant of the ways
in which I take up space.

02:01:56.200 --> 02:01:58.934
If I sneeze, is someone
going to give me a dirty look?

02:01:58.968 --> 02:02:01.300
Are people going to scoot back
when they see me

02:02:01.334 --> 02:02:02.767
because I'm Asian?

02:02:02.801 --> 02:02:06.701
And we too have been hurt
by systemic injustice.

02:02:06.734 --> 02:02:08.033
Yeah.

02:02:08.067 --> 02:02:09.968
Everyone has their own biases,

02:02:10.033 --> 02:02:14.234
everyone is biased in some way
about something or somebody,

02:02:14.267 --> 02:02:19.300
and then the line between just
being biased and being racist

02:02:19.334 --> 02:02:21.601
is like what you do after that.

02:02:21.634 --> 02:02:23.968
I think it's important
to talk about bias

02:02:24.000 --> 02:02:31.000
because it dispels any taboo
or stigma there is.

02:02:31.033 --> 02:02:33.901
When you name it,
when you call it out,

02:02:33.934 --> 02:02:35.934
when you know what it is,

02:02:35.968 --> 02:02:40.501
it kind of flashes a light on it.

02:02:40.534 --> 02:02:42.868
It can no longer hide in the dark.

02:02:42.901 --> 02:02:46.667
For my undergrad Capstone project

02:02:46.701 --> 02:02:50.267
I did a case study
on this woman.

02:02:50.300 --> 02:02:53.167
This black woman
who was in jail

02:02:53.200 --> 02:02:55.334
and she was pregnant
and she died in jail

02:02:55.367 --> 02:02:57.934
because she had
an ectopic pregnancy

02:02:57.968 --> 02:03:02.033
and the officers and the
nurses didn't help her.

02:03:02.067 --> 02:03:04.167
The other inmates
were saying like,

02:03:04.200 --> 02:03:08.067
"Hi, can you hear her,
she's like dying?"

02:03:08.100 --> 02:03:10.367
and nobody did anything.

02:03:10.400 --> 02:03:12.334
But in doing more research

02:03:12.367 --> 02:03:16.100
we see that those same guards
and nurses in that same jail

02:03:16.133 --> 02:03:18.901
15 years earlier let the
same exact thing happen

02:03:18.934 --> 02:03:21.534
to another black women
from the same exact cause.

02:03:21.567 --> 02:03:25.000
When you're saying
that too with the whole—

02:03:25.033 --> 02:03:26.901
kind of the health disparities

02:03:26.934 --> 02:03:29.534
between black and
brown communities,

02:03:29.567 --> 02:03:32.968
I'm thinking about the father
of gynecology

02:03:33.000 --> 02:03:36.868
perfected his fistula
operation on slave women

02:03:36.901 --> 02:03:38.734
without anesthetics.

02:03:38.767 --> 02:03:41.934
Something that we talked about
a lot in my classes

02:03:41.968 --> 02:03:44.934
and in my extracurricular
during my Master's

02:03:44.968 --> 02:03:48.033
was that this kind
of like general idea

02:03:48.067 --> 02:03:53.133
that black children, and black
women, girls in particular,

02:03:53.167 --> 02:03:55.334
kind of seen
as older than they are

02:03:55.367 --> 02:03:59.934
and seen as like being able
to withstand pain.

02:04:00.067 --> 02:04:05.667
I think a lot of our
understanding of this comes,

02:04:05.701 --> 02:04:09.067
you know, growing up, is
obviously in our history books.

02:04:09.100 --> 02:04:11.534
And they don't talk
about stuff like this

02:04:11.567 --> 02:04:14.601
and they kind of paint the
whole civil rights movement

02:04:14.634 --> 02:04:19.400
as like, oh, Dr. King came
and he fixed racism,

02:04:19.434 --> 02:04:22.601
Rosa Parks fixed racism and
now we're good, you know,

02:04:22.634 --> 02:04:26.434
and kids are kind of growing
up and they're like, you know,

02:04:26.467 --> 02:04:28.601
they see these things happen
and are like, wait,

02:04:28.634 --> 02:04:31.567
but I thought like racism is fixed.

02:04:31.601 --> 02:04:33.000
- No.
- Yeah.

02:04:33.033 --> 02:04:37.000
The first person to do
the Rosa Parks thing

02:04:37.033 --> 02:04:39.868
I guess and not get off
the bus was this young,

02:04:39.901 --> 02:04:42.300
dark-skinned girl
who was pregnant,

02:04:42.334 --> 02:04:44.667
but they didn't want her to be
the face of the movement.

02:04:44.701 --> 02:04:48.934
There's so many facets
of bias and racism

02:04:48.968 --> 02:04:53.267
within communities even,
not just between communities.

02:04:53.300 --> 02:04:57.267
My dad, he's very light-skinned
and I tried to tell him one time

02:04:57.300 --> 02:05:00.300
that colorism is a thing

02:05:00.334 --> 02:05:02.767
and light-skinned
privileges is a thing

02:05:02.801 --> 02:05:06.367
and he was like,
"No it's not.

02:05:06.400 --> 02:05:07.934
When I go to places,

02:05:07.968 --> 02:05:09.901
people will respect me
because I'm me."

02:05:09.934 --> 02:05:11.767
And I was just like,
"Okay but"—

02:05:11.801 --> 02:05:13.567
he almost couldn't recognize

02:05:13.601 --> 02:05:15.267
that if we were
to go someplace,

02:05:15.300 --> 02:05:17.000
we wouldn't get
the same reaction

02:05:17.033 --> 02:05:20.734
because like me being
a darker-skinned black woman

02:05:20.767 --> 02:05:23.000
and him being
a lighter-skinned man,

02:05:23.033 --> 02:05:27.167
not only is there the
lighter-skinned privilege,

02:05:27.200 --> 02:05:29.267
but also just you being a man.

02:05:29.300 --> 02:05:32.133
In a lot of spaces
that holds a lot of weight.

02:05:32.167 --> 02:05:35.334
I have also had a few conversations

02:05:35.367 --> 02:05:38.868
regarding, I guess just like
microaggressions.

02:05:38.901 --> 02:05:41.634
One conversation was
with my supervisor

02:05:41.667 --> 02:05:44.834
because I experienced some
microaggression at work.

02:05:44.868 --> 02:05:47.701
And he is a white male.

02:05:47.734 --> 02:05:49.467
He's in his 50s.

02:05:49.501 --> 02:05:51.834
And first thing he says is,

02:05:51.868 --> 02:05:53.801
"Angela, you know,

02:05:53.834 --> 02:05:57.868
I will never be able to
understand your experience.

02:05:57.901 --> 02:06:01.300
I recognize that I am a white male,

02:06:01.334 --> 02:06:04.167
but can you help me understand?"

02:06:04.200 --> 02:06:07.601
And I literally broke down
when he said that

02:06:07.634 --> 02:06:13.767
because it was such a place
of humility, that he took

02:06:13.801 --> 02:06:16.000
and I strongly believe

02:06:16.033 --> 02:06:18.901
that like the person
in the conversation

02:06:18.934 --> 02:06:21.033
in the room,
that has the least power,

02:06:21.067 --> 02:06:23.133
should not be the person
to bring up

02:06:23.167 --> 02:06:25.267
the hardest topic, right?

02:06:25.300 --> 02:06:29.367
And I think that
he created a space

02:06:29.400 --> 02:06:31.567
where he recognized that

02:06:31.601 --> 02:06:35.801
and he kind of lowered
himself to elevate me.

02:06:35.834 --> 02:06:38.133
I love that story.

02:06:38.167 --> 02:06:39.801
My question for you

02:06:39.834 --> 02:06:44.000
would also be like how does
your community—

02:06:44.033 --> 02:06:47.868
and I'm speaking specifically
black community—

02:06:47.901 --> 02:06:50.601
view Asian Americans?

02:06:50.634 --> 02:06:56.701
I have personally seen
and experienced

02:06:56.734 --> 02:06:59.968
a fair amount of racism from—

02:07:00.067 --> 02:07:02.667
I don't want to say the Asian
community but specific people,

02:07:02.701 --> 02:07:05.300
but I've also experienced
the opposite.

02:07:05.334 --> 02:07:06.834
But I do think that,

02:07:06.868 --> 02:07:09.067
in particular when
traveling abroad,

02:07:09.100 --> 02:07:11.567
it's like something that
I think about a lot,

02:07:11.601 --> 02:07:14.067
just the way that
anti-blackness is everywhere.

02:07:14.100 --> 02:07:20.767
And, again, I don't think this
is just in an Asian thing;

02:07:20.801 --> 02:07:25.601
I think a lot
of non-black communities

02:07:25.634 --> 02:07:29.300
want to have
the benefits of blackness,

02:07:29.334 --> 02:07:34.200
but none of the other stuff
that comes with it,

02:07:34.234 --> 02:07:36.767
none of the negative things
that come with it.

02:07:36.801 --> 02:07:41.601
I think that there is
a history of strife

02:07:41.634 --> 02:07:44.901
between the Asian community
and the black community

02:07:44.934 --> 02:07:48.901
and I think a lot of it is designed.

02:07:48.934 --> 02:07:53.133
The model minority, that term,
that model minority term

02:07:53.167 --> 02:07:55.534
was created by white people

02:07:55.567 --> 02:07:59.968
as a way to perpetuate
anti-blackness.

02:08:00.067 --> 02:08:04.434
What the model minority myth
is basically a term

02:08:04.467 --> 02:08:08.801
that white people have
created, saying that Asians,

02:08:08.834 --> 02:08:11.634
you know, look
how hard-working they are.

02:08:11.667 --> 02:08:13.200
They are a group of people

02:08:13.234 --> 02:08:15.634
that can pull themselves up
by the boot straps.

02:08:15.667 --> 02:08:18.868
Look how successful
they can be in our society.

02:08:18.901 --> 02:08:21.334
And then kind of pit that
against the black community

02:08:21.367 --> 02:08:23.467
and be like why aren't you
doing anything?

02:08:23.501 --> 02:08:27.968
If this group of immigrants
can do this, why can't you?

02:08:28.000 --> 02:08:31.801
To only justify
like they're position of like,

02:08:31.834 --> 02:08:34.868
oh, we're not racist;
you're the problem.

02:08:34.901 --> 02:08:36.267
Yeah.

02:08:36.300 --> 02:08:38.534
So I think we must
understand that,

02:08:38.567 --> 02:08:41.234
how we've been used
in that way,

02:08:41.267 --> 02:08:43.834
like weaponized, you know,
against the black community.

02:08:43.868 --> 02:08:49.200
We have this like perpetual
foreigner status.

02:08:49.234 --> 02:08:53.467
We are a model minority until
you're a perpetual foreigner.

02:08:53.501 --> 02:08:56.634
It's kind of like the idea
of like pet to threat.

02:08:56.667 --> 02:09:02.534
Like we're good with you
until like corona virus happens

02:09:02.567 --> 02:09:05.000
and you're a threat,
like your yellow peril.

02:09:05.033 --> 02:09:08.033
Yeah, wow, yeah.

02:09:08.067 --> 02:09:13.200
In kind of the beginning
of the pandemic I guess,

02:09:13.234 --> 02:09:17.267
I had like a little bit
of a heated conversation

02:09:17.300 --> 02:09:18.934
with a friend of mine

02:09:18.968 --> 02:09:22.033
because he had been
in Shanghai for work

02:09:22.067 --> 02:09:25.834
and when he came back
his coworker said something

02:09:25.868 --> 02:09:29.901
like, "I hope you didn't bring
anything back,

02:09:29.934 --> 02:09:31.434
we're going to have
to separate

02:09:31.467 --> 02:09:33.934
one of the Asian
American employees."

02:09:33.968 --> 02:09:36.501
And he told me about it
later and I said,

02:09:36.534 --> 02:09:38.634
"Okay, so what did you say?"

02:09:38.667 --> 02:09:40.467
And he said, "Nothing."

02:09:40.501 --> 02:09:45.367
And I was so upset
because in that moment

02:09:45.400 --> 02:09:48.601
that person has enough
either respect for you

02:09:48.634 --> 02:09:51.200
or has enough
of a relationship with you

02:09:51.234 --> 02:09:52.934
to tell a joke like that,

02:09:52.968 --> 02:09:56.434
then it's on you to use that position

02:09:56.467 --> 02:09:59.734
to say, "Hey, this is not okay."

02:09:59.767 --> 02:10:02.133
It's just unacceptable at this point

02:10:02.167 --> 02:10:05.701
to not speak up about things,

02:10:05.734 --> 02:10:08.200
especially when the other
person is not in the room

02:10:08.234 --> 02:10:09.868
to defend themselves.

02:10:09.901 --> 02:10:13.567
I feel like you can't choose
when to be like antiracist

02:10:13.601 --> 02:10:15.701
because all these issues
are connected

02:10:15.734 --> 02:10:19.267
and all of these issues
affect real human beings.

02:10:19.300 --> 02:10:21.167
How do you think

02:10:21.200 --> 02:10:25.567
Asian Americans
can show up for black lives

02:10:25.601 --> 02:10:29.133
in an Asian American way,
however you perceive that?

02:10:29.167 --> 02:10:31.901
For a start, I would just say like

02:10:31.934 --> 02:10:36.100
using your own voice
to speak out generally

02:10:36.133 --> 02:10:41.000
and then also recognizing
the privilege

02:10:41.033 --> 02:10:45.601
that some people have to benefit

02:10:45.634 --> 02:10:48.667
from black culture and blackness

02:10:48.701 --> 02:10:54.000
but not have any
of the bad implications of it.

02:10:54.033 --> 02:10:59.167
And then also when you're
elevated to a certain level,

02:10:59.200 --> 02:11:02.567
making space for other people
like you in that level;

02:11:02.601 --> 02:11:04.501
whether you're a woman
in the boardroom,

02:11:04.534 --> 02:11:07.901
make room for other women
in those spaces or make room

02:11:07.934 --> 02:11:10.200
for other people of color
in those spaces

02:11:10.234 --> 02:11:12.000
I think is important.

02:11:12.033 --> 02:11:15.300
I think often times
the racial discourse

02:11:15.334 --> 02:11:18.234
has been under a binary
of black and white

02:11:18.267 --> 02:11:21.167
and that kind of renders
people in-between

02:11:21.200 --> 02:11:23.501
as feeling invisible

02:11:23.534 --> 02:11:26.033
or feeling irrelevant
to the discourse.

02:11:26.067 --> 02:11:28.000
And I think that's
why a lot of people

02:11:28.033 --> 02:11:29.834
feel like they can't speak up

02:11:29.868 --> 02:11:32.100
because it's always about
black and white, right?

02:11:32.133 --> 02:11:33.767
Mm-hmm, yeah.

02:11:33.801 --> 02:11:39.534
And so what would be helpful
is to even offer space

02:11:39.567 --> 02:11:43.267
to non-black people of color

02:11:43.300 --> 02:11:47.200
to have those voices too,

02:11:47.234 --> 02:11:49.801
because there is something

02:11:49.834 --> 02:11:52.901
unique about their experiences.

02:11:52.934 --> 02:11:56.767
That is going to contribute
to allyship.

02:11:56.801 --> 02:12:00.133
Just like I might expect
friends of mine

02:12:00.167 --> 02:12:02.434
to be allies for
the black community,

02:12:02.467 --> 02:12:06.667
I expect for myself to be an
ally for other communities,

02:12:06.701 --> 02:12:09.467
including the Asian American
community,

02:12:09.501 --> 02:12:12.934
including the Hispanic
Latin communities,

02:12:12.968 --> 02:12:15.200
including any and everybody.

02:12:17.334 --> 02:12:19.400
I really enjoyed
our conversation, Kaylah,

02:12:19.434 --> 02:12:21.801
and like you said,

02:12:21.834 --> 02:12:24.934
I think that we do have
a lot in common

02:12:24.968 --> 02:12:26.367
and everything
that you were saying

02:12:26.400 --> 02:12:28.067
my heart was like yes!

02:12:28.100 --> 02:12:29.267
[laughs]

02:12:29.300 --> 02:12:31.133
Even though our experiences
are different,

02:12:31.167 --> 02:12:34.367
that feeling of not being seen,

02:12:34.400 --> 02:12:39.534
it's like the theme
of our conversation, right?

02:12:39.567 --> 02:12:40.968
Yeah, for sure.

02:12:41.033 --> 02:12:42.801
Thank you for talking with me.

02:12:42.834 --> 02:12:45.300
Yeah, thank you so much
for everything

02:12:45.334 --> 02:12:47.100
that you've shared with me.

02:12:47.133 --> 02:12:50.200
I think it's just something that
I really will treasure, thanks.

02:12:50.234 --> 02:12:51.300
Bye!

02:12:51.334 --> 02:12:52.901
Bye!

02:13:12.200 --> 02:13:14.834
I love the discussion
around women's health.

02:13:14.868 --> 02:13:19.334
They really sort of did
a deep dive on women's health

02:13:19.367 --> 02:13:23.400
in regards to especially
black women, in particular,

02:13:23.434 --> 02:13:27.467
and how our bodies have been
used for centuries

02:13:27.501 --> 02:13:30.334
within the medical field to test

02:13:30.367 --> 02:13:34.667
and to prod and pry
and figure things out.

02:13:34.701 --> 02:13:38.801
And specifically from the
"Father of Gynecology,"

02:13:38.834 --> 02:13:40.167
J. Marion Sims,

02:13:40.200 --> 02:13:44.667
he was perfecting his process
on black women's bodies

02:13:44.701 --> 02:13:46.767
only to blow up

02:13:46.801 --> 02:13:50.200
and really start this whole
sector of the medical field

02:13:50.234 --> 02:13:52.133
that hadn't really
been ironed out yet.

02:13:52.167 --> 02:13:55.901
Black women are constantly
kicked out of hospitals,

02:13:55.934 --> 02:13:58.334
or left for dead and neglected

02:13:58.367 --> 02:14:00.734
because they are not
only not treated

02:14:00.767 --> 02:14:02.467
as valuable human beings,

02:14:02.501 --> 02:14:05.000
but their symptoms must be—
that's also gendered—

02:14:05.033 --> 02:14:07.000
because they must be hysterical
because they're women.

02:14:07.033 --> 02:14:09.000
They're considered
to be stronger like oxen

02:14:09.033 --> 02:14:11.901
because we're born
here as chattels,

02:14:11.934 --> 02:14:14.734
as pieces of animals,
not human beings.

02:14:14.767 --> 02:14:17.467
So all that stuff, all that
bias, the slightest bias,

02:14:17.501 --> 02:14:19.701
well, I just thought
she was asymptomatic

02:14:19.734 --> 02:14:21.901
or I thought that was
probably just indigestion.

02:14:21.934 --> 02:14:24.767
That is fueled by a bias,

02:14:24.801 --> 02:14:27.167
like they're less reliable people

02:14:27.200 --> 02:14:30.234
that can't be trusted to know
themselves or others as well,

02:14:30.267 --> 02:14:31.701
they're less mature.

02:14:31.734 --> 02:14:33.234
Or they can handle it.

02:14:33.267 --> 02:14:34.667
Yeah they're stronger.

02:14:34.701 --> 02:14:38.868
Y'all, you're the face
of undying strength.

02:14:38.901 --> 02:14:40.167
Oh absolutely.

02:14:40.200 --> 02:14:41.634
I mean, I can say myself,
you know,

02:14:41.667 --> 02:14:43.501
I've heard the term
time and time again

02:14:43.534 --> 02:14:45.968
in regards to the Asian
American community

02:14:46.000 --> 02:14:48.367
as being the "model minorities,"
that kind of thing.

02:14:48.400 --> 02:14:52.033
And it totally makes sense,
but I never—

02:14:52.067 --> 02:14:54.968
like the way
that she articulated

02:14:55.033 --> 02:14:58.000
it was so eye-opening
because she was saying

02:14:58.033 --> 02:15:00.334
that term was developed
by white people.

02:15:00.367 --> 02:15:01.400
Absolutely.

02:15:01.434 --> 02:15:03.801
In order to weaponize
Asian people

02:15:03.834 --> 02:15:06.968
to really separate
the marginalized communities

02:15:07.033 --> 02:15:09.334
from each other so that
we can't band together

02:15:09.367 --> 02:15:12.434
and we don't feel like we are
sort of all in this together.

02:15:12.467 --> 02:15:14.501
It's really a lot of that separation.

02:15:14.534 --> 02:15:16.234
It's just like colorism
and slavery,

02:15:16.267 --> 02:15:21.033
white people in having
the house slaves

02:15:21.067 --> 02:15:22.601
and the field slaves,

02:15:22.634 --> 02:15:25.100
really driving that friction
between these communities.

02:15:25.133 --> 02:15:26.767
One of the things

02:15:26.801 --> 02:15:28.601
that I'm reminded of
in this conversation

02:15:28.634 --> 02:15:30.534
is, when I was studying
the Black Panthers

02:15:30.567 --> 02:15:32.133
I think in high school,

02:15:32.167 --> 02:15:36.734
and learning about Japanese
and Asian support

02:15:36.767 --> 02:15:38.400
for the Black Panthers

02:15:38.434 --> 02:15:41.901
and the term "yellow peril"
and seeing it in (inaudible)

02:15:41.934 --> 02:15:44.667
one of the first examples
to me of one minority

02:15:44.701 --> 02:15:46.634
standing up for the other.

02:15:46.667 --> 02:15:48.968
We're used to seeing
it around gender,

02:15:49.000 --> 02:15:51.901
sexual orientation in
this generation;

02:15:51.934 --> 02:15:53.934
men standing up
in the Me Too movement

02:15:53.968 --> 02:15:56.267
and people advocating
against sexual assault

02:15:56.300 --> 02:15:57.701
or rape culture

02:15:57.734 --> 02:16:00.667
or certainly straight cis
culture standing up

02:16:00.701 --> 02:16:04.400
and advocating for those
in the LGBTQI community.

02:16:04.434 --> 02:16:07.167
So we're used
to the structures there,

02:16:07.200 --> 02:16:09.767
but in minority
community there

02:16:09.801 --> 02:16:11.968
tends to be such isolation

02:16:12.033 --> 02:16:14.567
which is both its strength
and, in many ways,

02:16:14.601 --> 02:16:16.267
possibly it's a weakness

02:16:16.300 --> 02:16:17.701
because it creates
separateness.

02:16:17.734 --> 02:16:18.934
Yeah, yeah.

02:16:18.968 --> 02:16:20.634
And I think it sort of brings

02:16:20.667 --> 02:16:22.467
us back to the idea of allyship

02:16:22.501 --> 02:16:24.167
versus appropriation

02:16:24.200 --> 02:16:27.601
and making sure that if you
are taking and appreciating

02:16:27.634 --> 02:16:30.000
the cool parts of certain cultures

02:16:30.033 --> 02:16:32.601
and like the parts
that are like convenient,

02:16:32.634 --> 02:16:36.334
that are fun right, that
you're also doing research

02:16:36.367 --> 02:16:40.667
and you're also acknowledging
and amplifying the bad

02:16:40.701 --> 02:16:43.601
that also goes on
within those communities

02:16:43.634 --> 02:16:45.601
in efforts to change.

02:16:45.634 --> 02:16:48.067
So I think it's really just
being intentional.

02:16:48.100 --> 02:16:50.567
I think an example of that,
Freddie, that has worked

02:16:50.601 --> 02:16:52.534
to communicate
this idea to others

02:16:52.567 --> 02:16:55.234
is black women's hair styles
have been banned,

02:16:55.267 --> 02:16:57.567
made unprofessional,
you can't wear them.

02:16:57.601 --> 02:17:01.367
Or basically any hair styles
that white folks can't do

02:17:01.400 --> 02:17:03.234
are considered unprofessional
for black women;

02:17:03.267 --> 02:17:05.167
various braids,
dreadlocks, twists,

02:17:05.200 --> 02:17:06.701
Bantu knots, etcetera.

02:17:06.734 --> 02:17:08.400
They're just considered
unprofessional,

02:17:08.434 --> 02:17:10.934
just happens to be primarily
black hair expression.

02:17:10.968 --> 02:17:13.400
But once you start seeing

02:17:13.434 --> 02:17:16.534
pop-culture icons that are white

02:17:16.567 --> 02:17:18.501
or not black wearing corn rows

02:17:18.534 --> 02:17:20.567
and they rename them
as something else

02:17:20.601 --> 02:17:23.467
and they're on runways
for Chanel and Dior

02:17:23.501 --> 02:17:24.834
and durags
and all these things

02:17:24.868 --> 02:17:26.000
that we weren't allowed in

02:17:26.033 --> 02:17:28.334
your establishment if we had
our hair that way,

02:17:28.367 --> 02:17:30.734
if we wore Tim boots,
if we had drank this beer.

02:17:30.767 --> 02:17:33.100
There's all types
of codified racism, right?

02:17:33.133 --> 02:17:37.601
An example of how to be an ally

02:17:37.634 --> 02:17:41.033
is once you get in with
that hair style

02:17:41.067 --> 02:17:42.734
advocating that we all get in.

02:17:42.767 --> 02:17:43.567
Yep.

02:17:43.601 --> 02:17:46.167
If you just walk in and leave
them all out in the cold,

02:17:46.200 --> 02:17:47.801
that's when
you're appropriating,

02:17:47.834 --> 02:17:49.467
that's when
you're actually not

02:17:49.501 --> 02:17:51.133
down for anything
but yourself.

02:17:51.167 --> 02:17:53.167
Beautifully put.

02:17:53.200 --> 02:17:56.300
Well, that completes this
round of conversations.

02:17:56.334 --> 02:17:58.167
I think we covered
a lot of ground,

02:17:58.200 --> 02:18:01.901
had people from all over the
country being really personal

02:18:01.934 --> 02:18:04.167
and having really
productive conversations.

02:18:04.200 --> 02:18:07.334
I think there's a lot
to break down.

02:18:07.367 --> 02:18:09.634
You're doing it in the
comments of Dr. L'Pree.

02:18:09.667 --> 02:18:12.734
And we've tried to do our part

02:18:12.767 --> 02:18:14.467
by carrying you through
some of these things

02:18:14.501 --> 02:18:17.300
and sharing our thoughts,
insights and experiences.

02:18:17.334 --> 02:18:18.167
Yes.

02:18:18.200 --> 02:18:20.033
Thank you so much
to all the participants.

02:18:20.067 --> 02:18:22.901
This was such
a valuable experience

02:18:22.934 --> 02:18:24.834
and hopefully all the viewers
have enjoyed it

02:18:24.868 --> 02:18:28.534
and learned something as well,
because I definitely did.

02:18:28.567 --> 02:18:30.968
Yeah, it does take some bravery.

02:18:31.000 --> 02:18:34.567
I don't know if all of us would
have signed up to go in blind

02:18:34.601 --> 02:18:37.033
for these one-on-one
conversations sight unseen

02:18:37.067 --> 02:18:40.300
and for it to be broadcast
around the world;

02:18:40.334 --> 02:18:42.000
a hats-off to you all
for stepping up

02:18:42.033 --> 02:18:43.367
and being brave enough

02:18:43.400 --> 02:18:45.000
to have some
of these conversations.

02:18:45.033 --> 02:18:48.000
I've taken a lot from this
experience, I must say.

02:18:48.033 --> 02:18:49.634
I think throughout
we talked about

02:18:49.667 --> 02:18:52.067
some of the commonalities
that reared their heads,

02:18:52.100 --> 02:18:53.501
not necessary good or bad

02:18:53.534 --> 02:18:55.267
but in a way
that really helps us

02:18:55.300 --> 02:18:58.234
kind of understand exactly
why we do exercises like this.

02:18:58.267 --> 02:19:00.534
Do you have any ideas
that stuck out to you

02:19:00.567 --> 02:19:02.234
as particularly resonant

02:19:02.267 --> 02:19:04.334
and where people
showed some growth?

02:19:04.367 --> 02:19:08.567
Yeah, I mean, I think everyone
walked out, hopefully-

02:19:08.601 --> 02:19:11.334
and I really do think based
on what we were able to see,

02:19:11.367 --> 02:19:14.334
I think everyone walked out
of this experiment

02:19:14.367 --> 02:19:16.567
a little bit more aware

02:19:16.601 --> 02:19:18.400
and I think that,
that is the goal.

02:19:18.434 --> 02:19:20.100
You know, it's not
about being perfect,

02:19:20.133 --> 02:19:22.968
it's not about not having
bias, we all have our biases.

02:19:23.033 --> 02:19:24.734
So I think that it's really important

02:19:24.767 --> 02:19:27.367
that you recognize them

02:19:27.400 --> 02:19:28.968
and then what really counts

02:19:29.033 --> 02:19:30.701
is what you do once
you recognize them

02:19:30.734 --> 02:19:34.601
and how you sort of affect
change in the world.

02:19:34.634 --> 02:19:36.167
I think a big take away for me

02:19:36.200 --> 02:19:39.167
was also sort of had to do
with the short film &lt;i&gt;The Look&lt;/i&gt;

02:19:39.200 --> 02:19:43.200
and, you know, black man
walking through the world

02:19:43.234 --> 02:19:44.801
being mistreated

02:19:44.834 --> 02:19:46.934
and then the reveal at
the end is that he's a judge.

02:19:46.968 --> 02:19:49.534
And that's what we've seen
so much of in this last year

02:19:49.567 --> 02:19:52.934
is folks letting those
biases tumble, tumble,

02:19:52.968 --> 02:19:55.868
until they grow so heavy
that they can't be stopped

02:19:55.901 --> 02:19:58.234
and people just get out of control.

02:19:58.267 --> 02:20:00.400
I'm glad that didn't happen
in any of these conversations,

02:20:00.434 --> 02:20:02.901
so we could actually have
productive dialogue.

02:20:02.934 --> 02:20:05.033
Really impressed by the ability

02:20:05.067 --> 02:20:06.968
of a lot of our participants

02:20:07.033 --> 02:20:11.033
to step up, to adjust,
to not like what they hear

02:20:11.067 --> 02:20:13.901
but still find a way
to stay on an even keel

02:20:13.934 --> 02:20:15.667
and have a productive conversation.

02:20:15.701 --> 02:20:18.634
Yeah, I'm really happy
we did this, and, again,

02:20:18.667 --> 02:20:23.100
thankful to P&amp;G and BuzzFeed
for creating a space for it.

02:20:23.133 --> 02:20:25.033
It's pretty damn smart.

02:20:25.067 --> 02:20:27.267
Yes, thank you so much.

02:20:27.300 --> 02:20:29.501
And it's been great chatting
with you, Jesse,

02:20:29.534 --> 02:20:31.567
and just getting to know you more

02:20:31.601 --> 02:20:33.434
and really bounce ideas off
of each other.

02:20:33.467 --> 02:20:37.868
I mean I can't think of a better
pairing with us, you know?

02:20:37.901 --> 02:20:39.934
Yeah, for real, for real.
Take this show on the road.

02:20:39.968 --> 02:20:41.133
Yeah, yeah.

02:20:41.167 --> 02:20:42.534
One of the main goals

02:20:42.567 --> 02:20:44.234
for today's experiment
and conversation

02:20:44.267 --> 02:20:46.801
is for you to walk away
with some more confidence,

02:20:46.834 --> 02:20:48.801
some more tools,
some more courage

02:20:48.834 --> 02:20:50.801
to have similar
conversations about bias

02:20:50.834 --> 02:20:52.300
with the folks around you.

02:20:52.334 --> 02:20:54.234
We've also created a resource
guide for how to best have

02:20:54.267 --> 02:20:56.801
these conversations and in,
can't tress this enough,

02:20:56.834 --> 02:20:58.534
a productive manner.

02:20:58.567 --> 02:21:01.300
The only point is not
just an exercise

02:21:01.334 --> 02:21:04.100
in getting your bars off.

02:21:04.133 --> 02:21:06.834
It's about actually how can
you move the ball forward.

02:21:06.868 --> 02:21:09.200
And it's not always the person
I would say you're talking to.

02:21:09.234 --> 02:21:11.501
It's the others that
are listening as well.

02:21:11.534 --> 02:21:13.501
So we've partnered
with a few organizations

02:21:13.534 --> 02:21:16.033
that are leading the charge
in this department

02:21:16.067 --> 02:21:19.033
and work with them
to develop a detailed plan

02:21:19.067 --> 02:21:21.000
to help in the best ways
that we can.

02:21:21.033 --> 02:21:22.734
And some of that
includes learning

02:21:22.767 --> 02:21:25.300
about historical
and present-day experiences

02:21:25.334 --> 02:21:29.334
that fuel systematic racism,
that fuel inequality,

02:21:29.367 --> 02:21:31.667
ways that you, where you live,
wherever that is,

02:21:31.701 --> 02:21:34.601
you can activate your local
communities directly.

02:21:34.634 --> 02:21:36.834
You can be an ally,
an advocate, an activist.

02:21:36.868 --> 02:21:38.701
You can certainly
be antiracist.

02:21:38.734 --> 02:21:40.434
We've talked before

02:21:40.467 --> 02:21:42.400
and the choice
that P&amp;G put together

02:21:42.434 --> 02:21:44.334
really does a good job
of illustrating

02:21:44.367 --> 02:21:46.567
that you can't be neutral
on a moving train,

02:21:46.601 --> 02:21:48.334
as Howard Zinn used to say.

02:21:48.367 --> 02:21:49.934
You have to be anti-racist.

02:21:49.968 --> 02:21:51.734
Donate to these organizations

02:21:51.767 --> 02:21:53.834
that we've laid out that
advanced equity and justice.

02:21:53.868 --> 02:21:57.467
There are people that are
spending their time and energy

02:21:57.501 --> 02:22:00.501
on justice for folks,
they need our support.

02:22:00.534 --> 02:22:03.067
And they need our money
obviously, also.

02:22:03.100 --> 02:22:04.534
And we can't stress
that enough.

02:22:04.567 --> 02:22:07.067
All it takes is, in life these
little pivot points,

02:22:07.100 --> 02:22:08.501
where you're exposed
to something

02:22:08.534 --> 02:22:10.033
that you otherwise wouldn't,

02:22:10.067 --> 02:22:12.033
might inform a lot of designs
going forward.

02:22:12.067 --> 02:22:14.567
So go ahead
and share the video.

02:22:14.601 --> 02:22:16.501
You could have some really
interesting conversations,

02:22:16.534 --> 02:22:18.400
not only for yourself
but for others.

02:22:18.434 --> 02:22:21.601
So go ahead and click the link
on the video description

02:22:21.634 --> 02:22:23.167
for a page with more details
on how to learn,

02:22:23.200 --> 02:22:24.868
how to activate and donate.

02:22:24.901 --> 02:22:27.834
Freddie, I think we came,
we saw, we learned.

02:22:27.868 --> 02:22:29.434
I'm really pleasantly surprised.

02:22:29.467 --> 02:22:31.200
I didn't know how
this was going to go.

02:22:31.234 --> 02:22:33.000
I was excited about it

02:22:33.033 --> 02:22:36.100
but I'm proud of what
we not only learned

02:22:36.133 --> 02:22:39.200
and experienced, but shared
with each other today.

02:22:39.234 --> 02:22:40.601
So I want to
thank you for your

02:22:40.634 --> 02:22:41.901
time, energy, and expertise.

02:22:41.934 --> 02:22:43.801
It really made a world
of difference.

02:22:43.834 --> 02:22:45.200
Yes. Thank you so much.

02:22:45.234 --> 02:22:47.367
I honestly, I mean I loved
talking about this stuff.

02:22:47.400 --> 02:22:50.601
I could talk about it all day,
so this is not work for me.

02:22:50.634 --> 02:22:54.534
It's definitely something that
is more than that for me,

02:22:54.567 --> 02:22:57.267
so I'm happy to be a part
of this whole experiment

02:22:57.300 --> 02:22:58.868
and project as a whole.

02:22:58.901 --> 02:23:00.567
And to everyone watching,

02:23:00.601 --> 02:23:02.501
thank you so much
for watching and stay safe,

02:23:02.534 --> 02:23:04.667
stay loud, and create change.

02:23:04.734 --> 02:23:06.300
That's right, peace.

